(window.QUIZ_CHUNKS=window.QUIZ_CHUNKS||{})["e1c.6699c7fd40.js"]={"12":[{"id":"sdl12_q01","sdlNumber":12,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the terms complementary medicine, alternative medicine, and integrative medicine and discuss them in the frameworks of physical, psychological, or combination.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which term describes the use of complementary health practices as a substitute for conventional standard care rather than alongside it?","choices":{"A":"Complementary medicine, which refers specifically to practices used together with conventional therapies rather than as a substitute for them","B":"Integrative health care, which refers to coordinated use of both conventional and complementary practices with emphasis on whole-person care across social, psychological, and physical domains","C":"Whole person health, which is a framework focusing on restoring health, promoting resilience, and preventing disease across the full lifespan rather than treating individual organs or systems","D":"Alternative medicine, which denotes practices used in place of conventional standard care and is increasingly less preferred than the term \"complementary,\" which implies use alongside conventional therapy","E":"Salutogenesis, which describes the process by which health is actively restored when recovering from injury, acute illness, or exacerbation of chronic disease through behavioral rather than pharmacologic change"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Alternative medicine means used instead of conventional care; complementary means used alongside it. \"Complementary\" is increasingly preferred over \"alternative.\" Integrative health care combines both in a coordinated way with a whole-person focus. Salutogenesis is pathogenesis in reverse — restoring health through behavioral change.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know the distinction: complementary = with, alternative = instead of. COMLEX: Osteopathic medicine is classified as integrative (mainstream + OMM), not alternative. Understanding this framework is directly tested in COMLEX clinical presentation scenarios.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — Therapeutic exercises as the physical input category within integrative medicine"},{"id":"sdl12_q02","sdlNumber":12,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the terms complementary medicine, alternative medicine, and integrative medicine and discuss them in the frameworks of physical, psychological, or combination.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Under which primary therapeutic input category would meditation and cognitive behavioral therapy (CBT) be correctly classified?","choices":{"A":"Physical input, which includes modalities delivered manually such as massage, by device such as acupuncture, or generated by the patient such as exercise","B":"Dietary input, which encompasses herbs, vitamins, minerals, and dietary supplements consumed to influence biological function through nutritional pathways","C":"Multicomponent system input, which describes whole health systems such as traditional Chinese medicine, Ayurveda, naturopathy, and chiropractic that combine multiple therapeutic approaches","D":"Combined physical and psychological input, which characterizes practices such as yoga and tai chi that integrate movement with meditative elements into a single intervention","E":"Psychological input, as both target mental processes as their primary therapeutic mechanism, alongside mindfulness-based stress reduction, hypnosis, and biofeedback-assisted relaxation techniques"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Complementary therapies are classified by their primary therapeutic input: dietary (herbs, supplements), psychological (CBT, MBSR, meditation, hypnosis, relaxation), physical (massage, acupuncture, exercise), or combined (yoga, tai chi). CBT and meditation have predominantly mental/psychological mechanisms, making them primary psychological inputs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Memorize the 4 input categories and their representative modalities — this framework organizes an otherwise unwieldy list. COMLEX: OMM and spinal manipulation are classified as physical integrative medicine; understanding this helps frame osteopathic practice within the broader integrative health landscape.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — Therapeutic exercises as the physical input category within integrative medicine"},{"id":"sdl12_q03","sdlNumber":12,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the terms complementary medicine, alternative medicine, and integrative medicine and discuss them in the frameworks of physical, psychological, or combination.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A health system launches a program built on the concept of whole person health. Which description best matches how the course reading defines whole person health?","choices":{"A":"Treating each diagnosed disease with the single most effective therapy, whether conventional or complementary, organ by organ","B":"Viewing the person across biological, behavioral, social, and environmental areas to restore health and resilience","C":"Replacing conventional care with complementary practices so that patients avoid the adverse effects of prescription drugs","D":"Using complementary practices as self-care without physician involvement, paid out of pocket rather than through insurance","E":"Limiting care to mind and body practices that combine physical movement with meditation, such as yoga and tai chi"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The course reading defines whole person health as looking at the whole person rather than separate organs or body systems, considering multiple interconnected biological, behavioral, social, and environmental factors. Instead of treating a specific disease, it focuses on restoring health, promoting resilience, and preventing disease across the lifespan. Treating each disease organ by organ is the opposite of this approach. Replacing conventional care describes alternative medicine. Self-care paid out of pocket describes how many complementary practices are used, not the definition of whole person health. Yoga and tai chi are examples of combined psychological and physical input, not a definition of whole person health.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Complementary = alongside conventional care; alternative = instead of it; integrative = coordinated use of both; whole person health = multiple interconnected domains, restoring health and resilience. COMLEX: Osteopathic tenets (body as a unit of body, mind, and spirit) align with whole person health.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 4 — psychological versus physical integrative inputs"},{"id":"sdl12_q04","sdlNumber":12,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize disorders which may be amenable to integrative care.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"According to the American College of Physicians (ACP) clinical practice guidelines, which integrative therapy is strongly recommended as the initial treatment for chronic insomnia before pharmacologic agents?","choices":{"A":"Cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia (CBT-I), which the ACP strongly recommends as the initial treatment, often with relaxation techniques as components","B":"Melatonin supplementation, which the ACP recommends as the initial treatment for chronic insomnia because it reduces sleep-onset latency and daytime sleepiness","C":"Acupuncture, which the ACP recommends as the initial treatment for chronic insomnia on the basis of its benefit in chronic pain and osteoarthritis","D":"Progressive muscle relaxation, which the ACP recommends as a standalone first-line treatment for chronic insomnia because it lowers cortisol and anxiety","E":"Mindfulness-based stress reduction, which the ACP recommends as the initial treatment for chronic insomnia given its benefits for pain and anxiety"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The 2016 ACP practice guidelines strongly recommend CBT-I as the initial treatment for chronic insomnia. CBT-I often includes relaxation techniques but the full multi-component CBT-I program carries the guideline endorsement. Melatonin has evidence but is not ACP first-line; it also has safety concerns in children and mislabeling issues. Acupuncture is ACP-recommended for low-back pain, not insomnia, and it is not clear that relaxation alone (such as progressive muscle relaxation) is beneficial; MBSR helps pain and anxiety but is not the ACP initial treatment for insomnia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CBT-I = ACP first-line for chronic insomnia (before sleep aids). COMLEX: Insomnia is a common clinical presentation where osteopathic physicians are expected to recommend non-pharmacologic interventions first; CBT-I is the evidence-based answer before considering hypnotics.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 1 — OA as the leading musculoskeletal condition amenable to integrative care"},{"id":"sdl12_q05","sdlNumber":12,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize disorders which may be amenable to integrative care.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"The American College of Rheumatology and the Arthritis Foundation issue which level of recommendation for tai chi as a treatment for knee and hip osteoarthritis?","choices":{"A":"Conditional recommendation for tai chi as a second-line option for OA after failure of NSAIDs and corticosteroid injections, reflecting limited evidence compared to acupuncture","B":"Strong recommendation for tai chi alongside other nondrug approaches for knee and/or hip OA, given its benefits for motor function, balance, stability, sleep, and quality of life","C":"No formal recommendation exists for tai chi in OA; the ACR and Arthritis Foundation only recognize acupuncture and massage as having sufficient evidence for guideline inclusion","D":"Weak recommendation against tai chi in elderly OA patients due to fall risk, with guidance instead favoring low-impact aquatic exercise programs for patients with severe hip OA","E":"Moderate recommendation pending ongoing randomized trials, with current use suggested only as an adjunct to pharmacologic therapy rather than as a standalone intervention"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The ACR and Arthritis Foundation strongly recommend tai chi for knee/hip OA as a nondrug approach. Acupuncture receives a conditional (not strong) recommendation for knee/hip/hand OA. Tai chi also improves balance, stability in elderly, sleep quality in mild insomnia, and quality of life in heart disease and cancer.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know the guideline levels — tai chi STRONG recommendation for OA (ACR/AF); acupuncture CONDITIONAL for OA (ACR/AF); acupuncture RECOMMENDED for low-back pain (ACP 2017). COMLEX: Tai chi exemplifies combined physical-psychological integrative medicine specifically endorsed for the musculoskeletal conditions most common in COMLEX clinical presentations.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 1 — OA as the leading musculoskeletal condition amenable to integrative care"},{"id":"sdl12_q06","sdlNumber":12,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize disorders which may be amenable to integrative care.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Clinical trials have shown that omega-3 fatty acid supplementation is beneficial for which of the following musculoskeletal conditions by reducing tender joints, morning stiffness, and the daily NSAID requirement?","choices":{"A":"Knee osteoarthritis, where fish oil reverses cartilage loss on MRI and lowers the daily NSAID requirement in randomized trials","B":"Fibromyalgia, where omega-3 supplementation is the guideline-endorsed first-line integrative therapy for tender points and morning stiffness","C":"Ankylosing spondylitis, where fish oil halts progression of spinal fusion and lowers inflammatory back pain scores in clinical trials","D":"Gout, where omega-3 fatty acids lower serum uric acid enough to replace allopurinol and prevent acute flares in clinical trials","E":"Rheumatoid arthritis (RA), where fish oil reduces tender joints, morning stiffness, and daily NSAID requirements in clinical trials"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Omega-3 fatty acids have the strongest integrative evidence in RA: reduced tender joints, reduced morning stiffness, reduced NSAID requirement. Omega-3 supplementation has not been shown to reverse cartilage loss in OA, to be first-line for fibromyalgia, to halt spinal fusion in ankylosing spondylitis, or to replace urate-lowering therapy in gout. For depression, omega-3 data are inconclusive as monotherapy. High-dose fish oil is associated with increased atrial fibrillation risk. For AMD, AREDS2 antioxidants are the relevant supplement.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Omega-3 = RA (reduces NSAID need, tender joints, morning stiffness). Not a replacement for DMARDs. High-dose fish oil → increased AF risk. COMLEX: Omega-3 supplementation is a dietary integrative medicine strategy; knowing its evidence base versus areas where evidence is lacking is a classic COMLEX pharmacology-nutrition integration question.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 1 — OA as the leading musculoskeletal condition amenable to integrative care"},{"id":"sdl12_q07","sdlNumber":12,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss challenges to the use of integrative medicine including toxicity, research, and regulation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"The Dietary Supplement Health and Education Act (DSHEA) of 1994 establishes which specific regulatory limitation on claims that dietary supplement manufacturers may make on their product labels?","choices":{"A":"Supplements must demonstrate clinical safety and efficacy through FDA premarket approval similar to pharmaceutical drugs before being sold to consumers in the United States","B":"Manufacturers may claim a supplement maintains \"normal structure and function\" of body systems but may not claim it prevents, treats, or cures any disease; false claims fall under FTC enforcement","C":"Dietary supplements are prohibited from listing any health benefits on their labels and may only display ingredient lists, serving sizes, and recommended daily values without any wellness claims","D":"All dietary supplement advertising claims must be reviewed and approved by the FDA before they appear on product labels, in online marketing, or in any print or broadcast media","E":"Supplement manufacturers must disclose all known herb-drug interactions on product labels, similar to the drug interaction warning requirements imposed on pharmaceutical manufacturers"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Under DSHEA (1994), dietary supplements do NOT require FDA premarket approval for efficacy. Manufacturers can claim to \"maintain normal joint health\" or \"support normal structure and function\" but cannot claim to \"treat arthritis\" or \"prevent cancer.\" The FTC (not FDA) regulates advertising claims. The FDA regulates labeling.","boardPrep":"Step 1: DSHEA (1994) = structure/function claims OK; disease claims NOT OK. The burden of proving harm falls on the FDA, not the manufacturer. FTC regulates advertising. Classic board trap: supplements do not require proof of efficacy before sale. COMLEX: This regulatory framework creates risk — patients may use unproven supplements believing they are equivalent to evidence-based medicines.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 4 — NSAID adverse effects and regulation as contrast to supplement toxicity challenges"},{"id":"sdl12_q08","sdlNumber":12,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss challenges to the use of integrative medicine including toxicity, research, and regulation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"The FDA banned dietary supplements containing Ephedra sinica (ma huang) in the United States in 2004. Which pharmacologic property of this plant directly led to that regulatory action?","choices":{"A":"Ephedra sinica was found to contain aristolochic acid alkaloids that were specifically associated with genitourinary malignancies and interstitial nephritis in consumers using herbal weight-loss preparations","B":"Ephedra sinica caused the eosinophilia-myalgia syndrome through contamination of amino acid supplements during manufacturing, resulting in multisystem inflammation affecting thousands of users","C":"Ephedra sinica supplements were found to contain 1,3-dimethylamylamine, a synthetic stimulant frequently adulterated into herbal products that caused cardiovascular problems and heart attacks in users","D":"Ephedra sinica was withdrawn after being found to cause anosmia in users of a homeopathic nasal spray product, later determined to result from significant zinc content rather than the herb itself","E":"Ephedra sinica contains ephedrine and pseudoephedrine alkaloids; supplements sold for weight loss and athletic performance caused severe and fatal cardiovascular adverse events including heart attacks"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Ephedra sinica (ma huang) contains ephedrine and pseudoephedrine. Used for weight loss and athletic performance, it caused severe cardiovascular adverse events. FDA banned it in 2004. Aristolochia = genitourinary malignancies and nephritis. Tryptophan contamination = eosinophilia-myalgia syndrome. DMAA = cardiovascular problems in its own right. Zicam = anosmia from zinc.","boardPrep":"Step 1: HY toxicology: ephedra/ma huang = cardiovascular events (ephedrine/pseudoephedrine) → FDA banned 2004. Aristolochia = nephritis + GU cancer. Tryptophan contamination = eosinophilia-myalgia. Keep each toxin matched to its specific supplement and outcome. COMLEX: Counseling patients about natural product risks includes knowing these specific examples to cite.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 4 — NSAID adverse effects and regulation as contrast to supplement toxicity challenges"},{"id":"sdl12_q09","sdlNumber":12,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss challenges to the use of integrative medicine including toxicity, research, and regulation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"St. John's Wort is known to reduce the bioavailability of many co-administered drugs. Which molecular mechanism is responsible for this herb-drug interaction?","choices":{"A":"Upregulation of the pregnane X receptor (PXR) by hyperforin in St. John's Wort, which increases expression of hepatic oxidative, conjugative, and efflux enzymes involved in drug metabolism and transport","B":"Direct competitive inhibition of cytochrome P450 isoforms at the catalytic active site, permanently reducing the capacity of these enzymes to metabolize co-administered drugs to their active or inactive metabolites","C":"Chelation of divalent cations required as cofactors for intestinal drug transport proteins, reducing the bioavailability of drugs that require metal-dependent active transport across the gastrointestinal epithelium","D":"Displacement of protein-bound drugs from albumin and alpha-1-acid glycoprotein binding sites, dramatically increasing the unbound free fraction of narrow therapeutic index drugs and raising toxicity risk","E":"Inhibition of renal tubular secretion through competitive blockade of the organic anion transporter system, extending the half-life of antibiotics and chemotherapeutic agents dependent on renal clearance"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Hyperforin in St. John's Wort activates PXR (pregnane X receptor), a promiscuous nuclear regulatory factor that upregulates hepatic oxidative (CYP enzymes), conjugative, and efflux enzymes. This was first shown with indinavir (HIV protease inhibitor), then cyclosporine, irinotecan, and many others. Particularly dangerous with narrow therapeutic index drugs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: St. John's Wort → PXR activation → CYP induction → reduced drug levels. Classic examples: indinavir (HIV drug — first documented 2000), cyclosporine (transplant rejection risk), irinotecan (chemo). COMLEX: Herb-drug interactions are a real clinical concern in integrative medicine; asking patients about natural product use is an osteopathic competency.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 4 — NSAID adverse effects and regulation as contrast to supplement toxicity challenges"},{"id":"sdl12_q10","sdlNumber":12,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Differentiate between psychological and physical integrative medicine.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following integrative practices is correctly categorized as having a primary PHYSICAL therapeutic input?","choices":{"A":"Cognitive behavioral therapy, which targets maladaptive thought patterns and behaviors through a structured, time-limited series of therapy sessions","B":"Mindfulness-based stress reduction, which cultivates present-moment awareness through guided meditation practice in a structured group program","C":"Acupuncture, which delivers therapeutic input by stimulating defined anatomic points with solid metallic needles or electrical stimulation","D":"Yoga, which integrates physical postures and breathing with meditative elements and improves back-related function in chronic low-back pain","E":"Tai chi, which combines slow, gentle movements originating in China with meditative practice and improves balance and stability"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Physical input modalities are delivered manually (massage), by device (acupuncture), or generated by the patient (exercise). Psychological inputs target mental processes (CBT, MBSR, hypnosis). Combined inputs integrate both (yoga, tai chi). Acupuncture is purely physical — the therapeutic mechanism is stimulation of anatomic points, not a mental/psychological process.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know the input category for each major integrative modality: acupuncture = physical; CBT/MBSR = psychological; yoga/tai chi = combined. COMLEX: OMM, spinal manipulation, and massage are all physical integrative medicine — understanding this classification helps frame osteopathic medicine within the Harrison's integrative medicine framework.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — Physical therapy modalities as exemplars of physical integrative medicine"},{"id":"sdl12_q11","sdlNumber":12,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Differentiate between psychological and physical integrative medicine.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"The 2017 American College of Physicians clinical guidelines recommend which integrative therapy as one of the initial treatment options for patients with acute, subacute, and chronic low-back pain?","choices":{"A":"Melatonin supplementation, which the 2017 ACP guideline recommends for low-back pain because poor sleep amplifies the perception of pain","B":"Omega-3 fatty acid supplementation, which the 2017 ACP guideline recommends for low-back pain because of its anti-inflammatory effects","C":"Acupuncture, which the ACP recommends as one initial option for acute, subacute, and chronic low-back pain, amid opioid concerns","D":"Glucosamine and chondroitin supplementation, which the 2017 ACP guideline recommends for acute, subacute, and chronic low-back pain","E":"Hypnotherapy, which the 2017 ACP guideline recommends for low-back pain because it reduces the need for sedatives and analgesics"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The 2017 ACP guidelines recommend nonpharmacologic first-line treatments for low-back pain including acupuncture (all stages: acute, subacute, chronic), spinal manipulation, and massage, reflecting the opioid crisis. Melatonin, omega-3 fatty acids, hypnotherapy, and glucosamine-chondroitin are not among the ACP's recommended initial treatments for low-back pain. Acupuncture has been shown superior to both usual care and sham acupuncture for musculoskeletal pain with effects lasting up to 12 months.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ACP 2017 low-back pain guideline: nonpharmacologic first (acupuncture, spinal manipulation, massage, exercise, yoga, tai chi) before NSAIDs, before opioids. This reflects the opioid epidemic response. COMLEX: Spinal manipulation is also in these guidelines — osteopathic physicians are uniquely positioned to offer multiple ACP-recommended nonpharmacologic options.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — Physical therapy modalities as exemplars of physical integrative medicine"},{"id":"sdl12_q12","sdlNumber":12,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Differentiate between psychological and physical integrative medicine.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Self-hypnosis training has been shown to significantly reduce the need for sedatives and analgesia during which type of medical procedure?","choices":{"A":"Interventional radiologic procedures, where self-hypnosis training reduced the need for sedatives and analgesia; hypnotherapy also helps anxiety and quality of life in IBS","B":"Orthopedic joint replacement surgery, where preoperative hypnosis training has been shown to reduce postoperative opioid consumption more effectively than any other mind-body intervention","C":"Cardiac catheterization, where hypnosis is the recommended primary sedation modality in patients with contrast allergy who cannot receive standard intravenous sedation protocols","D":"Colonoscopy, where hypnosis has been shown to reduce both procedural discomfort and polyp detection rates by altering bowel motility through autonomic nervous system mechanisms","E":"Radiation therapy for prostate cancer, where hypnosis is recommended by American Society for Radiation Oncology guidelines to reduce both anxiety and radiation-induced tissue injury"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Studies showed self-hypnosis significantly reduced need for sedatives and analgesia during interventional radiologic procedures. Hypnotherapy also benefits anxiety and quality of life in IBS and may help smoking cessation. These are specific evidence-based indications. No comparable hypnosis evidence is described for joint replacement, cardiac catheterization, colonoscopy, or prostate radiation therapy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hypnosis clinical evidence: interventional radiology (sedation/analgesia reduction), IBS (anxiety and QoL), smoking cessation (some evidence, not definitive). COMLEX: Hypnosis is classified as a primary psychological integrative modality; distinguishing its evidence-based applications from unsupported claims is a clinical reasoning competency.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — Physical therapy modalities as exemplars of physical integrative medicine"},{"id":"sdl12_q13","sdlNumber":12,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Discuss research challenges in the study of integrative care.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"Which regulatory requirement does the Dietary Supplement Health and Education Act (DSHEA) of 1994 explicitly NOT impose on dietary supplement manufacturers?","choices":{"A":"Prohibition against claiming to prevent, treat, or cure specific diseases such as arthritis or cancer on product labels, with violators subject to FTC enforcement actions and FDA warning letters","B":"Labeling requirements identifying the product as a dietary supplement with a list of ingredients, serving size, and a disclosure that the FDA has not evaluated any structure or function claims","C":"Proof of efficacy before marketing, as DSHEA allows supplements to be sold without demonstrating clinical effectiveness, unlike prescription drugs, which require FDA premarket approval","D":"Compliance with Current Good Manufacturing Practices establishing minimum quality control standards for supplement production, including identity, purity, strength, and composition of ingredients","E":"Prohibition of fraudulent or deceptive advertising claims in digital, print, and broadcast media, enforced through FTC authority to take legal action against promoters of false supplement claims"},"correct":"C","explanation":"DSHEA does NOT require pre-market proof of efficacy — the burden of proving harm lies with the FDA, not the manufacturer. DSHEA does require: no disease claims, structure/function label disclosures, Good Manufacturing Practice compliance. Advertising is regulated by FTC, not FDA. This asymmetry from pharma regulations creates significant consumer safety risks.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Key DSHEA distinction: supplements ≠ drugs. No premarket efficacy proof required. Manufacturer makes safety-responsible, FDA proves harm after the fact. This is why supplements can reach market with limited evidence. COMLEX: Counseling patients about supplement risks requires understanding this regulatory gap — \"sold in stores\" does not mean \"FDA approved.\"","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 2 — Conventional drug mechanism research as contrast to integrative medicine research limitations"},{"id":"sdl12_q14","sdlNumber":12,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Discuss research challenges in the study of integrative care.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"Classic randomized controlled trial (RCT) design faces which specific challenge when applied to studying multicomponent complementary systems such as naturopathy or Ayurvedic medicine?","choices":{"A":"Ethical review boards prohibit the use of any control arm in studies of ancient healing traditions because denying patients access to culturally meaningful care constitutes a violation of research ethics principles","B":"The FDA requires a separate investigational new drug application for each natural product contained in a multicomponent naturopathic or Ayurvedic treatment regimen, making the regulatory pathway prohibitive","C":"Insurance companies uniformly deny reimbursement for study visits using complementary interventions, making participant retention impossible in trials where patients must pay out of pocket for study procedures","D":"Their individualized, dynamic nature makes treatment hard to standardize, so pragmatic comparative effectiveness studies with usual care comparators are used, though they cannot isolate the active component","E":"Blinding is structurally impossible in studies of physical integrative therapies because practitioners and participants can always identify the active treatment, rendering all such research inherently unblinded"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Multicomponent systems like naturopathy and Ayurveda are inherently individualized. Classic RCTs require standardized interventions, but these systems adapt to each patient. Pragmatic designs with usual care comparators work better but cannot isolate active components. \"Manualized\" treatment protocols with some flexibility are being developed to address this. Blinding challenges exist but are not the primary issue cited.","boardPrep":"Step 1: RCT limitations for integrative research: individualization prevents standardization; cannot isolate which component works; pragmatic designs used instead. COMLEX: OMM research faces similar challenges — individualized treatment plans, practitioner variability, and difficulty blinding make it methodologically complex to study.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 2 — Conventional drug mechanism research as contrast to integrative medicine research limitations"},{"id":"sdl12_q15","sdlNumber":12,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Discuss research challenges in the study of integrative care.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"Which of the following represents a specific research challenge unique to studying natural products compared to psychological or physical integrative interventions?","choices":{"A":"The complexity and variability of natural products, including unstable active components, impurities, conflicting low-powered studies, and no standardized dosing for over-the-counter products","B":"The impossibility of blinding any natural product study because participants can always distinguish an active herbal supplement from a placebo by taste, smell, or physical appearance","C":"The complete absence of federal funding for natural product research from agencies such as the NIH National Center for Complementary and Integrative Health, requiring industry sponsorship for all studies","D":"The requirement that all natural product researchers hold dual degrees in pharmacology and botany, creating a severe workforce shortage that restricts the number of clinical trials that can be conducted","E":"The ethical prohibition against studying natural products alongside conventional drugs, which prevents investigation of the real-world usage patterns most commonly observed in clinical practice"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Natural product research challenges: complexity and variability; possible active component instability; impurities; conflicting literature; low statistical power; paucity of safety data in modern contexts. \"More is not necessarily better\" — excessive doses of vitamins can be harmful. These are distinct from the multicomponent/individualization challenges of systems like naturopathy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know the natural product research challenges: dose variability, instability, impurities, low statistical power. The NCCIH exists specifically to fund this research (so federal funding is NOT absent). COMLEX: When patients ask about natural products, acknowledging the research limitations and directing them to NCCIH resources is appropriate clinical guidance.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 2 — Conventional drug mechanism research as contrast to integrative medicine research limitations"},{"id":"sdl12_q16","sdlNumber":12,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the terms complementary medicine, alternative medicine, and integrative medicine and discuss them in the frameworks of physical, psychological, or combination.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 57-year-old man with rheumatoid arthritis tells his physician that, in addition to his DMARD, he takes fish oil capsules and a turmeric supplement and follows an anti-inflammatory diet. Using the course scheme that classifies complementary practices by their primary therapeutic input, how are these practices classified?","choices":{"A":"Primary physical input, because the supplements act on the joints directly in the way that massage acts on soft tissue","B":"Primary psychological input, because the benefit of supplements comes mainly from expectation and a sense of self-care","C":"Combined physical and psychological input, because diet changes involve both behavior and the body, as in tai chi","D":"Primary dietary input, the category that includes diet changes, herbs, and other natural products such as fish oil","E":"Alternative medicine, because any supplement taken with a disease-modifying drug replaces part of conventional care"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The course reading classifies complementary practices by primary therapeutic input: dietary (diet, herbs, and other natural products), psychological (for example meditation), physical (for example massage and acupuncture), or combined psychological and physical (yoga, tai chi). Fish oil, turmeric, and an anti-inflammatory diet are dietary inputs. The reading also notes that fish oil can reduce tender joints, morning stiffness, and NSAID needs in RA. Physical input is delivered manually, by a device, or by the patient's own movement. Psychological input targets mental processes. Combined input describes movement plus meditation. Because he uses these alongside his DMARD, they are complementary, not alternative.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Natural products are complex mixtures; risks include toxicity, contamination, adulteration, and herb-drug interactions (St. John's wort induces PXR). COMLEX: Ask every patient about supplements and check interaction resources for narrow-therapeutic-index drugs.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 3 — toxicity, regulation, and herb-drug interactions of natural products"},{"id":"sdl12_q17","sdlNumber":12,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the terms complementary medicine, alternative medicine, and integrative medicine and discuss them in the frameworks of physical, psychological, or combination.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 62-year-old man with knee osteoarthritis asks his rheumatologist about yoga and tai chi as options. The rheumatologist explains that these two practices share a classification that makes them distinct from acupuncture alone or meditation alone. Which classification do yoga and tai chi share?","choices":{"A":"Primary physical input, as both yoga and tai chi involve predominantly physical movement and exercise, with any meditative components considered secondary and clinically insignificant for classification purposes","B":"Combined physical and psychological input, as both integrate physical movement and postures with meditative elements, unlike purely physical acupuncture or purely psychological meditation","C":"Primary psychological input, as both yoga and tai chi originate from Eastern meditation traditions and their therapeutic benefits arise primarily from psychological mechanisms rather than from physical movement","D":"Multicomponent system input, as both originate from traditional Asian medical systems and therefore belong to the same category as traditional Chinese medicine, Ayurveda, and naturopathy","E":"Dietary input, as both yoga and tai chi traditions emphasize dietary practices alongside movement, making nutrition the primary therapeutic input category for these ancient health systems"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Yoga and tai chi combine physical postures/movements with meditative elements, classifying them uniquely as combined physical and psychological inputs. This distinguishes them from: acupuncture (primary physical), massage (primary physical), CBT/MBSR (primary psychological), traditional Chinese medicine (multicomponent system). The combination gives them a broader therapeutic target.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Combined physical + psychological = yoga, tai chi. Physical only = acupuncture, massage, exercise. Psychological only = CBT, MBSR, hypnosis, meditation. This classification has appeared in exam questions asking to categorize specific modalities. COMLEX: The combined nature of yoga/tai chi mirrors the holistic osteopathic approach, explaining why they are endorsed in multiple ACR and ACP guidelines.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 1 — Therapeutic exercises: closed kinetic chain, concentric, core stability, eccentric, isometric, isotonic, open kinetic chain"},{"id":"sdl12_q18","sdlNumber":12,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the terms complementary medicine, alternative medicine, and integrative medicine and discuss them in the frameworks of physical, psychological, or combination.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"An integrative medicine specialist explains that their practice incorporates \"salutogenesis\" as a guiding principle. A medical student asks what this term means. Which of the following best defines salutogenesis in the context of integrative health?","choices":{"A":"Salutogenesis is pathogenesis in reverse: the process by which health is actively restored after injury, acute illness, or a chronic disease flare, or when prediabetes is reversed by behavioral change","B":"Salutogenesis describes the simultaneous development of multiple chronic comorbidities as a result of shared metabolic risk factors, representing the intersection of pathological pathways in complex patients","C":"Salutogenesis refers to the prevention of disease through prophylactic pharmacologic treatment before symptoms arise, as distinguished from reactive treatment given only after a diagnosis has been established","D":"Salutogenesis is the systematic review of a patient's social determinants of health to identify upstream factors that increase disease risk, forming the basis for population-level public health interventions","E":"Salutogenesis refers to the spontaneous remission of disease that occurs in some patients without treatment, which integrative medicine attempts to harness and amplify through mind-body interventions"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Salutogenesis = pathogenesis in reverse. It emphasizes the process of restoring health rather than solely preventing or treating disease. Integrative health incorporates salutogenesis by addressing recovery from injury/illness and reversing predisease states (e.g., prediabetes, prehypertension) through behavioral interventions. It is not about spontaneous remission or social determinants specifically.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Salutogenesis = health restoration process (vs. pathogenesis = disease development). Integrative medicine frames health as a continuum, with salutogenesis as the return direction. COMLEX: The osteopathic philosophy of treating the whole person and supporting the body's self-healing mechanisms aligns directly with salutogenesis as a guiding principle.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 1 — Therapeutic exercises: closed kinetic chain, concentric, core stability, eccentric, isometric, isotonic, open kinetic chain"},{"id":"sdl12_q19","sdlNumber":12,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize disorders which may be amenable to integrative care.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman with fibromyalgia and chronic low-back pain asks about a single integrative intervention that has evidence for both conditions. She prefers something she can do independently after initial instruction. Which integrative practice has evidence supporting benefit for BOTH fibromyalgia AND chronic low-back pain?","choices":{"A":"Acupuncture, which has evidence for chronic low-back pain, headache, and osteoarthritis, with treatment effects persisting for up to 12 months","B":"Biofeedback, which has evidence for tension headaches and for reducing pediatric and adult migraine frequency over about 17 months","C":"Hypnotherapy, which has evidence for reducing sedative use during interventional radiology and for anxiety and quality of life in IBS","D":"Yoga, which can benefit fibromyalgia and gives small to moderate gains in back-related function at 3 and 6 months in chronic low-back pain","E":"Omega-3 supplementation, which reduces tender joints, morning stiffness, and NSAID requirements in rheumatoid arthritis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Yoga has specific evidence for both fibromyalgia (beneficial) and chronic low-back pain (small-moderate improvement at 3 and 6 months vs. nonexercise controls). MBSR also benefits fibromyalgia and low-back pain. Acupuncture, biofeedback, hypnotherapy, and omega-3 fatty acids have evidence for other conditions, but not for both fibromyalgia and chronic low-back pain. Yoga has additional benefits for anxiety, depression, smoking cessation, and quality of life in chronic diseases. The American Cancer Society recommends yoga for anxiety in breast cancer patients.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Yoga evidence base: fibromyalgia, chronic LBP, anxiety, smoking cessation, cancer QoL. Also recommended by American Cancer Society for breast cancer-related anxiety. COMLEX: As a combined physical-psychological modality, yoga exemplifies whole-person integrative care — its multimodal benefits reflect the osteopathic principle of treating the whole patient.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis: epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management"},{"id":"sdl12_q20","sdlNumber":12,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize disorders which may be amenable to integrative care.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 44-year-old woman undergoing cycle 3 of platinum-based chemotherapy for ovarian cancer reports refractory nausea and vomiting not controlled by standard antiemetics. Her oncologist mentions an integrative option that can be added to drug treatment. Which integrative practice has specific evidence for chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting in cancer patients?","choices":{"A":"Tai chi, which improves overall motor function, balance, stability, sleep quality, and quality of life in patients with chronic illness","B":"Yoga, which the American Cancer Society recommends for anxiety and stress reduction during and after breast cancer treatment","C":"Mindfulness-based stress reduction, which reduces pain through multiple non-opioidergic mechanisms and benefits anxiety","D":"Progressive muscle relaxation, which may reduce occupational stress and help anxiety in patients undergoing medical procedures","E":"Acupuncture or electroacupuncture, which may be an appropriate addition to drug treatment for chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Acupuncture or electroacupuncture may be an appropriate addition to drug treatment for chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting. Separately, 2022 Society for Integrative Oncology/ASCO guidelines found intermediate-level evidence that acupuncture may relieve pain in people with cancer, and acupuncture may also relieve allergic rhinitis symptoms. Yoga is recommended for anxiety/stress in cancer but not specifically nausea, and tai chi, MBSR, and progressive muscle relaxation have evidence for function, pain, anxiety, or stress rather than chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Acupuncture indications include: chronic MSK pain, headache, OA (ACR/AF, conditional), low-back pain (ACP), chemo-induced nausea (add-on to antiemetics), pain in cancer (SIO/ASCO 2022), depression (modest), allergic rhinitis. This broad evidence base makes it the most guideline-supported physical integrative modality. COMLEX: When asked about nonpharmacologic management of chemo-related symptoms, acupuncture is a testable answer.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis: epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management"},{"id":"sdl12_q21","sdlNumber":12,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize disorders which may be amenable to integrative care.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman with a 5-year history of chronic migraines asks about integrative options. She has tried two preventive medications without success. Which integrative intervention has the strongest evidence for reducing headache frequency in both pediatric and adult migraine patients, with effects documented over a follow-up period of approximately 17 months?","choices":{"A":"Acupuncture, which has evidence for chronic musculoskeletal pain, headache, and osteoarthritis, with effects lasting up to 12 months","B":"Yoga, which has evidence for reducing anxiety, improving quality of life in chronic diseases, and helping with smoking cessation","C":"Biofeedback, which decreases the frequency of pediatric and adult migraines, with some studies showing an effect over an average 17-month follow-up","D":"Mindfulness-based stress reduction, which reduces pain through multiple non-opioidergic mechanisms and benefits low-back pain and fibromyalgia","E":"Melatonin, which has evidence for reducing sleep-onset latency and improving daytime sleepiness in shift work sleep disorder"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Biofeedback has the most specific evidence for migraine: decreased frequency in pediatric and adult populations, with effects lasting ~17 months. Relaxation techniques more broadly also benefit migraines. Acupuncture has broad pain evidence including headache, but the specific 17-month migraine frequency data cited in Harrison's is for biofeedback.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Biofeedback = tension headaches and migraines (strongest evidence, ~17-month durability). Relaxation techniques broadly help anxiety and stress conditions. Acupuncture helps headache but biofeedback has the specific pediatric and adult migraine-frequency evidence. COMLEX: Biofeedback is a psychological integrative modality that trains patients to control autonomic functions — an important distinction from physical manipulation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis: epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management"},{"id":"sdl12_q22","sdlNumber":12,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss challenges to the use of integrative medicine including toxicity, research, and regulation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 58-year-old man is hospitalized with acute hepatic failure. His family reports he had been consuming wild mushrooms he foraged himself, believing them to have medicinal properties based on information from a wellness website. Which category of integrative medicine risk does this case exemplify?","choices":{"A":"Inherent toxicity of natural products, as misidentification of medicinal mushrooms can cause liver failure, a reminder that \"natural\" does not equate to \"safe\" when counseling patients","B":"Herb-drug interaction, as the hepatotoxicity resulted from the wild mushrooms upregulating pregnane X receptor expression in hepatic tissue, reducing the bioavailability of his prescribed medications","C":"Regulatory failure under DSHEA, as foraging websites making medicinal claims about wild mushrooms violate DSHEA structure-function claim restrictions and fall under FTC false advertising jurisdiction","D":"Adulteration of a dietary supplement product, as the mushrooms he consumed were contaminated with anabolic steroids and synthetic stimulants commonly found in products marketed for immune support","E":"Homeopathic product toxicity, as the diluted mushroom extract he consumed in homeopathic teething tablets contained significant amounts of undeclared pharmacologically active belladonna compounds"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Misidentification of medicinal mushrooms → liver failure is a specific example of inherent natural product toxicity. Other examples: tryptophan contamination → eosinophilia-myalgia syndrome; Aristolochia → GU malignancies and interstitial nephritis; ephedra → cardiovascular events. \"Natural\" ≠ \"safe\" is the core principle. This is NOT an herb-drug interaction, adulteration, or homeopathic scenario.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Natural product toxicity examples: mushrooms → liver failure; tryptophan → eosinophilia-myalgia; Aristolochia → GU cancer + nephritis; ephedra → cardiac events; DMAA → cardiovascular problems. Keep each example matched to its specific toxidrome. COMLEX: Counseling patients that natural products require the same safety scrutiny as medications is an osteopathic competency.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 4 — Adverse effects of NSAIDs by body system"},{"id":"sdl12_q23","sdlNumber":12,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss challenges to the use of integrative medicine including toxicity, research, and regulation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 54-year-old woman develops progressive renal insufficiency, and biopsy shows interstitial nephritis. She has been taking an imported herbal weight-loss product for 2 years. Months later she is also found to have a urothelial malignancy. Which natural product toxicity does this course reading describe that best fits her presentation?","choices":{"A":"Contaminated tryptophan supplements, which caused eosinophilia-myalgia syndrome with severe muscle pain and eosinophilia","B":"Misidentified medicinal mushrooms, which cause acute liver failure within days after a single foraged meal","C":"Ephedra-containing supplements, which caused heart attacks and death and were banned by the FDA in 2004","D":"DMAA stimulant supplements, which were touted as natural in 2013 and caused cardiovascular problems","E":"Herbal products with certain Aristolochia species, linked to interstitial nephritis and genitourinary malignancies"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The course reading's section on inherent toxicity lists several harms from natural products: herbal products containing particular species of Aristolochia were associated with genitourinary malignancies and interstitial nephritis, which matches her kidney disease and urothelial cancer. Contaminated tryptophan caused eosinophilia-myalgia syndrome, a muscle and blood eosinophil syndrome rather than nephritis and cancer. Misidentified mushrooms caused liver failure. Ephedra and DMAA caused cardiovascular events, including heart attacks. The reading uses these examples to show that natural does not mean safe.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Aristolochic acid → interstitial nephritis (\"Chinese herb nephropathy\") and urothelial carcinoma. Other natural-product harms: ephedra (CV events, banned 2004), contaminated tryptophan (eosinophilia-myalgia). COMLEX: Supplements under DSHEA need no premarket proof of safety or efficacy, so a supplement history is essential.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 5 — research challenges with variable, impure natural products"},{"id":"sdl12_q24","sdlNumber":12,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss challenges to the use of integrative medicine including toxicity, research, and regulation.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A patient shows you a dietary supplement label that states: \"This product treats arthritis and reverses joint degeneration.\" You explain this is an illegal disease claim. Which claim on a supplement label WOULD be permissible under DSHEA?","choices":{"A":"A claim that the supplement is FDA-approved as safe and effective for reduction of joint inflammation based on Phase III clinical trial data submitted for premarket review","B":"A claim that the supplement prevents the development of osteoarthritis in high-risk patients, as prevention of a named disease would constitute a legally permissible prophylactic health claim","C":"A claim that the supplement cures rheumatoid arthritis by modulating immune function and has been shown in head-to-head trials to be equivalent to approved disease-modifying antirheumatic drugs","D":"A claim that the supplement \"supports normal structure and function\" of joints, since DSHEA permits structure/function claims but bans claims to prevent, treat, or cure a disease","E":"A claim that the supplement treats the pain and inflammation of specific disease states including osteoarthritis and rheumatoid arthritis, provided the manufacturer discloses the absence of FDA evaluation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"DSHEA structure/function claims examples: \"maintains normal joint health,\" \"supports immune function,\" \"promotes bone health.\" Disease claims examples (prohibited): \"treats arthritis,\" \"prevents osteoporosis,\" \"cures RA.\" Disclosure of no FDA evaluation does NOT make disease claims permissible. FDA does NOT grant premarket approval for supplements.","boardPrep":"Step 1: DSHEA permitted = structure/function claims with FDA-disclaimer. DSHEA prohibited = disease claims (treat, prevent, cure). Classic example: can say \"maintains normal joint health\" but not \"treats arthritis.\" FTC acts against deceptive advertising; FDA acts against false label claims. COMLEX: Patients often show physicians supplement labels — recognizing illegal disease claims is a practical clinical skill.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 4 — Adverse effects of NSAIDs by body system"},{"id":"sdl12_q25","sdlNumber":12,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Differentiate between psychological and physical integrative medicine.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"The course reading notes that a physical therapeutic input can be delivered manually, delivered with a device, or generated by the patient. Which practice is an example of a physical input that is generated by the patient?","choices":{"A":"Massage, in which a therapist manipulates muscle and connective tissue","B":"Exercise, in which the patient's own movement provides the physical input","C":"Acupuncture, in which thin metallic needles stimulate defined anatomic points","D":"Biofeedback, in which a device helps the patient learn to control body functions","E":"Spinal manipulation, in which a practitioner applies a controlled thrust to a joint"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The reading states that a physical input can be delivered manually (for example massage), with a device (for example acupuncture), or generated by the patient (for example exercise). Exercise is the patient-generated example. Massage and spinal manipulation are delivered manually by a practitioner. Acupuncture is delivered with a device. Biofeedback uses electronic devices, but it belongs with the relaxation techniques under primary psychological input, because the patient learns to consciously control functions such as breathing or heart rate.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Physical inputs: manual (massage, spinal manipulation), device (acupuncture), patient-generated (exercise). Psychological inputs: CBT, meditation/MBSR, hypnosis, relaxation, biofeedback. Combined: yoga, tai chi. COMLEX: OMT is a manually delivered physical input with ACP support for low back pain.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — therapeutic exercise types in physical therapy"},{"id":"sdl12_q26","sdlNumber":12,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Differentiate between psychological and physical integrative medicine.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 46-year-old woman with fibromyalgia enrolls in a mindfulness-based stress reduction course and reports less pain. She asks whether the effect is just a placebo response or works through the same pathway as her prescribed opioid. According to the course reading, which statement best describes how mindfulness meditation reduces pain?","choices":{"A":"It reduces pain through endogenous opioid release, so naloxone blocks the effect in the same way it blocks morphine","B":"It produces no measurable change in pain beyond placebo, so any benefit reflects expectation and the course's attention","C":"It engages several unique nonopioidergic mechanisms that are distinct from placebo and vary with meditative training","D":"It works by stretching connective tissue during seated postures, which classifies it as a primary physical input","E":"It acts through the same peripheral COX inhibition as NSAIDs, lowering prostaglandin release in tender points"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The reading states that mindfulness meditation significantly reduces pain in experimental and clinical settings, including low back pain and fibromyalgia, and that neuroimaging and randomized trials show it reduces pain by engaging multiple unique nonopioidergic mechanisms that are distinct from placebo and vary across meditative training level. An opioid-mediated mechanism blocked by naloxone contradicts the nonopioidergic finding. A placebo-only effect contradicts the trials cited. Meditation is a primary psychological input; it does not act by stretching tissue. COX inhibition is the NSAID mechanism, not the mechanism of meditation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Opioid analgesia = mu receptor agonism, reversed by naloxone; mindfulness analgesia is nonopioidergic. Psychological inputs (CBT-I first-line for chronic insomnia, MBSR, hypnosis, biofeedback for migraine). COMLEX: Nonpharmacologic pain care reduces reliance on chronic opioids.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 7 — mechanism of action of opioid medications"},{"id":"sdl12_q27","sdlNumber":12,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Differentiate between psychological and physical integrative medicine.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with chronic low-back pain is treated with osteopathic spinal manipulative therapy. His wife asks what evidence supports this. According to ACP guidelines and current evidence, which statement best describes the evidence base for spinal manipulation in low-back pain?","choices":{"A":"Spinal manipulation is recommended against by ACP guidelines for low-back pain due to the risk of vascular injury during cervical manipulation, which outweighs any documented functional benefit in the lumbar spine","B":"Spinal manipulation has high-quality evidence demonstrating clear superiority to acupuncture, yoga, and massage therapy for both acute and chronic low-back pain across all patient age groups","C":"Spinal manipulation carries only a weak ACP recommendation for low-back pain because the evidence quality is rated high but the benefit magnitude is considered too small to justify its routine use clinically","D":"Spinal manipulation has a small effect on function and pain versus sham or inert control, and nonpharmacologic treatment including it carries a strong ACP recommendation amid opioid concerns","E":"Spinal manipulation is classified as alternative rather than complementary medicine by ACP guidelines and therefore falls outside the scope of its nonpharmacologic treatment recommendations for low-back pain"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Evidence for spinal manipulation: small effect on function and pain vs. control. ACP rates nonpharmacologic treatment (including spinal manipulation) as a STRONG recommendation for low-back pain, driven by opioid crisis concerns. Evidence quality is LOW (not high), but the strong recommendation reflects the importance of nonpharmacologic alternatives. Cervical manipulation has vascular risk concerns but this applies to the neck, not as a blanket recommendation against lumbar SMT.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Spinal manipulation = small effect, strong ACP recommendation (nonpharmacologic treatment category). Low-quality evidence overall but strong recommendation context = opioid avoidance. Cervical SMT has vascular risk (vertebral artery dissection) — important distinction from lumbar. COMLEX: OMM is the osteopathic version of spinal manipulation; understanding this evidence base prepares you to discuss it with patients.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 2 — Physical therapy modalities: ultrasound, phonophoresis, iontophoresis, electrical stimulation, low-level laser therapy"},{"id":"sdl12_q28","sdlNumber":12,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Discuss research challenges in the study of integrative care.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"The first large national survey documenting that more than 30% of Americans use complementary health products and practices surprised the medical community upon its publication. Who conducted this survey and in what year?","choices":{"A":"David Eisenberg and associates in 1993, whose survey revealed that more than 30% of Americans used complementary health products and practices, a finding that surprised the medical community and launched follow-up research","B":"The CDC National Center for Health Statistics in 1985, which performed the first National Health Interview Survey (NHIS) component specifically tracking complementary and alternative medicine use in U.S. households","C":"The National Institutes of Health NCCIH in 2002, which funded the first large-scale systematic review to establish prevalence estimates and annual out-of-pocket expenditures on complementary health practices","D":"The Agency for Healthcare Research and Quality in 1998, which established the first federal tracking system specifically documenting expenditures on complementary health practices as a proportion of total health spending","E":"The American Medical Association in 1975, which conducted the original national survey on patient self-referral to complementary health practitioners that prompted early professional interest in this phenomenon"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Eisenberg et al. 1993 = the landmark survey. Found >30% of Americans use complementary practices, surprising the medical community. The NHIS (conducted by CDC/NCHS) has since expanded this surveillance, surveying in 2002, 2007, 2012, 2017, and 2022. The 2022 NHIS found 36.7% use, up from 19.2% in 2002 for the seven tracked approaches.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Eisenberg 1993 = landmark survey showing >30% of Americans used complementary medicine. 2022 NHIS = 36.7% (vs. 19.2% in 2002). Out-of-pocket: $30.2 billion in 2012 (9.2% of total OOP costs). Pain = most common reason for complementary health use. COMLEX: Understanding the prevalence data helps osteopathic physicians recognize why integrative counseling is a routine clinical need.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 2 — Common mechanism of action for NSAID anti-inflammatory activity"},{"id":"sdl12_q29","sdlNumber":12,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Discuss research challenges in the study of integrative care.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A research team designs a study to evaluate a naturopathic treatment protocol for metabolic syndrome. They find that applying a classic parallel-group RCT design is problematic. Which feature of naturopathic medicine most directly creates this methodologic challenge?","choices":{"A":"Naturopathic practitioners lack licensure in most U.S. states, making study enrollment problematic because only licensed health care providers may legally serve as study investigators in federally funded clinical research","B":"The multicomponent, individualized nature of naturopathic protocols makes interventions hard to standardize, and pragmatic usual-care comparisons cannot identify which component produces the benefit","C":"FDA regulations require a separate investigational new drug application for each natural product included in a naturopathic protocol, creating an insurmountable regulatory barrier that prevents most such trials from being initiated","D":"Insurance companies uniformly deny reimbursement for naturopathic study visits, making participation financially prohibitive for patients with metabolic syndrome who face disproportionately high baseline health care costs","E":"Blinding is structurally impossible in all naturopathic research because both practitioners and participants can always identify whether active naturopathic treatment or a sham protocol is being administered"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Classic RCT requires a standardized, reproducible intervention. Naturopathy individualizes treatment — a given patient might receive different herbal, dietary, physical, and lifestyle combinations. Pragmatic trials with usual care comparators work better but cannot isolate which component helped. \"Manualized\" protocols with patient-level flexibility are being developed. Blinding challenges exist but are not unique to naturopathy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: RCT challenge in multicomponent integrative systems = individualization prevents standardization + cannot isolate active components. Solution = pragmatic designs + manualized protocols. The NCCIH funds much of this research. COMLEX: OMM research faces the same individualization challenge — treatment plans are patient-specific, making RCT design in osteopathic research similarly complex.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 2 — Common mechanism of action for NSAID anti-inflammatory activity"},{"id":"sdl12_q30","sdlNumber":12,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Discuss research challenges in the study of integrative care.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A health policy researcher asks you about U.S. out-of-pocket spending on complementary health practices based on the 2012 National Health Interview Survey data. Which statement accurately represents these expenditure findings?","choices":{"A":"Americans spent approximately $30.2 billion on complementary health practices in 2012, with the majority spent on yoga and meditation classes; natural product spending accounted for less than 5% of this total","B":"Americans spent approximately $5 billion on complementary health practices in 2012, primarily on acupuncture and chiropractic visits, representing a minor fraction of total out-of-pocket expenditures nationally","C":"Americans spent approximately $75 billion on complementary health practices in 2012, exceeding their total spending on conventional physician visits and making complementary care the single largest category of personal health expenditure","D":"Americans spent approximately $30.2 billion on complementary health practices in 2012, which exceeded total out-of-pocket spending on prescription drugs ($54.1 billion), making supplements the dominant out-of-pocket health cost","E":"Americans spent approximately $30.2 billion out of pocket in 2012 (9.2% of out-of-pocket health spending), including $12.8 billion on natural products, about one-quarter of what they spent on prescription drugs"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Exact 2012 NHIS data: $30.2 billion total OOP ($28.3B adults + $1.9B children). $14.7B on practitioner visits (vs. $49.6B conventional physicians). $12.8B on natural products (vs. $54.1B prescription drugs = ~24%). Represents 9.2% of total OOP costs and 1.1% of total health expenditures. Pain is the most common driver of spending.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know the 2012 expenditure data: $30.2B total; $14.7B practitioner visits; $12.8B natural products = ~24% of RX drug spending. This puts integrative medicine use in economic perspective. COMLEX: The high out-of-pocket cost burden reflects patient demand that is not covered by insurance — osteopathic physicians should be prepared to counsel on cost-effective integrative options.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 2 — Common mechanism of action for NSAID anti-inflammatory activity"}],"13":[{"id":"sdl13_q01","sdlNumber":13,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for shoulder pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which feature of a shoulder pain presentation most strongly suggests an EXTRINSIC rather than intrinsic cause?","choices":{"A":"Anterior shoulder pain aggravated by glenohumeral abduction and external rotation, with focal anterior joint line tenderness and a directional loss of motion corresponding to those same planes on examination","B":"Sharp, shooting, or electric pain radiating from the shoulder to the neck or down the arm, accompanied by dyspnea or diaphoresis, with symmetric shoulder appearance and glenohumeral motion unaffected on examination","C":"Gradually worsening anterior shoulder pain over several years with morning stiffness lasting less than 30 minutes, crepitus during motion, and age greater than 45 years suggesting glenohumeral osteoarthritis","D":"Lateral shoulder pain referred to the deltoid insertion on the humerus, worse at night and when sleeping on the affected side, associated with overhead activities and weakness if a significant rotator cuff tear is present","E":"Superior shoulder pain worsening with adduction and forward flexion, with localized tenderness directly over the acromioclavicular joint and a corresponding loss of motion in those specific loading planes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Extrinsic shoulder pain clues from the diagnostic workflow include: vague or poorly localized pain, pain associated with other symptoms such as dyspnea, abdominal pain, or diaphoresis, sharp or electric pain radiating to the neck or down the arm, a history concerning for extrinsic cause such as recent MVC or cardiac disease, glenohumeral motion does NOT reproduce the pain, and symmetric shoulder appearance, motion, and strength. All other choices describe intrinsic patterns (GH OA, rotator cuff, ACJ OA). The \"shooting with dyspnea\" combination is a classic red flag for referred or visceral pathology.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know the extrinsic shoulder pain red flags: dyspnea + shoulder pain = rule out cardiac/pulmonary; diaphoresis = autonomic activation from visceral source. Glenohumeral motion should NOT reproduce extrinsic pain. COMLEX: Osteopathic physicians use thorough somatic and visceral assessment — Chapman reflex for lung/heart can produce extrinsic shoulder pain in osteopathic context.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 3 — Glenohumeral joint dislocation as the primary trauma-positive finding in the shoulder pain diagnostic algorithm"},{"id":"sdl13_q02","sdlNumber":13,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for shoulder pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 60-year-old man presents with shoulder pain after a fall on an outstretched hand. Per the shoulder pain diagnostic algorithm, what is the FIRST branching point that determines the direction of the workup?","choices":{"A":"Determine whether the pattern of motion loss is directional (suggesting arthritis) or global (suggesting frozen shoulder) before proceeding to radiographs, since this classification avoids unnecessary imaging costs","B":"Determine whether the pain is primarily glenohumeral or extraglenohumereal based on whether gentle passive glenohumeral motion reproduces the pain during the physical examination portion of the visit","C":"Order plain radiographs as the first action in all shoulder pain presentations regardless of mechanism, since imaging is required before any classification of the shoulder pain source can be made","D":"Classify the shoulder complaint as intrinsic versus extrinsic based on pain character, associated symptoms, and the symmetry of shoulder appearance, motion, and strength on bilateral comparison examination","E":"Determine whether a significant history of trauma is present, since trauma versus no trauma is the first branch, routing toward imaging for fracture or dislocation versus assessment for intrinsic or extrinsic cause"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The UpToDate shoulder pain diagnostic workflow begins with a single gate: \"Is there a history of trauma?\" This is the primary bifurcation. Trauma-positive routes to history, physical exam, and plain radiographs to assess for fracture, glenohumeral dislocation, AC sprain, or sternoclavicular subluxation. Trauma-negative routes to the intrinsic versus extrinsic distinction. The intrinsic versus extrinsic differentiation and the glenohumeral versus extraglenohumereal distinction occur downstream from the trauma gate.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The trauma gate is intuitive but often tested in the context of missed fractures — always image a traumatized shoulder. COMLEX: Osteopathic evaluation adds palpatory assessment of the shoulder girdle (acromioclavicular, sternoclavicular, glenohumeral) and may identify somatic dysfunction at T2-T4 as a viscerosomatic source of shoulder pain.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 3 — Glenohumeral joint dislocation as the primary trauma-positive finding in the shoulder pain diagnostic algorithm"},{"id":"sdl13_q03","sdlNumber":13,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for shoulder pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which finding on physical examination best localizes a shoulder complaint to an EXTRAGLENOHUMEREAL (rather than glenohumeral intra-articular) intrinsic source?","choices":{"A":"Focal pain and tenderness outside the glenohumeral joint capsule, with little or no pain on gentle passive glenohumeral motion, pointing toward biceps tendinopathy, AC arthritis, subscapular bursitis, or distal clavicle osteolysis","B":"Anterior glenohumeral joint line tenderness with pain and loss of motion in abduction and external rotation that worsens with active resistance testing, consistent with a glenohumeral intra-articular source such as GH osteoarthritis","C":"Global loss of active and passive motion in multiple planes with preserved strength and absence of crepitus, consistent with a capsular process such as adhesive capsulitis of the glenohumeral joint interior","D":"Weakness of the supraspinatus on resisted abduction testing with a positive drop arm sign and pain referring to the lateral deltoid insertion on the humerus during overhead activity or active abduction","E":"Pain reproduced by gentle passive glenohumeral rotation, with anterior joint line tenderness and crepitus felt during passive motion, consistent with a glenohumeral intra-articular source"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Per the diagnostic workflow, extraglenohumereal causes are identified by two criteria: (1) focal pain and tenderness outside the GH joint, and (2) gentle passive motion of the glenohumeral joint generally does NOT cause pain. Extraglenohumereal conditions include biceps tendinopathy, biceps tendon tear, AC arthritis, scapulothoracic weakness, subscapular bursitis, and distal clavicle osteolysis. The other options describe glenohumeral patterns (GH OA, adhesive capsulitis, full-thickness cuff tear, and pain on passive GH motion).","boardPrep":"Step 1: The \"passive GH motion does not hurt\" rule is the key clinical divider. Biceps tendinopathy is the most classic extraglenohumereal diagnosis — Speed test and Yergason test assess it specifically. COMLEX: Biceps tendinopathy in the context of shoulder somatic dysfunction is a common COMLEX scenario where OMM to restore GH motion may reduce impingement on the biceps tendon.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 3 — Glenohumeral joint dislocation as the primary trauma-positive finding in the shoulder pain diagnostic algorithm"},{"id":"sdl13_q04","sdlNumber":13,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of shoulder osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 72-year-old woman has 3 years of gradually worsening anterior shoulder and upper arm pain, morning stiffness lasting 20 minutes, increased stiffness after prolonged rest, and loss of motion worst in abduction and external rotation. Exam shows anterior joint line tenderness and mild crepitus. Which diagnosis and mechanism best explains this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Adhesive capsulitis of the glenohumeral joint, characterized by idiopathic global loss of motion in all planes, preserved strength, pain-then-stiffness pattern, and stronger association with diabetes and thyroid disease than with age-related degeneration","B":"Glenohumeral osteoarthritis, with degenerative cartilage loss at the glenohumeral articulation producing usage-related anterior shoulder and upper arm pain that is directionally worst in abduction and external rotation, consistent with OARSI clinical criteria","C":"Acromioclavicular osteoarthritis, with degenerative changes at the superior shoulder articulation producing superior (not anterior) shoulder pain that is characteristically worst with adduction and forward flexion rather than abduction and external rotation","D":"Rotator cuff impingement with subacromial tendinosis, producing supraspinatus compression in the extra-articular subacromial space with pain referring to the lateral deltoid insertion on the humerus rather than diffusely to the upper arm","E":"Subacromial bursitis with fluid accumulation in the subdeltoid bursa causing anterior shoulder pain, but distinguished from OA by absence of crepitus, morning stiffness, and usage-related progressive course over years"},"correct":"B","explanation":"GH OA: most common type of arthritis at the GH joint; most patients >70 (women > men); anterior shoulder and upper arm pain (GH location); morning stiffness; stiffer with inactivity; loss of motion worst in abduction and external rotation; anterior joint line tenderness and crepitus. ACJ OA produces SUPERIOR shoulder pain worst with adduction/forward flexion. Adhesive capsulitis produces GLOBAL motion loss in all planes. Rotator cuff pain refers to the lateral humerus. These location and plane distinctions are high-yield board discriminators.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GH OA = anterior shoulder + upper arm, worst abduction/ER. ACJ OA = superior shoulder, worst adduction/forward flexion. COMLEX: GH OA has a COMLEX bump because shoulder somatic dysfunction and OMM are tested in the context of glenohumeral pathology and post-arthroplasty rehabilitation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis general framework: diagnostic criteria and stepwise management that apply directly to shoulder OA"},{"id":"sdl13_q05","sdlNumber":13,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of shoulder osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which statement most accurately distinguishes the clinical presentation of acromioclavicular joint osteoarthritis from glenohumeral osteoarthritis?","choices":{"A":"Acromioclavicular OA primarily affects patients under age 50 with prior overhead sports exposure, while glenohumeral OA primarily affects women over age 70 with prior dislocation, fracture, or massive rotator cuff tear history","B":"Acromioclavicular OA produces posterior shoulder pain worsening with internal rotation and extension, while glenohumeral OA produces lateral deltoid pain worsening with overhead motion due to subacromial compression","C":"Acromioclavicular OA produces superior shoulder pain that is worst with adduction and forward flexion, while glenohumeral OA produces anterior shoulder and upper arm pain that is worst with abduction and external rotation","D":"Acromioclavicular OA always requires radiographic joint space narrowing for clinical diagnosis, while glenohumeral OA is exclusively a clinical diagnosis that does not require or benefit from any radiographic assessment","E":"Acromioclavicular OA is managed with joint replacement as first-line therapy, while glenohumeral OA requires a stepwise conservative approach with pharmacologic management and physical therapy before surgical options"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The most tested distinction: ACJ OA = superior shoulder pain, worst with ADDUCTION and FORWARD FLEXION (planes that load the AC joint — cross-body adduction test is positive). GH OA = anterior shoulder and upper arm pain, worst with ABDUCTION and EXTERNAL ROTATION (planes that load the glenohumeral joint — GH joint line tenderness anteriorly). Both share similar diagnostic criteria (age ≥45, usage-related pain, morning stiffness ≤30 min) and both are managed with a similar OARSI-adapted framework. Joint replacement is a last resort for both.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Remember — ACJ = ADDUCTION worsens (cross-body maneuver). GH = ABDUCTION/ER worsens. These opposing plane findings appear frequently on USMLE. COMLEX: Both joints can develop somatic dysfunction and may respond to OMM — AC joint manipulation is a tested COMLEX technique.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis general framework: diagnostic criteria and stepwise management that apply directly to shoulder OA"},{"id":"sdl13_q06","sdlNumber":13,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of shoulder osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"According to the course, which statement best describes the epidemiology of glenohumeral and acromioclavicular osteoarthritis?","choices":{"A":"Most patients are older than 70 and women outnumber men; true prevalence is unclear because many patients are asymptomatic","B":"Most patients are younger than 50 and men outnumber women; prevalence is well defined because most patients have symptoms","C":"Rheumatoid arthritis, not osteoarthritis, is the most common type of arthritis in both joints, especially in older women","D":"Shoulder joints are the most common sites of osteoarthritis in the body, ahead of the knee and hip in every age group","E":"Most patients are teenage overhead athletes, because repeated throwing wears the glenoid cartilage before skeletal maturity"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The course states that glenohumeral and acromioclavicular OA are less common than OA of other joints, but that osteoarthritis is the most common type of arthritis seen in the GH and AC joints. Most patients are older than 70, women outnumber men, and prevalence is very difficult to determine because many patients are asymptomatic. A younger, male-predominant pattern with well-defined prevalence reverses these points. Rheumatoid arthritis is not the most common type in these joints. Shoulder joints are less commonly affected than other joints, not more. Shoulder OA is not a disease of adolescent throwers; risk factors are direct trauma, prior dislocation, prior fracture, and (for GH) severe rotator cuff tears.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Shoulder OA is a clinical diagnosis (usage-related pain, age ≥45, morning stiffness ≤30 min); GH OA = anterior pain worst in abduction/ER; AC OA = superior pain worst in adduction/forward flexion. COMLEX: \"The rotator cuff is not a joint\": cuff disease is a separate diagnosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 8 — knee osteoarthritis criteria and OARSI management"},{"id":"sdl13_q07","sdlNumber":13,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of shoulder pathologies, including rotator cuff injuries, bursitis, and AC joint arthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Rotator cuff impingement occurs in the subacromial space. Which anatomical fact about this space is most clinically important?","choices":{"A":"The subacromial space is an intra-articular compartment within the glenohumeral joint capsule, so glenohumeral corticosteroid injection simultaneously treats both impingement and GH osteoarthritis with a single injection procedure","B":"The subacromial space is extra-articular (outside the glenohumeral joint capsule), so impingement is distinct from glenohumeral joint disease and needs a targeted subacromial injection rather than an intra-articular injection","C":"The subacromial space is narrowed exclusively by edema of the supraspinatus tendon, while chronic tendinosis causing tendon thinning actually widens the space and reduces impingement because the tendon occupies less volume","D":"The subacromial space is a diarthrodial synovial joint lined by synovium, and inflammation of this space constitutes a septic bursitis that requires emergent aspiration, culture, and intravenous antibiotic therapy in all cases","E":"The subacromial space contains the infraspinatus tendon as its primary occupant, and infraspinatus impingement is the most common rotator cuff pathology because the posterior cuff is most exposed to mechanical stress"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The subacromial space is EXTRA-articular — outside the GH joint capsule. This is a key clinical distinction: subacromial injection targets the bursa and rotator cuff tendons directly, while GH intra-articular injection targets joint cartilage and synovium. The supraspinatus (not infraspinatus) occupies the subacromial space's \"critical zone.\" Two impingement mechanisms: (1) edematous supraspinatus too large to glide through the space; (2) thin/degenerated supraspinatus allows superior humeral migration, narrowing the space from the humeral side. Patients cycle between these states.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Extra-articular subacromial space = supraspinatus, subacromial-subdeltoid bursa. Intra-articular = GH joint capsule and cartilage. Injection target depends on pathology location. COMLEX: Subacromial injection is a common osteopathic procedural topic. Glenohumeral somatic dysfunction can reduce subacromial clearance — an OMM rationale for treating impingement conservatively.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 2 — Little league shoulder: pediatric rotator cuff overuse injury sharing the same subacromial anatomy as adult impingement"},{"id":"sdl13_q08","sdlNumber":13,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of shoulder pathologies, including rotator cuff injuries, bursitis, and AC joint arthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"The pathologic progression of rotator cuff tendinosis is best described as which of the following?","choices":{"A":"An acute inflammatory infiltration of the supraspinatus tendon that permanently resolves with anti-inflammatory treatment within 6 weeks, leaving no residual structural vulnerability or predisposition to re-injury in the affected tendon","B":"A process beginning with bursal-sided full-thickness tearing that propagates to the articular surface over time due to repetitive eccentric loading, and anti-inflammatory medications can halt propagation if initiated within the first 3 months","C":"Calcification of the supraspinatus tendon from calcium hydroxyapatite deposition that mechanically narrows the subacromial space, producing calcific tendinitis rather than true tendinosis with a distinct treatment algorithm","D":"Progressive glenohumeral joint capsule fibrosis triggered by rotator cuff pain inhibition, producing global loss of motion in all planes that is indistinguishable from primary adhesive capsulitis on clinical examination alone","E":"After about 3 months of ongoing cuff injury, the tendon becomes chronically degenerative with no inflammatory cells, showing disorganization and degeneration that can periodically re-inflame and perpetuate the impingement cycle"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Tendinosis vs. tendinitis: it is only a true \"tendinitis\" if acutely inflamed. Once chronic (approximately 3 months), no inflammatory cells are present — the tendon is in a state of disorganization and degeneration. This chronic state can periodically re-inflame, driving the rotator cuff impingement cycle. This distinction matters therapeutically: anti-inflammatories and corticosteroids address the acute inflammatory phase but do not reverse chronic degeneration. Surgery is reserved for recalcitrant cases and full or massive tears. PT is the mainstay of management throughout.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Tendinosis (3+ months, no inflammation, disorganized collagen) vs. tendinitis (acute, inflammatory cells, responds to NSAIDs). Classic board trap: calling a chronic rotator cuff condition \"tendinitis\" and treating only with NSAIDs. COMLEX: Osteopathic management of tendinosis includes prolotherapy and PRP (pro-inflammatory regenerative injections to stimulate repair) — listed explicitly in the course treatment options.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 2 — Little league shoulder: pediatric rotator cuff overuse injury sharing the same subacromial anatomy as adult impingement"},{"id":"sdl13_q09","sdlNumber":13,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of shoulder pathologies, including rotator cuff injuries, bursitis, and AC joint arthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which physical examination test specifically assesses rotator cuff tendon INTEGRITY (presence/absence of a significant tear) rather than the presence of impingement?","choices":{"A":"The Neer impingement sign, performed by stabilizing the scapula and passively forward flexing the internally rotated arm, which compresses the supraspinatus against the anterior acromion and is positive in subacromial impingement syndrome","B":"The Hawkins impingement test, performed by forward flexing the shoulder to 90 degrees and then passively internally rotating the arm, which compresses the supraspinatus against the coracoacromial ligament and is positive in impingement","C":"The cross-body adduction test, performed by passively adducting the arm horizontally across the chest at 90 degrees, which compresses the acromioclavicular joint and is positive in AC joint arthritis rather than rotator cuff pathology","D":"The Drop Arm Test, performed by passively abducting the patient's arm to 90 degrees and asking them to slowly lower it — inability to control the lowering indicates a significant full-thickness rotator cuff tear affecting active tendon function","E":"The Speed test, performed by forward flexing the arm against resistance with the elbow extended and forearm supinated, which loads the proximal biceps tendon and is positive in biceps tendinopathy rather than rotator cuff tear"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Per the course slides, tendon strength and integrity testing includes resisted abduction, external rotation, and internal rotation — PLUS the Drop Arm Test. The Drop Arm Test specifically tests whether the patient can actively control the lowered arm (requires intact supraspinatus function); inability = significant full-thickness tear. Neer's sign and Hawkins test assess for IMPINGEMENT. The distinction matters: impingement can occur with an intact tendon, while a positive Drop Arm suggests the tendon has lost functional integrity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Drop Arm = full-thickness tear (supraspinatus). Neer = impingement (supraspinatus against anterior acromion). Hawkins = impingement (supraspinatus against coracoacromial ligament). Empty Can test = supraspinatus strength. These physical exam tests are classic Step 1 discriminators. COMLEX: All of these tests are tested in COMLEX clinical skills and are part of the osteopathic shoulder structural examination.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 2 — Little league shoulder: pediatric rotator cuff overuse injury sharing the same subacromial anatomy as adult impingement"},{"id":"sdl13_q10","sdlNumber":13,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of frozen shoulder (adhesive capsulitis).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 54-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes presents with 6 months of gradually worsening right shoulder stiffness. She initially had pain that has now lessened; she has difficulty reaching overhead, behind her back, and across her body equally. Strength is 5/5 throughout. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Glenohumeral osteoarthritis, producing directional loss of motion specifically worst in abduction and external rotation with anterior joint line tenderness, morning stiffness under 30 minutes, and crepitus in a woman over age 45","B":"Rotator cuff tendinopathy with impingement, presenting with overhead pain, subacromial tenderness, a positive Neer or Hawkins sign, and pain referring to the lateral deltoid insertion on the humerus during resisted abduction testing","C":"Adhesive capsulitis (frozen shoulder), presenting with global loss of motion in multiple planes without significant strength loss or pain on resistance testing, in a patient at peak age (mid-50s) with a well-established risk factor (type 2 diabetes)","D":"Acromioclavicular osteoarthritis with secondary bursitis, producing superior shoulder stiffness and multiple planes of motion restriction due to AC joint osteophyte formation causing mechanical compression of the subacromial space","E":"Cervical radiculopathy at C5-C6, producing referred shoulder stiffness and apparent global motion loss due to chronic pain inhibition of the rotator cuff musculature and secondary periarticular contracture in a patient with diabetic neuropathy"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Adhesive capsulitis hallmarks: global loss of motion in ALL planes (not directional), no other structural explanation, gradual onset, pain then stiffness pattern (pain often lessens as stiffness progresses), preserved strength, peak mid-50s, and strong association with diabetes (most important metabolic risk factor), thyroid disease, hyperlipidemia, stroke, and autoimmune disease. Lifetime prevalence 2-5%; affects 8.2% of males and 10.1% of females (working age). GH OA would have directional loss (worst abduction/ER). Rotator cuff impingement preserves most motion. Imaging is NOT helpful for diagnosing frozen shoulder — it may identify other conditions.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Frozen shoulder = diabetes association (strongest), bilateral in 6-17% of patients, global motion loss in all planes. Classic board presentation is a middle-aged diabetic woman with progressively restricted shoulder motion in all planes. COMLEX: Adhesive capsulitis management includes OMM to restore GH motion — glenohumeral articulation with high-velocity low-amplitude technique or muscle energy is a tested COMLEX treatment approach.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 1 — Humerus fractures: prolonged immobilization post-fracture is a leading secondary cause of adhesive capsulitis"},{"id":"sdl13_q11","sdlNumber":13,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of frozen shoulder (adhesive capsulitis).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following is a recognized SECONDARY cause of adhesive capsulitis (frozen shoulder)?","choices":{"A":"Cervical C5-C6 radiculopathy in a manual laborer, which refers sharp pain into the shoulder and limits active elevation while gentle passive glenohumeral motion remains full and painless on examination","B":"Glenohumeral osteoarthritis with severe cartilage erosion, which progresses to capsular contracture when osteophytes mechanically block abduction and external rotation until the joint develops secondary adhesive changes","C":"Acromioclavicular joint arthritis with superior osteophyte formation that bridges the AC joint space, causing progressive mechanical impingement of the rotator cuff that leads to secondary GH capsular fibrosis over years","D":"Prolonged immobilization following a humerus fracture or a post-operative shoulder procedure, during which capsular contracture and adhesions form without adequate range of motion exercise and joint mobilization therapy","E":"Subacromial bursitis with marked edema of the subdeltoid bursa compressing the glenohumeral capsule externally, causing secondary fibrosis and reduced joint volume indistinguishable from primary idiopathic frozen shoulder"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Secondary causes of adhesive capsulitis listed in the course slides: rotator cuff disease, humerus fracture, prolonged immobilization, and post-operative procedures. Immobilization prevents the normal joint motion that maintains capsular compliance, allowing contracture and adhesion formation. Cervical radiculopathy is an extrinsic source of shoulder pain and does not contract the glenohumeral capsule; glenohumeral OA, AC arthritis, and subacromial bursitis restrict motion directionally or not at all. Idiopathic (primary) frozen shoulder is associated with diabetes, thyroid disease, hyperlipidemia, stroke, and autoimmune disease — these are NOT secondary causes. This distinction matters because prevention after humerus fracture or shoulder surgery requires aggressive early ROM, and failure to do so produces iatrogenic frozen shoulder.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Secondary frozen shoulder triggers: rotator cuff disease, humerus fracture, prolonged immobilization, post-op. Primary triggers: diabetes (strongest), thyroid disease, hyperlipidemia. The course slides explicitly list these categories. COMLEX: Post-surgical frozen shoulder is a preventable complication when OMM-guided early shoulder mobilization is incorporated into rehabilitation — an osteopathic perioperative care concept.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 1 — Humerus fractures: prolonged immobilization post-fracture is a leading secondary cause of adhesive capsulitis"},{"id":"sdl13_q12","sdlNumber":13,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of frozen shoulder (adhesive capsulitis).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"What is the standard first-line treatment approach for adhesive capsulitis (frozen shoulder), and what is the escalation step if initial management fails?","choices":{"A":"Glenohumeral intra-articular corticosteroid injection (or oral glucocorticoids) combined with physical therapy to restore range of motion as soon as possible, escalating to manipulation under anesthesia if this approach fails to restore adequate ROM","B":"Subacromial corticosteroid injection combined with NSAIDs and activity modification, targeting the subacromial bursa to reduce impingement-related inflammation and allow passive stretching to gradually restore capsular compliance over weeks","C":"Immediate surgical arthroscopic capsular release as first-line treatment due to the irreversible fibrotic nature of adhesive capsulitis, with glenohumeral injection reserved only for patients who are not surgical candidates due to comorbidities","D":"Physical therapy stretching alone for 12 to 18 months without any corticosteroid injection, since evidence shows corticosteroids worsen long-term outcomes by masking pain and reducing the patient's motivation to perform home exercises","E":"Platelet-rich plasma injection into the glenohumeral joint combined with hyaluronic acid viscosupplementation, which outperforms corticosteroids for the capsular fibrosis of frozen shoulder in meta-analyses of randomized controlled trials"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Treatment of adhesive capsulitis from the course slides: (1) PARAMOUNT — restore ROM as soon as possible; (2) FIRST LINE — glenohumeral intra-articular corticosteroid injection OR oral glucocorticoids + physical therapy; (3) ESCALATION — manipulation under anesthesia if the above fails. The injection target is GLENOHUMERAL (intra-articular) because the pathology is capsular, not subacromial. Arthroscopic capsular release exists but is not first-line. The emphasis on early ROM reflects the pathophysiology: continued immobility worsens capsular fibrosis and adhesion formation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Frozen shoulder Rx = GH intra-articular injection + PT → manipulation under anesthesia if failure. NOT subacromial injection (that targets rotator cuff/bursa). NOT immediate surgery. The intra-articular versus subacromial distinction is a classic board discriminator. COMLEX: OMM (articulatory/muscle energy) may be used as an adjunct to restore GH motion in adhesive capsulitis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 1 — Humerus fractures: prolonged immobilization post-fracture is a leading secondary cause of adhesive capsulitis"},{"id":"sdl13_q13","sdlNumber":13,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for shoulder pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman presents to the emergency department after a motor vehicle collision. She has right shoulder pain. Examination reveals diffuse, poorly localized shoulder pain without focal joint line tenderness. Glenohumeral range of motion is symmetric to the left and does not reproduce her pain. She also reports mild shortness of breath and diaphoresis. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?","choices":{"A":"Perform supraspinatus strength testing in resisted abduction and external rotation followed by Neer and Hawkins impingement maneuvers to assess for a traumatic rotator cuff tear associated with the motor vehicle collision","B":"Order MRI of the right shoulder with contrast to evaluate for labral tear, glenoid rim fracture, or glenohumeral instability, given the history of high-energy trauma from the motor vehicle collision mechanism of injury","C":"Order plain radiographs of the right shoulder to assess subacromial space dimension and acromial morphology, since plain films are the first imaging modality for all traumatically injured shoulders in the emergency setting","D":"Evaluate for an extrinsic or referred cause of her shoulder complaint, given poorly localized pain, unaffected glenohumeral motion, symmetric shoulder appearance, dyspnea, and diaphoresis suggesting a visceral source","E":"Perform glenohumeral joint aspiration to rule out post-traumatic hemarthrosis, since bloody effusion within the GH joint capsule is the most common cause of diffuse shoulder pain following a motor vehicle collision"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Despite the trauma history, this patient has multiple extrinsic pain markers: diffuse poorly localized pain, GH motion does NOT reproduce pain, symmetric shoulder appearance and motion, AND associated dyspnea and diaphoresis. These autonomic and respiratory symptoms alongside a trauma mechanism (MVC) raise concern for pneumothorax, cardiac contusion, aortic injury, or other thoracic pathology producing referred shoulder pain. The shoulder workflow directs evaluation for extrinsic cause when these features are present, regardless of trauma history. Proceeding directly to shoulder-specific imaging without considering the broader clinical picture would miss a potentially life-threatening cause.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Diaphoresis + dyspnea + diffuse shoulder pain after MVC = rule out thoracic/cardiac cause first. Referred pain to the left shoulder (Kehr sign) from diaphragm irritation (spleen rupture) is a classic Step 1 scenario. COMLEX: Osteopathic physicians trained in viscerosomatic reflexes recognize that T1-T5 somatic dysfunction can represent referred visceral pain — thoracic inlet assessment is always part of the OMM shoulder evaluation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 3 — Glenohumeral joint dislocations: presentation and management"},{"id":"sdl13_q14","sdlNumber":13,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for shoulder pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 41-year-old man falls off a bicycle onto his right shoulder. He has shoulder pain, and his history and examination are documented. Plain radiographs show no fracture of the clavicle or proximal humerus, no glenohumeral dislocation, no AC sprain, and no sternoclavicular subluxation. According to the shoulder pain workflow taught in the course, what is the next step?","choices":{"A":"Close the trauma pathway and evaluate for an extrinsic cause, such as cardiac or abdominal disease referring pain to the shoulder","B":"Order an MRI arthrogram before any further examination, because normal radiographs after trauma mean a labral tear is likely","C":"Evaluate for intrinsic soft tissue injury and decide whether the findings point to a glenohumeral or extraglenohumeral cause","D":"Refer directly to orthopedic surgery, because any shoulder trauma with pain and normal radiographs needs operative exploration","E":"Repeat the plain radiographs in 2 weeks, because occult fractures of the humerus become visible as resorption widens the line"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In the UpToDate shoulder pain workflow on the course slide, the trauma branch starts with history, examination (which may include ultrasound), and plain radiographs. If imaging shows a fracture, glenohumeral dislocation, AC sprain, or sternoclavicular injury, management is started and orthopedics is consulted as indicated. If none is found, the workflow moves to evaluating for intrinsic soft tissue injury and deciding between a glenohumeral cause (pain, weakness, or abnormal motion on glenohumeral exam, such as rotator cuff disease, labral tear, or instability) and an extraglenohumeral cause (focal tenderness outside the joint with painless gentle passive motion). Extrinsic causes are considered in the no-trauma branch. An MRI arthrogram is not the next step before the clinical examination sorts the cause. Orthopedic referral is for an identified structural injury. Repeat radiographs are not the workflow step.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Shoulder pain algorithm: trauma? → imaging for fracture/dislocation/AC/SC injury → if negative, intrinsic soft tissue injury (glenohumeral vs extraglenohumeral). No trauma → intrinsic vs extrinsic (referred) cause. COMLEX: Glenohumeral causes include rotator cuff disease, adhesive capsulitis, instability, labral tear, GH arthritis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 3 — clavicle fracture presentation and imaging"},{"id":"sdl13_q15","sdlNumber":13,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze and apply the diagnostic workflow for shoulder pain.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 35-year-old male recreational rock climber presents with 6 weeks of right shoulder pain after a bouldering fall. He reports diffuse anterior shoulder pain. On exam, the glenohumeral joint produces pain and restricted motion with passive testing. Bilateral shoulder comparison reveals asymmetric shoulder contour and motion. Radiographs show no fracture but confirm the humeral head is displaced anteriorly relative to the glenoid. Per the shoulder pain diagnostic algorithm, which pathway does this patient follow?","choices":{"A":"Trauma-negative pathway: assess whether the pain pattern suggests intrinsic versus extrinsic cause, then determine if examination findings are consistent with a glenohumeral versus extraglenohumereal etiology before ordering any imaging studies","B":"Trauma-positive pathway: obtain the history of trauma, perform a physical exam, obtain plain radiographs, and identify the specific structural finding (here, glenohumeral dislocation), then manage and refer to orthopedics as indicated","C":"Trauma-positive pathway: first determine whether the cause is glenohumeral or extraglenohumereal by assessing whether passive GH motion produces pain, then order MRI for soft tissue characterization before plain radiographs given the high-energy mechanism","D":"Trauma-negative intrinsic pathway: classify as a glenohumeral cause and order MRI with contrast to evaluate for labral pathology associated with the traumatic mechanism, reserving plain radiographs for elderly patients with concern for osteoporosis","E":"Extrinsic cause pathway: the diffuse nature of the pain and the history of trauma from a fall suggest referred visceral pain and a cardiac or pulmonary source should be excluded before any shoulder-specific imaging or intervention is initiated"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Trauma is present → trauma-positive pathway: history + exam + plain radiographs + identify specific pathology. This patient has glenohumeral dislocation (anterior displacement of humeral head, asymmetric contour, restricted motion). Per the workflow: workup and imaging reveal a specific diagnosis (fracture, GH dislocation, AC sprain, SCJ subluxation) → initiate appropriate management → refer to orthopedics as indicated. The workflow explicitly lists glenohumeral dislocation as a trauma-positive finding. The extraglenohumereal and passive motion assessment come AFTER the trauma gate routes to \"no\" (trauma-negative).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Anterior GH dislocation (most common) presents with arm held in slight abduction and external rotation, humeral head palpable anteriorly. Axillary nerve injury (deltoid weakness, lateral arm numbness) is the classic associated nerve injury. COMLEX: Post-dislocation shoulder somatic dysfunction is a COMLEX scenario — glenohumeral restriction (especially external rotation) often persists after reduction and may respond to OMM.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 3 — Glenohumeral joint dislocations: presentation and management"},{"id":"sdl13_q16","sdlNumber":13,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of shoulder osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 74-year-old woman has a 5-year history of right anterior shoulder and upper arm pain progressively worsening with activity. Morning stiffness lasts 25 minutes. Exam shows anterior joint line tenderness, pain and reduced motion worst in abduction and external rotation, mild crepitus, and no weakness. She has a history of right glenohumeral dislocation at age 45. Which management approach is MOST consistent with the OARSI-adapted framework taught for this condition?","choices":{"A":"Immediate referral for total shoulder arthroplasty given her 5-year disease duration and prior dislocation history, since surgical joint replacement is indicated for glenohumeral OA once conservative management has exceeded 3 years","B":"Subacromial corticosteroid injection as the primary pharmacologic intervention, targeting the subacromial space and rotator cuff rather than the glenohumeral joint space, since reactive bursitis from cartilage degeneration causes most of the pain","C":"A stepwise approach beginning with pharmacologic management, physical therapy, and exercise, escalating to intra-articular corticosteroid injections, and reserving regenerative injections, acupuncture, and arthroplasty for refractory cases","D":"Long-term daily oral glucocorticoid therapy as the pharmacologic anchor of GH OA management combined with passive physical therapy modalities, avoiding exercise due to concerns that loading an arthritic joint accelerates cartilage degradation","E":"Avoidance of physical therapy and exercise because evidence shows resistance training accelerates cartilage loss in glenohumeral osteoarthritis, making all weight-bearing and load-bearing shoulder activities contraindicated in elderly women"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The OARSI-adapted framework for shoulder OA management (from the course slides): pharmacologic management → physical therapy and exercise → intra-articular corticosteroid injections → regenerative injections (prolotherapy, PRP, stem cells) → acupuncture → joint replacement. It is escalating and stepwise. PT/exercise is a core component (NOT contraindicated). The injection for GH OA is INTRA-ARTICULAR (not subacromial — that targets rotator cuff/bursa). Daily oral glucocorticoids are NOT the anchor. Prior dislocation is a known risk factor for GH OA but does not accelerate the timing for surgery.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GH OA treatment is conservative-first: analgesics, PT, intra-articular injection, then surgery. The injection goes IN the joint (intra-articular), not above it (subacromial). This distinction is tested. COMLEX: Acupuncture is listed as a treatment option in the OARSI framework for OA — integrative medicine approaches for musculoskeletal OA are COMLEX-tested, connecting to SDL 12 content.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis: epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management in general"},{"id":"sdl13_q17","sdlNumber":13,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of shoulder osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 74-year-old woman has 3 years of gradually worsening anterior shoulder and upper arm pain, stiffness that is worse after inactivity, and loss of abduction and external rotation. Radiographs show glenohumeral joint space narrowing with osteophytes. Which item in her history is listed in the course as a risk factor for this condition?","choices":{"A":"A displaced proximal humerus fracture treated 20 years ago after a fall onto the shoulder","B":"A history of lateral epicondylitis on the same side treated with a counterforce brace","C":"Years of daily swimming laps, which the course lists as the leading cause of shoulder OA","D":"Long-term use of an oral bisphosphonate started after a vertebral compression fracture","E":"A prior episode of adhesive capsulitis on the opposite shoulder that resolved with therapy"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The course lists risk factors for glenohumeral and AC osteoarthritis as direct trauma, prior dislocation, prior fracture, and, for the glenohumeral joint, severe rotator cuff tears. A remote proximal humerus fracture is a prior fracture involving the joint region. Lateral epicondylitis is an elbow tendinopathy and is not a shoulder OA risk factor. Swimming is not listed as a risk factor; overhead activity is listed for rotator cuff injury instead. Bisphosphonate use is not a listed risk factor. Adhesive capsulitis on the opposite side raises the chance of contralateral frozen shoulder (6 to 17%), not of osteoarthritis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Post-traumatic arthritis follows intra-articular injury, malunion, and chondrocyte death. Shoulder OA risk: trauma, prior dislocation or fracture, massive cuff tear (cuff tear arthropathy). COMLEX: Treatment follows an OARSI-adapted ladder: analgesics → PT/exercise → IA steroid → regenerative injection/acupuncture → arthroplasty.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 1 — proximal humerus fractures in older adults"},{"id":"sdl13_q18","sdlNumber":13,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of shoulder osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 68-year-old man with a known massive rotator cuff tear managed conservatively for 4 years presents with new progressive anterior shoulder pain and loss of motion. Radiographs now show superior migration of the humeral head toward the acromion. Morning stiffness lasts 25 minutes. Exam: anterior joint line tenderness, pain and loss of abduction and external rotation, crepitus. Strength testing shows weakness. Which complication best explains his new presentation?","choices":{"A":"Progressive extension of the rotator cuff tear into the subscapularis tendon, producing new internal rotation weakness and anterior shoulder pain at the lesser tuberosity rather than anterior joint line tenderness indicating cartilage involvement","B":"Development of isolated subacromial bursitis due to mechanical impingement by the superiorly migrated humeral head, producing anterior and lateral shoulder pain that is distinct from the progressive articular cartilage degeneration of OA","C":"Glenohumeral instability secondary to the massive rotator cuff tear, with the humeral head subluxing anteriorly during abduction attempts and causing anterior joint pain that mimics OA without true articular cartilage degeneration","D":"Glenohumeral osteoarthritis secondary to the massive rotator cuff tear, since severe cuff tears are a recognized risk factor for GH OA through superior humeral migration and resulting cartilage damage (rotator cuff tear arthropathy)","E":"Adhesive capsulitis triggered by pain-mediated disuse of the shoulder, producing secondary capsular contracture with global motion loss in all planes in a patient whose rotator cuff deficiency impairs normal glenohumeral mechanics"},"correct":"D","explanation":"From the course slides: GH OA risk factors include direct trauma, prior dislocation, prior fracture, AND severe (massive) rotator cuff tears. Massive cuff tears permit superior migration of the humeral head (visible as \"high-riding humerus\" on radiograph), which shears articular cartilage and produces GH OA — called cuff tear arthropathy. Clinical features: anterior joint line tenderness, directional loss (worst abduction/ER), morning stiffness ≤30 min, crepitus, and in advanced cases weakness from combined articular and tendon dysfunction. Adhesive capsulitis would show GLOBAL, not directional, loss of motion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Massive rotator cuff tear → superior humeral migration (\"high-riding humerus\" on X-ray) → cuff tear arthropathy (secondary GH OA). This is a tested progression. The subacromial space becomes so narrow it is obliterated. COMLEX: Rotator cuff tear arthropathy is managed with shoulder arthroplasty (reverse total shoulder arthroplasty) in surgical candidates — a COMLEX management escalation point.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis: epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management in general"},{"id":"sdl13_q19","sdlNumber":13,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of shoulder pathologies, including rotator cuff injuries, bursitis, and AC joint arthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 52-year-old female physical therapist has 14 months of right shoulder pain worst with overhead activity and when sleeping on the right side. Pain refers to the lateral deltoid region. Abduction is limited to 110 degrees. Neer and Hawkins tests are both positive. Drop Arm Test is negative. MRI shows supraspinatus edema without tear. Which mechanism BEST explains her impingement?","choices":{"A":"The supraspinatus has undergone full-thickness articular-sided tearing, allowing the humeral head to migrate superiorly and mechanically narrow the subacromial space from the humeral side, consistent with the thinning mechanism of impingement","B":"The subdeltoid bursa has ruptured into the glenohumeral joint capsule, creating a reactive synovitis that directly compresses the supraspinatus tendon at the musculotendinous junction from within the intra-articular compartment","C":"The supraspinatus has calcified from calcium hydroxyapatite deposition in the critical zone, creating a fixed mechanical block in the subacromial space that prevents normal tendon gliding regardless of the inflammation state","D":"The supraspinatus has undergone chronic tendinosis with complete loss of inflammatory cells and collagen disorganization, causing tendon thinning and superior humeral migration that narrows the subacromial space from the humeral side as a separate process","E":"The supraspinatus tendon is acutely edematous and too large to glide smoothly through the subacromial space, representing the edematous-tendon mechanism of impingement rather than the degenerative thinning mechanism that allows superior humeral migration"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The course slides describe two impingement mechanisms: (1) Edematous supraspinatus — tendon becomes too large to glide through subacromial space → mechanical impingement from the tendon side; (2) Thin supraspinatus from chronic tendinosis — humerus shifts superiorly → space narrows from the humeral side. The negative Drop Arm Test and MRI showing edema without tear exclude a significant full-thickness tear. 14 months is chronic but patients cycle between states. The current state is edema-driven impingement (positive Neer/Hawkins, edema on MRI, no tear). The thinning/tendinosis mechanism would be the other part of the cycle.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Two mechanisms of subacromial impingement — edema (tendon too big) vs. thinning (humerus migrates up). Both produce positive impingement tests. MRI differentiates edema (no tear) from tendinosis/tear. Drop Arm negative = no significant full-thickness tear. COMLEX: Impingement cycle (edema → thinning → re-inflammation) is the pathophysiologic rationale for PT, injections, and avoiding cortisone repeatedly (degrades collagen in already-diseased tendon).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — Little league shoulder and elbow: pediatric rotator cuff and proximal humeral stress injury in the same subacromial anatomy"},{"id":"sdl13_q20","sdlNumber":13,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of shoulder pathologies, including rotator cuff injuries, bursitis, and AC joint arthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 45-year-old male contractor has 3 weeks of acute right shoulder pain triggered by overhead work. Exam: positive Hawkins test, tenderness over the greater tuberosity, painful arc between 60 and 120 degrees of abduction. Radiograph shows superior humeral migration with subacromial space narrowing. Which imaging modality would BEST characterize the SOFT TISSUE pathology of his rotator cuff?","choices":{"A":"CT arthrogram of the right shoulder with intravenous contrast, which provides superior visualization of bony architecture and articular cartilage compared to MRI or ultrasound and is the preferred modality for subacromial space assessment","B":"Repeat plain radiographs of the right shoulder in three additional projections, since multiple-view radiography is the most sensitive modality for diagnosing partial-thickness rotator cuff tears and quantifying the degree of tendon involvement","C":"MRI or musculoskeletal ultrasound of the right shoulder, as both offer equivalent sensitivity for soft tissue pathology including rotator cuff tendinosis, partial-thickness tears, full-thickness tears, and subacromial bursitis","D":"Technetium bone scan of the right shoulder, which identifies areas of increased metabolic activity in the supraspinatus insertion and acromion corresponding to mechanical stress and is more sensitive than MRI for subacromial impingement","E":"Arthrographic fluoroscopy of the glenohumeral joint with contrast injection, which is the gold standard for detecting full-thickness rotator cuff tears by demonstrating contrast communication between the joint space and the subacromial bursa"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Per the course slides: (1) Radiographs assess subacromial space dimension and acromion shape — good for bony architecture and space measurement. (2) MRI is sensitive and specific for soft tissue pathology, especially with contrast for cartilage and labral pathology; NOT as helpful for spacing/arthritis as radiographs. (3) Musculoskeletal ultrasound has EQUIVALENT sensitivity to MRI for soft tissue pathology. This equivalence is the high-yield teaching point — ultrasound is increasingly preferred in clinical practice for its availability, lower cost, and dynamic imaging capability. CT arthrogram and bone scans are not first-line.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MRI = gold standard for soft tissue shoulder pathology. MSK ultrasound = equivalent sensitivity. X-ray = bony architecture + subacromial space. Know when to use each. COMLEX: Musculoskeletal ultrasound is used by osteopathic sports medicine physicians for real-time guided injection procedures (subacromial, glenohumeral) — a COMLEX procedural competency.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — Little league shoulder and elbow: pediatric rotator cuff and proximal humeral stress injury in the same subacromial anatomy"},{"id":"sdl13_q21","sdlNumber":13,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of shoulder pathologies, including rotator cuff injuries, bursitis, and AC joint arthritis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 60-year-old male has had rotator cuff tendinosis for 8 months. He has taken NSAIDs and completed 16 weeks of physical therapy without significant improvement. MRI shows a partial-thickness bursal-sided rotator cuff tear with intact articular surface. He continues to have significant pain with overhead activity. Which of the following represents the most appropriate next step in management?","choices":{"A":"Subacromial corticosteroid injection targeting the subacromial subdeltoid bursa to address the inflammatory component, which is the injection step in the rotator cuff treatment hierarchy after analgesics and physical therapy fail","B":"Immediate referral for arthroscopic surgical repair, since any bursal-sided partial-thickness tear is a mandatory indication for surgical intervention after 8 months of conservative management regardless of tear thickness or percentage","C":"Platelet-rich plasma injection into the glenohumeral joint as the standard evidence-based second-line treatment after physical therapy failure, given its Level I evidence for partial-thickness rotator cuff tears in systematic reviews","D":"Glenohumeral intra-articular corticosteroid injection targeting the joint space rather than the subacromial space, since the bursal-sided tear involves the bursal compartment which communicates directly with the GH joint interior","E":"MRI arthrography with gadolinium contrast to reclassify the tear size and compartment involvement before any further intervention, since precise tear classification is required to determine the surgical versus conservative management threshold"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Rotator cuff treatment hierarchy: analgesics → physical therapy (mainstay) → injections (subacromial corticosteroid for anti-inflammatory, OR regenerative options: prolotherapy/PRP/stem cells) → surgery (recalcitrant cases, full-thickness tears, massive tears). After 8 months of analgesics + PT failure, injections are next. For bursal-sided pathology, SUBACROMIAL injection directly targets the subacromial-subdeltoid bursa and the tendon surface. Surgery (arthroscopic repair) is indicated for FULL-THICKNESS and MASSIVE tears, not routinely for partial-thickness. PRP is a regenerative (pro-inflammatory) option, not the standard first injection choice, and goes into the subacromial space not the GH joint. The bursa does NOT communicate with the GH joint in a partial tear.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Partial tear → conservative management including injection. Full or massive tear → surgery. The treatment escalation ladder is a classic board sequence. Bursal-sided tear = subacromial space injection. COMLEX: PRP and prolotherapy (pro-inflammatory, regenerative injections) are explicitly listed in the course slides as treatment options — COMLEX tests knowledge of regenerative injection indications.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — Little league shoulder and elbow: pediatric rotator cuff and proximal humeral stress injury in the same subacromial anatomy"},{"id":"sdl13_q22","sdlNumber":13,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of frozen shoulder (adhesive capsulitis).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes and hypothyroidism presents with 9 months of progressively worsening right shoulder stiffness. She initially had significant pain that has partially resolved; she now has difficulty with forward flexion, abduction, internal rotation, and external rotation equally. Strength is 5/5 throughout. Radiographs are unremarkable. Which mechanism best explains the underlying pathophysiology?","choices":{"A":"Progressive supraspinatus tendinosis with eccentric overload producing bursal-sided tendon thinning and superior humeral migration, creating mechanical restriction of glenohumeral abduction and external rotation in a patient with diabetes-impaired tendon healing","B":"Glenohumeral osteoarthritis accelerated by diabetes-related metabolic syndrome, with articular cartilage degradation producing directional loss of motion worst in abduction and external rotation and progressive joint space narrowing visible on plain radiography","C":"Massive rotator cuff tear arthropathy with superior humeral migration, producing weakness on manual testing and combined articular and tendon dysfunction in a patient whose hypothyroidism predisposes to tendon collagen fragility and multidirectional tear extension","D":"Cervical spondylosis at C5-C6 producing referred shoulder stiffness with pain inhibition of the rotator cuff musculature and sympathetically mediated capsular contracture in a patient with diabetic autonomic neuropathy affecting proprioceptive feedback","E":"Idiopathic adhesive capsulitis (frozen shoulder) of poorly understood pathology, producing global loss of motion in all planes with no structural radiographic explanation, and strongly associated with diabetes mellitus and thyroid disease as metabolic risk factors"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Classic adhesive capsulitis: global loss of motion in ALL planes (forward flexion + abduction + IR + ER — this multiplanar global pattern is the key distinguishing feature), no structural radiographic explanation (radiographs NOT helpful for diagnosis), preserved strength, pain-then-stiffness pattern (pain may lessen as stiffness progresses), peak mid-50s. Strong associations: diabetes (strongest), thyroid disease, hyperlipidemia, stroke, autoimmune disease. Pathology is poorly understood (idiopathic). GH OA would show directional loss (worst abduction/ER) and likely radiographic changes. Rotator cuff tear arthropathy would show weakness and radiographic superior migration.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Frozen shoulder = global motion loss in all planes + diabetes association + unremarkable imaging + peak mid-50s. Bilateral in 6-17%. Preserved strength distinguishes it from massive rotator cuff tears. COMLEX: Osteopathic physicians use glenohumeral articulation and muscle energy techniques to restore joint motion in adhesive capsulitis — OMM is a non-pharmacologic intervention that directly addresses the ROM loss.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 1 — Humerus fractures: epidemiology, mechanism, presentation, imaging, and management"},{"id":"sdl13_q23","sdlNumber":13,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of frozen shoulder (adhesive capsulitis).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 48-year-old man underwent open rotator cuff repair 4 months ago and was immobilized in a sling for 8 weeks per surgical protocol. He now presents with severe restriction of right shoulder motion in all planes equally. His surgeon confirms there are no hardware complications, no re-tear on ultrasound, and no incisional scar tissue limiting motion. What is the most likely diagnosis and most important treatment principle?","choices":{"A":"Rotator cuff re-tear at the surgical repair site causing protective guarding and apparent global motion limitation, requiring repeat MRI before initiating any physical therapy to confirm tendon integrity before applying passive mobilization forces","B":"Secondary adhesive capsulitis from prolonged post-operative immobilization, for which the paramount principle is restoring range of motion quickly with glenohumeral intra-articular corticosteroid injection plus physical therapy","C":"Post-operative hemarthrosis of the glenohumeral joint causing secondary capsular fibrosis from blood-derived fibrin deposition, requiring ultrasound-guided joint aspiration to remove residual hemarthrosis before physical therapy can be effective","D":"Complex regional pain syndrome type II developing after open shoulder surgery, producing diffuse allodynia, autonomic changes, and apparent motion limitation that requires a multidisciplinary pain management approach rather than joint mobilization","E":"Normal expected post-operative stiffness after open rotator cuff repair, which is expected to persist for 3 to 6 months after surgery and requires only reassurance, analgesics, and continuation of the sling for another 4 weeks before exercises begin"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Prolonged post-operative immobilization is an explicit secondary cause of adhesive capsulitis in the course slides. The hallmark remains global loss of motion in all planes with no other structural explanation. The cardinal treatment principle from the course slides is: \"It is paramount to restore ROM as soon as possible.\" First-line treatment: glenohumeral intra-articular corticosteroid injection (or oral glucocorticoids) + PT. If this fails: manipulation under anesthesia. This case illustrates an iatrogenic frozen shoulder from excessive immobilization after rotator cuff repair — early ROM protocols after shoulder surgery exist specifically to prevent this complication.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Post-op shoulder immobilization → frozen shoulder is a preventable surgical complication. Early ROM exercises after rotator cuff repair are the prevention strategy. Treatment is GH injection (intra-articular, NOT subacromial) + PT → manipulation under anesthesia if refractory. COMLEX: Osteopathic perioperative rehabilitation incorporates early OMM-guided joint mobilization to prevent post-surgical adhesive capsulitis, a COMLEX-relevant complication prevention concept.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 1 — Humerus fractures: epidemiology, mechanism, presentation, imaging, and management"},{"id":"sdl13_q24","sdlNumber":13,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of frozen shoulder (adhesive capsulitis).","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman has 11 months of left shoulder stiffness. Her primary care physician treated her with NSAIDs and a series of subacromial corticosteroid injections without improvement. Current exam shows globally restricted shoulder motion: forward flexion 80 degrees, external rotation 10 degrees, internal rotation to L4. Strength is intact. Which statement best identifies the error in prior management and the correct next step?","choices":{"A":"The subacromial injection series was underdosed — the correct next step is a higher-concentration subacromial triamcinolone injection (80 mg rather than 40 mg) targeting the same subacromial compartment with prolonged post-injection physical therapy","B":"Referral for total shoulder arthroplasty is indicated since she has failed one course of subacromial corticosteroid therapy, because capsular fibrosis in frozen shoulder is irreversible without surgical joint replacement in patients with 11 months of stiffness","C":"Long-term oral NSAID therapy combined with supervised home stretching is the correct next step, since this approach avoids the systemic effects of glucocorticoids and the procedural risks of manipulation under anesthesia in a middle-aged woman","D":"The subacromial injections were incorrectly targeted for a capsular (intra-articular) condition; the correct treatment is glenohumeral intra-articular corticosteroid injection plus physical therapy, then manipulation under anesthesia if it fails","E":"Initiation of biologic therapy with an interleukin-6 receptor antagonist targeting the fibroinflammatory capsular process, which has Level I evidence supporting its use in refractory idiopathic adhesive capsulitis before surgical intervention is attempted"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The critical teaching point: adhesive capsulitis is a CAPSULAR (intra-articular) condition — the contracted joint capsule is the pathological structure. The correct injection target is the GLENOHUMERAL joint space (intra-articular corticosteroid or oral glucocorticoids). Subacromial injection targets the subacromial bursa and rotator cuff — an entirely different compartment for a different pathology (rotator cuff impingement/tendinosis). The patient has never received the correct treatment. The correct sequence: GH intra-articular injection + PT → manipulation under anesthesia if failure. Total shoulder arthroplasty is for GH OA or cuff tear arthropathy, not adhesive capsulitis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Frozen shoulder injection = INTRA-ARTICULAR (GH joint). Rotator cuff injection = SUBACROMIAL. This is a high-yield board discriminator — wrong injection site is a common clinical error. COMLEX: OMM is an alternative to corticosteroid injection for early adhesive capsulitis — glenohumeral muscle energy and articulation techniques directly address the capsular restriction and are a COMLEX-preferred non-pharmacologic intervention.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 1 — Humerus fractures: epidemiology, mechanism, presentation, imaging, and management"}],"14":[{"id":"sdl14_q01","sdlNumber":14,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lateral epicondylitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which muscle-tendon unit is most commonly injured in lateral epicondylitis?","choices":{"A":"Extensor carpi ulnaris at its origin on the posteromedial epicondyle of the humerus","B":"Flexor carpi radialis at its origin on the medial epicondyle of the humerus","C":"Common flexor tendon at its origin on the medial epicondyle of the humerus","D":"Extensor pollicis longus at its origin on the radial shaft distal to the elbow","E":"Extensor carpi radialis brevis at its origin on the lateral epicondyle of the humerus"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Lateral epicondylitis involves the extensor carpi radialis brevis (ECRB), which originates at the lateral epicondyle and is the most commonly affected tendon due to its eccentric loading during wrist extension and forearm activities. The extensor carpi ulnaris originates on the lateral epicondyle as well but is less commonly the primary lesion. The flexor carpi radialis and common flexor tendon originate at the medial epicondyle, placing them in the territory of medial epicondylitis. The extensor pollicis longus arises from the ulnar shaft in the third dorsal compartment, not the epicondyle.","boardPrep":"Step 1: FA 2025: lateral (tennis) elbow tendinopathy = repetitive wrist extension; the course slides name the ECRB as the most commonly injured tendon; expect a vignette with a tennis player or office worker with lateral elbow pain on wrist extension. COMLEX: Counterforce brace placement and the eccentric loading mechanism map directly to COMLEX CP8 musculoskeletal presentations.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 2 — Little league shoulder and elbow: pediatric overuse of the common extensor origin parallels adult lateral epicondylitis pathomechanics"},{"id":"sdl14_q02","sdlNumber":14,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lateral epicondylitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which term correctly describes the pathological state of lateral epicondylitis that has become a chronic, degenerative condition?","choices":{"A":"Epicondylitis, characterized by acute neutrophilic infiltrate and classical inflammatory mediators within the ECRB tendon","B":"Epicondylosis, with degenerative angiofibroblastic change and disorganized collagen but no inflammatory cells","C":"Epicondyloma, characterized by fibroblastic hyperplasia with lymphocytic infiltration at the tendon-bone junction","D":"Enthesopathy, characterized by calcific deposits forming at the tendon-bone interface from chronic calcium influx","E":"Bursitis, characterized by inflammatory fluid accumulation within the lateral epicondylar bursa sac"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Chronic lateral epicondylitis is more accurately termed epicondylosis (tendinosis), in which the dominant pathology is angiofibroblastic hyperplasia with disorganized collagen and neovascularization rather than classical acute inflammation. Epicondylitis (tendinitis) correctly applies only to the acute phase with neutrophilic inflammatory infiltrate. Epicondyloma and enthesopathy are not standard diagnostic categories for this condition. Bursitis implies a distinct bursal structure, which is not the primary pathology in lateral epicondylitis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The -itis vs -osis distinction is a high-yield conceptual pair for all tendon overuse injuries; tendinosis involves degeneration rather than inflammation, which changes treatment from anti-inflammatory to regenerative approaches. COMLEX: Treatment algorithm differences between acute and chronic presentations (corticosteroids for acute, PRP/OT for chronic) align with COMLEX clinical management questions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 2 — Little league shoulder and elbow: pediatric overuse of the common extensor origin parallels adult lateral epicondylitis pathomechanics"},{"id":"sdl14_q03","sdlNumber":14,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lateral epicondylitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A patient with confirmed acute lateral epicondylitis fails a 3-week course of NSAIDs and rest. Which non-surgical intervention is most appropriate as the next step?","choices":{"A":"Surgical debridement and open release of the ECRB tendon at the lateral epicondyle given NSAID failure","B":"Platelet-rich plasma injection into the ECRB tendon to stimulate regenerative tendon healing","C":"Corticosteroid injection at the lateral epicondyle with occupational therapy and a counterforce brace","D":"Extracorporeal shock wave therapy applied weekly over the common extensor origin","E":"Nitroglycerin patch applied daily over the lateral epicondyle to promote tendon repair"},"correct":"C","explanation":"After NSAID failure in acute lateral epicondylitis, corticosteroid injection combined with occupational therapy and a counterforce brace represents the appropriate next non-surgical step. Surgical debridement is reserved for chronic cases failing prolonged conservative management (course slide p5 lists percutaneous tendon procedures under chronic treatment). Platelet-rich plasma, nitroglycerin patches, and extracorporeal shock wave therapy are interventions for the chronic tendinosis phase, not the acute phase following NSAID failure. The counterforce brace (unloader strap) reduces the force transmitted through the ECRB origin.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lateral epicondylitis treatment follows an escalating stepwise algorithm; boards test recognition of when to escalate from NSAIDs to injection versus when surgery is appropriate. COMLEX: The distinction between acute and chronic treatment is explicitly tested, with PRP representing the regenerative approach to the degenerative chronic phase.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 2 — Little league shoulder and elbow: pediatric overuse of the common extensor origin parallels adult lateral epicondylitis pathomechanics"},{"id":"sdl14_q04","sdlNumber":14,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of medial epicondylitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which tendon is primarily injured in medial epicondylitis, and where does it originate?","choices":{"A":"Extensor carpi radialis brevis originating at the lateral epicondyle of the humerus during backhand motions","B":"Common extensor tendon originating at the lateral epicondyle and stressed by repetitive wrist extension","C":"Biceps brachii tendon at its distal insertion on the radial tuberosity from repetitive forearm supination","D":"Triceps brachii tendon at its olecranon insertion stressed by repetitive elbow extension overhead","E":"Common flexor tendon originating at the medial epicondyle of the humerus, stressing the flexor-pronator mass"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Medial epicondylitis, known as \"golfer's elbow,\" involves the common flexor tendon at its origin on the medial epicondyle of the humerus. The flexor-pronator mass includes the flexor carpi radialis, pronator teres, flexor carpi ulnaris, and palmaris longus. The ECRB at the lateral epicondyle is the target in lateral epicondylitis. The biceps tendon inserts on the radial tuberosity and is unrelated to epicondylitis. The triceps olecranon insertion is associated with olecranon bursitis or triceps tendinopathy, not medial epicondylitis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Medial vs lateral epicondylitis is a classic boards distinction; \"golfer's elbow\" = medial/common flexor, \"tennis elbow\" = lateral/ECRB. FA 2025: tennis elbow = repetitive wrist extension; golfer's elbow = repetitive wrist flexion, so the sport or activity is the contextual clue. COMLEX: The proximity of the ulnar nerve to the medial epicondyle is a testable complication, especially in overhead athletes with medial elbow symptoms.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 5 — Achilles tendinopathy: the acute tendinitis to chronic tendinosis overuse spectrum applies equally to medial epicondyle flexor tendon pathology"},{"id":"sdl14_q05","sdlNumber":14,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of medial epicondylitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which movement pattern most reliably provokes pain in medial epicondylitis on physical examination?","choices":{"A":"Resisted wrist extension with the elbow in extension and the forearm in supination stressing the ECRB tendon","B":"Resisted wrist flexion with the elbow in extension and the forearm in supination stressing the common flexor origin","C":"Passive elbow flexion beyond 90 degrees with the wrist in neutral and forearm supinated maximally","D":"Active shoulder internal rotation against resistance with the elbow extended at the patient's side","E":"Maximum elbow flexion held for 60 seconds to reproduce ulnar nerve paresthesias at the cubital tunnel"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Resisted wrist flexion with the elbow extended and forearm supinated directly loads the common flexor tendon at the medial epicondyle, reproducing pain in medial epicondylitis. Resisted wrist extension loads the ECRB and provokes lateral epicondylitis (Cozen's test). Passive elbow flexion is used in the maximum elbow flexion test for cubital tunnel syndrome. Active shoulder internal rotation against resistance tests the rotator cuff. Maximum elbow flexion for 60 seconds evaluates the ulnar nerve, not the common flexor tendon.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The examination finding of medial elbow pain with resisted wrist flexion versus resisted wrist extension reliably separates medial from lateral epicondylitis on boards. COMLEX: The clinical presentation of medial elbow pain in a throwing or golf athlete is a prototypical COMLEX CP8 musculoskeletal vignette.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 5 — Achilles tendinopathy: the acute tendinitis to chronic tendinosis overuse spectrum applies equally to medial epicondyle flexor tendon pathology"},{"id":"sdl14_q06","sdlNumber":14,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of medial epicondylitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A pitcher with medial epicondylitis reports new ring and small finger tingling during overhead throwing. Which anatomical structure is most likely responsible for this additional symptom?","choices":{"A":"Radial nerve, which passes anterior to the lateral epicondyle and is stretched during elbow valgus stress","B":"Median nerve, which traverses the cubital fossa anterior to the medial epicondyle during overhead motion","C":"Posterior interosseous nerve, a radial nerve branch passing through the radial tunnel at the elbow","D":"Ulnar nerve, which courses posterior to the medial epicondyle and is stretched during overhead throwing","E":"Musculocutaneous nerve, which supplies the lateral forearm and is stretched during overhead abduction"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The ulnar nerve runs in a groove posterior to the medial epicondyle within the cubital tunnel. In overhead athletes with medial epicondylitis, the valgus stress and repetitive elbow flexion can compress or traction the ulnar nerve, producing ring and small finger paresthesias in addition to medial elbow pain. The radial nerve is lateral and not associated with medial elbow pathology. The median nerve does not course posterior to the medial epicondyle. The posterior interosseous and musculocutaneous nerves serve different anatomical regions not involving ring and small finger sensation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ulnar nerve vulnerability at the medial epicondyle is high-yield; a vignette combining medial elbow pain with ring/small finger tingling in a throwing athlete points to concomitant cubital tunnel involvement. COMLEX: The combination of medial epicondylitis and ulnar nerve compression at the same anatomical region is a classic COMLEX multiple-pathology clinical scenario.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 5 — Achilles tendinopathy: the acute tendinitis to chronic tendinosis overuse spectrum applies equally to medial epicondyle flexor tendon pathology"},{"id":"sdl14_q07","sdlNumber":14,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ulnar nerve entrapment at the elbow.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which provocative test is most specific for reproducing symptoms in cubital tunnel syndrome during the physical examination?","choices":{"A":"Phalen's test, in which both wrists are held in full flexion at 90 degrees for 60 seconds to compress the carpal tunnel","B":"Maximum elbow flexion test, in which the patient holds the elbow in full flexion with wrist extension for 60 seconds","C":"Reverse Phalen's test, in which both wrists are held in full extension at 90 degrees for 60 seconds","D":"Cozen's test, in which the examiner resists wrist extension while the elbow is held in full extension","E":"Finkelstein maneuver, in which the patient grasps the thumb in the fist and the examiner ulnar deviates"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The maximum elbow flexion test, with the elbow in full flexion and wrist in extension held for 60 seconds, stretches and compresses the ulnar nerve at the cubital tunnel and is the most specific provocative test for cubital tunnel syndrome. Phalen's test evaluates the carpal tunnel for median nerve compression. Reverse Phalen's also assesses the carpal tunnel. Cozen's test loads the ECRB and is used for lateral epicondylitis. The Finkelstein maneuver specifically evaluates the first dorsal compartment tendons in De Quervain's tenosynovitis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cubital tunnel syndrome is tested as the 2nd most common upper extremity nerve entrapment; knowing the maximum elbow flexion test (analogous to Phalen's for CTS) is key for the exam. COMLEX: Tinel's sign at the posteromedial elbow and the maximum flexion test are both expected on COMLEX clinical examination questions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 7 — Meralgia paresthetica: a lower extremity peripheral nerve entrapment sharing conservative management principles with cubital tunnel syndrome"},{"id":"sdl14_q08","sdlNumber":14,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ulnar nerve entrapment at the elbow.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A patient with cubital tunnel syndrome has weakness of grip and an inability to separate the fingers against resistance. Which finding correctly identifies the motor deficit?","choices":{"A":"Weakness of the ulnar-innervated interossei and hypothenar muscles, causing loss of finger abduction and adduction","B":"Weakness of the thenar muscles innervated by the median nerve, producing loss of thumb opposition and palmar abduction","C":"Weakness of the extensor digitorum communis innervated by the radial nerve at the posterior interosseous branch","D":"Weakness of the flexor carpi radialis innervated by the median nerve proximal to the wrist at the forearm","E":"Weakness of the brachioradialis innervated by the radial nerve in the lateral compartment of the arm"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The ulnar nerve innervates the intrinsic hand muscles including the interossei (which abduct and adduct the fingers), the hypothenar muscles (abductor digiti minimi, flexor digiti minimi, opponens digiti minimi), and the medial two lumbricals. Weakness of these structures produces loss of finger abduction and adduction, and contributes to claw hand deformity. The thenar muscles are median nerve territory. Extensor digitorum communis is radial nerve territory. Flexor carpi radialis and brachioradialis are median and radial nerve territories, respectively.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ulnar nerve motor deficit (interossei weakness, claw hand) versus median nerve deficit (thenar wasting, loss of thumb opposition) is a classic boards distinction. COMLEX: The claw hand deformity specifically affects the ring and small fingers due to loss of ulnar intrinsic muscles and is a named COMLEX musculoskeletal physical finding.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 7 — Meralgia paresthetica: a lower extremity peripheral nerve entrapment sharing conservative management principles with cubital tunnel syndrome"},{"id":"sdl14_q09","sdlNumber":14,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ulnar nerve entrapment at the elbow.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which initial conservative management is most appropriate for a patient with mild cubital tunnel syndrome and no intrinsic (interosseous or hypothenar) wasting or weakness?","choices":{"A":"Immediate surgical ulnar nerve transposition to move the nerve anterior regardless of severity or conservative trial","B":"Immobilization in a full arm cast at 90 degrees of elbow flexion for 6 weeks to reduce nerve traction","C":"Activity modification to avoid prolonged elbow flexion plus a padded elbow brace to limit cubital tunnel compression","D":"Immediate NCS/EMG testing followed by percutaneous cubital tunnel release regardless of symptom severity","E":"Corticosteroid injection directly into the cubital tunnel adjacent to the ulnar nerve at the medial elbow"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Mild cubital tunnel syndrome without motor deficits is initially managed conservatively with activity modification to avoid prolonged elbow flexion, padding of the elbow, and bracing that limits full flexion. Surgical ulnar nerve transposition is reserved for cases failing conservative management or with significant motor deficits. A full arm cast in flexion would worsen cubital tunnel compression, not relieve it. Immediate surgery before a conservative trial is not indicated for mild cases. Corticosteroid injection directly into the cubital tunnel risks nerve injury and is not first-line.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The boards test the step-wise approach to cubital tunnel syndrome: conservative first (activity mod, brace), then electrodiagnostics if uncertain, then surgery for failures or motor deficits. COMLEX: Padding and ergonomic modification align with COMLEX's emphasis on functional and non-pharmacological interventions before procedural management.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 7 — Meralgia paresthetica: a lower extremity peripheral nerve entrapment sharing conservative management principles with cubital tunnel syndrome"},{"id":"sdl14_q10","sdlNumber":14,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of carpal tunnel syndrome.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"In carpal tunnel syndrome, which nerve is compressed, and through which anatomical structure does the compression occur?","choices":{"A":"Ulnar nerve, compressed between the pisiform and hook of hamate within Guyon's canal at the volar wrist","B":"Radial nerve, compressed beneath the first dorsal compartment retinaculum at the radial styloid","C":"Median nerve, compressed between the two heads of pronator teres muscle in the proximal forearm","D":"Ulnar nerve, compressed under the flexor carpi ulnaris at the distal two-thirds of the forearm","E":"Median nerve, compressed under the transverse carpal ligament at the roof of the carpal canal of the wrist"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Carpal tunnel syndrome involves compression of the median nerve under the transverse carpal ligament, which forms the roof of the carpal canal at the wrist. Any condition that increases pressure within the canal can compress the median nerve. The ulnar nerve is compressed in Guyon's canal between the pisiform and hamate. Radial nerve compression under the first dorsal compartment retinaculum is not a recognized entrapment syndrome. Median nerve compression between pronator teres heads is pronator teres syndrome, a separate condition. Flexor carpi ulnaris compression of the ulnar nerve is a distinct entity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CTS is the highest-yield nerve entrapment syndrome on Step 1; FA 2025 emphasizes thenar eminence wasting (APB weakness) from chronic median nerve compression as the classic motor finding. COMLEX: CTS is a named COMLEX CP8 condition; wrist OMM and somatic dysfunction at C5-T1 contributing to median nerve tension are COMLEX-specific clinical angles.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 5 — Hand and wrist fractures: distal radius fractures acutely raise carpal canal pressure, precipitating acute carpal tunnel syndrome"},{"id":"sdl14_q11","sdlNumber":14,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of carpal tunnel syndrome.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A patient with suspected carpal tunnel syndrome undergoes provocative testing. Which combination of physical examination tests is most appropriate to confirm the diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Maximum elbow flexion test combined with Tinel's sign at the posteromedial elbow cubital tunnel","B":"Finkelstein maneuver combined with tenderness palpation over the first dorsal compartment radial styloid","C":"Cozen's test combined with passive wrist extension and resisted forearm supination at the elbow","D":"Phalen's sign combined with Tinel's sign at the volar wrist, both targeting the median nerve distribution","E":"Drop arm test combined with Neer's and Hawkins-Kennedy impingement signs at the shoulder"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Phalen's sign (wrist flexion at 90 degrees for 60 seconds reproducing median distribution paresthesias) and Tinel's sign at the volar wrist (percussion over the carpal tunnel) are the two classic provocative tests for CTS. The maximum elbow flexion test and cubital tunnel Tinel's are for cubital tunnel syndrome. The Finkelstein maneuver with first dorsal compartment tenderness evaluates De Quervain's tenosynovitis. Cozen's test and wrist extension/forearm supination combination targets lateral epicondylitis. The drop arm test and shoulder impingement signs evaluate rotator cuff and subacromial pathology.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Phalen's test is more sensitive and Tinel's more specific for CTS; boards may ask which is more sensitive or appropriate as a first test. COMLEX: Both tests are expected clinical knowledge for COMLEX, with the additional consideration of NCS/EMG for confirmation when clinical findings are equivocal.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 5 — Hand and wrist fractures: distal radius fractures acutely raise carpal canal pressure, precipitating acute carpal tunnel syndrome"},{"id":"sdl14_q12","sdlNumber":14,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of carpal tunnel syndrome.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which demographic pattern is most consistent with the epidemiology of carpal tunnel syndrome in the general population?","choices":{"A":"Female-predominant, with an incidence approximately three times higher in women than in men across all age groups","B":"Male-predominant, with a 2:1 male-to-female ratio most pronounced in adults over 60 who perform heavy manual labor","C":"Equal sex distribution, with rates driven entirely by occupation regardless of biological sex","D":"Pediatric-predominant, with most cases arising in adolescents during periods of rapid skeletal growth","E":"Elderly-predominant, with first presentation occurring typically after age 70 in both males and females"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Carpal tunnel syndrome is 3 times more common in females than males, with female incidence reported at 2.2-5.4 per 1,000 persons per year versus 1.1-3 per 1,000 for males. CTS affects 1-5% of the general population and is not pediatric-predominant or elderly-specific in onset. While occupational exposure increases risk, sex remains a significant independent risk factor likely related to hormonal influences and anatomical differences in carpal canal dimensions. The condition is not equally distributed across sexes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CTS demographics are testable; women 3:1 over men, peak incidence in working-age adults. FA 2025 lists pregnancy as a major risk factor due to edema, which connects CTS to the obstetrics section. COMLEX: Postpartum and pregnancy-related CTS are classically tested COMLEX clinical presentations connecting OB and musculoskeletal pathology.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 5 — Hand and wrist fractures: distal radius fractures acutely raise carpal canal pressure, precipitating acute carpal tunnel syndrome"},{"id":"sdl14_q13","sdlNumber":14,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ulnar nerve entrapment at the wrist.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which anatomical boundaries correctly define Guyon's canal, the site of ulnar nerve compression at the wrist?","choices":{"A":"Bounded by the flexor carpi radialis medially and the median nerve laterally at the proximal carpal row","B":"Bounded by the extensor carpi ulnaris dorsally and flexor carpi ulnaris volarly at the ulnar wrist","C":"Bounded by the pisiform medially, the hook of hamate laterally, and the palmar carpal ligament above","D":"Bounded by the transverse carpal ligament roofing the carpal canal and the carpal bones at the floor","E":"Bounded by the first dorsal compartment laterally and the radial styloid medially at the radial wrist"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Guyon's canal is a fibro-osseous tunnel at the ulnar wrist bounded by the pisiform bone on the medial side, the hook of the hamate on the lateral side, and the palmar carpal ligament forming the roof. The ulnar nerve and artery pass through this tunnel into the hand. This is distinct from the carpal tunnel (transverse carpal ligament roof, carpal bones floor), the radial styloid region (De Quervain's), and the extensor compartments on the dorsum of the wrist.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Guyon's canal anatomy is tested in comparison to the carpal tunnel; knowing the pisiform (medial) and hook of hamate (lateral) as the side walls is key. COMLEX: Cyclists with hypothenar wasting from handlebar pressure on Guyon's canal is a classic COMLEX clinical vignette linking biomechanics to peripheral nerve entrapment.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 8 — Lower extremity peripheral nerve injuries: peripheral nerve entrapment syndromes share anatomical compression mechanisms and electrodiagnostic evaluation"},{"id":"sdl14_q14","sdlNumber":14,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ulnar nerve entrapment at the wrist.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which occupational or recreational activity is classically associated with ulnar nerve entrapment at Guyon's canal?","choices":{"A":"Overhead throwing motions that repetitively stress the medial elbow flexor-pronator mass with valgus force","B":"Repetitive wrist extension activities such as prolonged keyboard typing producing carpal tunnel compression","C":"Postpartum infant care with repeated pinch-grip lifting creating first dorsal compartment friction stress","D":"Prolonged handlebar pressure during cycling that compresses the ulnar nerve at the hypothenar eminence","E":"Backhand tennis strokes that repetitively load the ECRB at the lateral epicondyle with wrist extension"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Cyclists applying prolonged pressure on handlebars directly compress the hypothenar eminence over Guyon's canal, making this the classic occupational/recreational association. The resulting ulnar neuropathy is sometimes called \"cyclist's palsy\" or \"handlebar palsy.\" Overhead throwing stresses the medial elbow and ulnar nerve at the cubital tunnel, not Guyon's canal. Keyboard typing is associated with carpal tunnel syndrome. Postpartum lifting is associated with De Quervain's tenosynovitis. Tennis backhand strokes cause lateral epicondylitis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The cycling-Guyon's canal association is a classic boards vignette; the key distinguishing feature from cubital tunnel is preserved dorsal ulnar sensation since the dorsal ulnar cutaneous branch exits proximal to Guyon's canal. COMLEX: Ergonomic counseling and padded gloves as management represent COMLEX's non-pharmacological first-line approach.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 8 — Lower extremity peripheral nerve injuries: peripheral nerve entrapment syndromes share anatomical compression mechanisms and electrodiagnostic evaluation"},{"id":"sdl14_q15","sdlNumber":14,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ulnar nerve entrapment at the wrist.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A patient with Guyon's canal syndrome has normal sensation on the dorsal ulnar hand. How does this finding help distinguish Guyon's canal from cubital tunnel syndrome?","choices":{"A":"The dorsal ulnar cutaneous branch joins the median nerve proximally, so it is never affected by ulnar compression","B":"The dorsal ulnar cutaneous branch leaves the ulnar nerve proximal to Guyon's canal, so wrist compression spares it","C":"Guyon's canal compression always affects dorsal sensation because the branch enters the canal distal to the pisiform","D":"Cubital tunnel syndrome selectively spares the dorsal ulnar hand because it only involves the terminal palmar branch","E":"Loss of dorsal ulnar sensation is pathognomonic of Guyon's canal and cannot occur with cubital tunnel compression"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The dorsal ulnar cutaneous branch of the ulnar nerve arises from the ulnar nerve in the distal half of the forearm (Moore Ch 3), well before Guyon's canal. This means that compression within Guyon's canal at the wrist will spare this branch, leaving dorsal ulnar hand sensation intact. In contrast, cubital tunnel syndrome at the elbow occurs proximal to this branch point, so dorsal ulnar hand sensation IS affected. The preserved dorsal ulnar sensation in a patient with hypothenar weakness and ring/small finger palmar paresthesias helps localize the lesion to the wrist rather than the elbow.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The preserved dorsal ulnar cutaneous branch in Guyon's canal compression is the classic localizing feature that distinguishes it from cubital tunnel syndrome. COMLEX: Localization of nerve lesions by sensory territory is a core COMLEX neurological examination skill applied to upper extremity entrapment syndromes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 8 — Lower extremity peripheral nerve injuries: peripheral nerve entrapment syndromes share anatomical compression mechanisms and electrodiagnostic evaluation"},{"id":"sdl14_q16","sdlNumber":14,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of De Quervain's tenosynovitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which tendons are specifically affected in De Quervain's tenosynovitis, and in which anatomical compartment do they reside?","choices":{"A":"Flexor carpi radialis and flexor pollicis longus within the carpal canal at the volar wrist crease","B":"Extensor pollicis longus and extensor indicis in the third dorsal compartment at Lister's tubercle","C":"Abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis in the first dorsal compartment at the radial styloid","D":"Extensor carpi radialis longus and extensor carpi radialis brevis in the second dorsal compartment of the wrist","E":"Flexor pollicis longus and abductor pollicis brevis within the carpal tunnel at the thenar eminence"},"correct":"C","explanation":"De Quervain's tenosynovitis affects the abductor pollicis longus (APL) and extensor pollicis brevis (EPB) tendons, which share the first dorsal compartment of the wrist at the level of the radial styloid. Repetitive or overuse activities cause friction-based inflammation within this fibro-osseous tunnel. The flexor carpi radialis and flexor pollicis longus are volar structures in the carpal canal. The third dorsal compartment at Lister's tubercle contains the extensor pollicis longus. The second dorsal compartment contains the ECR longus and brevis. Abductor pollicis brevis is a thenar intrinsic muscle, not a dorsal compartment structure.","boardPrep":"Step 1: De Quervain's is a classic FA 2025 vignette: APL and EPB in the first dorsal compartment, Finkelstein test, and postpartum women. COMLEX: The first dorsal compartment as a fibro-osseous tunnel fits COMLEX's anatomical entrapment framework; somatic dysfunction at the wrist affecting the radial styloid region is a COMLEX-specific management angle.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 4 — Posterior tibial tendon dysfunction: both conditions involve a tendon entrapped within a fibro-osseous canal producing stenosing tenosynovitis"},{"id":"sdl14_q17","sdlNumber":14,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of De Quervain's tenosynovitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which physical examination maneuver is diagnostic for De Quervain's tenosynovitis?","choices":{"A":"Phalen's test: the patient holds both wrists in full flexion at 90 degrees sustained for 60 seconds","B":"Finkelstein maneuver: the patient makes a fist over the thumb and the examiner ulnar-deviates the wrist","C":"Maximum elbow flexion test: the patient holds the elbow in full flexion for 60 seconds with wrist extended","D":"Cozen's test: the examiner resists wrist extension while the patient holds the elbow fully extended","E":"Tinel's sign: the examiner percusses over the volar wrist crease to reproduce thumb paresthesias"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The Finkelstein maneuver is performed by having the patient place the thumb across the palm, flex the fingers over the thumb, and then the examiner passively ulnar-deviates the wrist. This stretches the APL and EPB tendons over the radial styloid and reproduces the patient's pain, confirming De Quervain's tenosynovitis. Phalen's test evaluates carpal tunnel syndrome. The maximum elbow flexion test evaluates cubital tunnel syndrome. Cozen's test evaluates lateral epicondylitis. Tinel's sign at the wrist evaluates for carpal tunnel syndrome, not De Quervain's.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The Finkelstein maneuver is high-yield for De Quervain's; the course mechanics are thumb in palm, then ulnar deviation reproduces radial wrist pain (FA 2025: pain at the radial styloid with stretch of the thumb tendons). COMLEX: Boards expect you to distinguish Finkelstein (De Quervain's) from Phalen's (CTS) from the maximum flexion test (cubital tunnel) as three different conditions with three different diagnostic maneuvers.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 4 — Posterior tibial tendon dysfunction: both conditions involve a tendon entrapped within a fibro-osseous canal producing stenosing tenosynovitis"},{"id":"sdl14_q18","sdlNumber":14,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of De Quervain's tenosynovitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which demographic group has the highest epidemiological risk for developing De Quervain's tenosynovitis?","choices":{"A":"Young male contact sport athletes aged 18 to 30 with high-velocity wrist flexion demands from sport","B":"Elderly men over age 65 performing heavy manual labor with repetitive gripping and lifting activities","C":"Adolescent females during growth spurts who have increased susceptibility to wrist tendon overuse","D":"Postmenopausal women over age 65 who develop concurrent wrist and hand osteoarthritis with synovitis","E":"Women aged 30 to 50, with a notably elevated risk specifically during the 4 to 6 weeks following childbirth"},"correct":"E","explanation":"De Quervain's tenosynovitis predominantly affects women aged 30-50, with a particularly high incidence in the 4-6 weeks postpartum period. The combination of hormonal changes, repetitive infant-lifting mechanics (pinch grip with wrist ulnar deviation), and breastfeeding-related wrist positions creates significant stress on the first dorsal compartment tendons. Young male athletes, elderly men, adolescent females, and postmenopausal women with OA are not the primary demographic for this condition, though any repetitive radial wrist deviation activity can trigger it.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The postpartum woman with radial wrist pain and positive Finkelstein is a classic Step 1 clinical vignette connecting obstetrics to musculoskeletal pathology. COMLEX: COMLEX tests the management (thumb spica bracing, corticosteroid injection) and the postpartum timing as a clinical trigger for the condition.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 4 — Posterior tibial tendon dysfunction: both conditions involve a tendon entrapped within a fibro-osseous canal producing stenosing tenosynovitis"},{"id":"sdl14_q19","sdlNumber":14,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lateral epicondylitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 42-year-old woman and recreational tennis player has an 8-week history of right lateral elbow pain worsened by gripping and backhand strokes. No prior trauma or neurological symptoms. Exam: point tenderness at the lateral epicondyle, pain with resisted wrist extension. X-ray is normal. Which of the following most accurately describes the underlying pathological process?","choices":{"A":"Acute neutrophilic inflammatory infiltrate invading the ECRB tendon and surrounding periosteal tissue","B":"Angiofibroblastic hyperplasia with degeneration and disorganized collagen within the ECRB tendon origin","C":"Calcium hydroxyapatite crystal deposition within the common extensor tendon producing calcific tendinitis","D":"Fibrocartilaginous metaplasia of the common extensor tendon from repetitive valgus stress at the elbow","E":"Synovial hypertrophy with pannus formation eroding the articular cartilage of the radiocapitellar joint"},"correct":"B","explanation":"With persistent symptoms, this presentation reflects the transition from the acute tendinitis phase into chronic tendinosis, characterized by angiofibroblastic hyperplasia, disorganized collagen, and neovascularization rather than classic acute neutrophilic inflammation. This histopathological distinction has treatment implications: anti-inflammatory approaches are less effective in the chronic phase compared with regenerative or eccentric loading approaches. Calcific tendinitis involves hydroxyapatite deposits and presents distinctly on imaging. Fibrocartilaginous metaplasia and synovial pannus are not characteristic of lateral epicondylitis histopathology.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The chronicity of lateral epicondylitis shifts histopathology from neutrophilic inflammation to angiofibroblastic degeneration; this distinction drives the treatment shift from NSAIDs to OT and regenerative injections. COMLEX: The chronic versus acute management algorithm is explicitly tested on COMLEX clinical management questions.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — Little league shoulder and elbow: pediatric overuse rotator cuff and medial elbow apophyseal injury"},{"id":"sdl14_q20","sdlNumber":14,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lateral epicondylitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 48-year-old male electrician has 4 months of left lateral elbow pain. He completed 6 weeks of NSAIDs and activity modification without adequate improvement. No weakness or neurological deficits. Exam confirms lateral epicondylitis. Which intervention is most appropriate as the next step in management?","choices":{"A":"Occupational therapy with eccentric strengthening and a counterforce forearm brace for chronic tendinosis","B":"Immediate surgical ECRB tendon debridement given failure of initial conservative pharmaceutical management","C":"Full elbow immobilization in a long arm cast for 6 weeks to allow ECRB tendon collagen remodeling","D":"Monthly corticosteroid injection repeated for 3 consecutive months regardless of initial injection response","E":"Extracorporeal shock wave therapy combined with ultrasound-guided open surgical release of the tendon"},"correct":"A","explanation":"After NSAIDs and activity modification fail, occupational therapy with eccentric strengthening and a counterforce brace is the appropriate next step for chronic lateral epicondylitis. Eccentric loading programs are strongly supported for chronic tendinosis by promoting controlled collagen remodeling. Surgical debridement is not indicated this early; it is reserved for prolonged failure of conservative care. Full elbow casting is counterproductive and may promote stiffness. Repeated corticosteroid injections are not recommended as they may further weaken the tendon. ESWT is not combined with surgery.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Boards test the stepwise treatment ladder for lateral epicondylitis; OT with eccentric loading is the key second-line intervention after NSAIDs in the chronic phase. COMLEX: The counterforce brace and occupational therapy are considered conservative non-pharmacological interventions preferred by COMLEX before escalating to injection or surgery.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — Little league shoulder and elbow: pediatric overuse rotator cuff and medial elbow apophyseal injury"},{"id":"sdl14_q21","sdlNumber":14,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of lateral epicondylitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 35-year-old female chef has bilateral lateral elbow pain for 3 months, worse on the dominant right side. She reports pain with repetitive gripping and forearm activities during work. Exam shows point tenderness at the lateral epicondyle bilaterally, with a positive Cozen's test. X-ray is normal. Which of the following best explains why her occupation exposes her to this injury?","choices":{"A":"Repetitive wrist flexion generates excessive valgus stress at the medial epicondyle during food preparation","B":"Overhead lifting generates axial loading that compresses the radiocapitellar articular cartilage directly","C":"Repetitive eccentric loading of the ECRB at the lateral epicondyle during gripping causes tendon microtrauma","D":"Sustained elbow flexion during preparation work compresses the ulnar nerve at the posteromedial elbow","E":"Repetitive forearm supination creates traction stress at the biceps tendon insertion on the radial tuberosity"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Repetitive eccentric loading during gripping and forearm activities generates tensile stress at the ECRB origin on the lateral epicondyle, causing cumulative microtrauma that over time produces tendinosis. Chefs perform constant gripping, lifting, and cutting motions that repeatedly load this tendon eccentrically. Wrist flexion and medial epicondyle stress describes medial epicondylitis. Radiocapitellar compression suggests intra-articular pathology. Sustained elbow flexion and ulnar nerve compression describes cubital tunnel syndrome. Forearm supination and biceps traction describe a separate entity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Understanding the biomechanical mechanism of eccentric loading at the ECRB origin is fundamental to explaining lateral epicondylitis in any occupation, not just tennis. COMLEX: Ergonomic counseling about grip technique and tool modification is a COMLEX-favored management component for occupational lateral epicondylitis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — Little league shoulder and elbow: pediatric overuse rotator cuff and medial elbow apophyseal injury"},{"id":"sdl14_q22","sdlNumber":14,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of medial epicondylitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 28-year-old amateur golfer presents with a 6-week history of right medial elbow pain that worsens progressively during his round. No numbness or tingling. Exam shows tenderness at the medial epicondyle and pain with resisted wrist flexion and forearm pronation. Grip strength is mildly reduced. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Medial epicondylitis from overuse degeneration of the common flexor tendon at the medial epicondyle","B":"Lateral epicondylitis caused by eccentric overuse of the ECRB tendon at the lateral epicondyle","C":"Cubital tunnel syndrome caused by ulnar nerve compression at the posteromedial elbow during swinging","D":"UCL sprain caused by repetitive valgus stress on the ulnar collateral ligament of the medial elbow","E":"Pronator teres syndrome caused by median nerve compression between the two heads of pronator teres"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The combination of medial epicondyle tenderness, pain with resisted wrist flexion and forearm pronation, and a golf history establishes medial epicondylitis. The flexor-pronator mass generates high valgus stress during the golf swing, causing eccentric overload at the medial epicondyle origin. Lateral epicondylitis presents with lateral tenderness and pain on wrist extension, not flexion. Cubital tunnel syndrome would include ring/small finger paresthesias. UCL injury presents with valgus instability rather than resisted wrist flexion pain. Pronator teres syndrome includes median nerve distribution paresthesias in the forearm.","boardPrep":"Step 1: \"Golfer's elbow\" = medial epicondylitis = common flexor tendon at medial epicondyle; pain with resisted wrist flexion is the key differentiating exam finding from lateral epicondylitis. COMLEX: The medial elbow anatomy including ulnar nerve proximity is a testable COMLEX clinical pearl within the medial epicondylitis vignette.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 5 — Achilles tendinopathy: epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management"},{"id":"sdl14_q23","sdlNumber":14,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of medial epicondylitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 51-year-old office worker has had medial elbow pain for 10 months. An acute-phase course of NSAIDs, an unloader strap, and occupational therapy helped only briefly. Pain is reproduced with resisted wrist flexion and pronation, and there are no ulnar nerve symptoms. Ultrasound shows degeneration of the common flexor tendon without a tear. Which option does the course list for this chronic stage?","choices":{"A":"A series of three corticosteroid injections a month apart, since injections are the chronic-stage option for tendinosis","B":"Anterior transposition of the ulnar nerve, because chronic medial elbow pain reflects traction on the nerve behind the epicondyle","C":"Regenerative injection or a percutaneous tendon procedure, which the course lists with nitroglycerin patches for chronic disease","D":"Rigid long arm cast immobilization for 6 weeks, because complete rest lets the degenerated tendon remodel back to normal","E":"Open release of the transverse carpal ligament, because flexor tendon overload at the elbow begins with wrist compression"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course divides epicondylitis treatment by stage. Acute (itis) care uses NSAIDs, corticosteroid injection, an unloader strap, and occupational therapy. Chronic (osis) disease is degenerative, and the listed options are occupational therapy, nitroglycerin patches, regenerative injections, and percutaneous tendon procedures. The proposed pathology is eccentric overload without matching muscular endurance. Corticosteroid injection is an acute-phase option, and repeated injections do not treat degeneration. Ulnar nerve transposition is a cubital tunnel procedure, and this patient has no nerve symptoms. Rigid casting is not a listed treatment and causes stiffness. Carpal tunnel release treats median nerve compression at the wrist, not the common flexor origin.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Medial epicondylitis (golfer's elbow) = common flexor-pronator origin; lateral (tennis elbow) = ECRB. Acute = inflammation (itis); chronic = degeneration (osis), no inflammatory cells. COMLEX: Check the ulnar nerve behind the medial epicondyle in every medial elbow pain patient.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — eccentric exercise for tendinopathy rehabilitation"},{"id":"sdl14_q24","sdlNumber":14,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of medial epicondylitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 52-year-old male pitcher with chronic medial elbow pain has pain with wrist flexion and forearm pronation but no pain with wrist extension. X-ray shows no fracture or valgus instability. Which of the following most reliably distinguishes medial from lateral epicondylitis on physical examination?","choices":{"A":"Pain with passive elbow flexion above 90 degrees reliably distinguishes medial epicondylitis from lateral epicondylitis","B":"Tenderness directly over the olecranon process rather than the epicondyle is pathognomonic for medial pathology","C":"The presence of neurological symptoms is required to distinguish medial from lateral epicondylitis clinically","D":"Pain with resisted wrist flexion and pronation marks medial epicondylitis; resisted extension pain marks lateral","E":"Bilateral symptom involvement is characteristic of medial epicondylitis and absent in lateral epicondylitis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The key examination distinction is the movement that reproduces pain: resisted wrist flexion and forearm pronation provoke medial epicondylitis by loading the common flexor tendon at the medial epicondyle, while resisted wrist extension (Cozen's test) provokes lateral epicondylitis by loading the ECRB at the lateral epicondyle. Passive elbow flexion evaluates cubital tunnel syndrome. Olecranon tenderness suggests olecranon bursitis or triceps tendinopathy. Neurological symptoms may coexist but are not required for the distinction. Both conditions can be bilateral.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The resisted wrist flexion vs extension distinction is the single most important physical exam finding to separate medial from lateral epicondylitis on boards. COMLEX: Boards expect efficient clinical differentiation using one or two targeted examination maneuvers.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 5 — Achilles tendinopathy: epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management"},{"id":"sdl14_q25","sdlNumber":14,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ulnar nerve entrapment at the elbow.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 58-year-old long-haul truck driver presents with 4 months of right ring and small finger numbness and tingling that is worst during long drives. He holds the wheel with his right elbow flexed for hours. Exam: positive Tinel's sign at the right posteromedial elbow, symptoms reproduced with maximum elbow flexion test, mildly reduced grip strength. Which of the following most likely explains this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Carpal tunnel syndrome from median nerve compression under the transverse carpal ligament at the wrist","B":"Lateral epicondylitis from ECRB overuse at the lateral epicondyle during steering wheel gripping","C":"De Quervain's tenosynovitis from APL and EPB tendon inflammation at the first dorsal compartment","D":"Guyon's canal syndrome from ulnar nerve compression at the wrist between the pisiform and hamate","E":"Cubital tunnel syndrome from ulnar compression at the elbow due to sustained flexion while driving"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Sustained elbow flexion while driving compresses and stretches the ulnar nerve within the cubital tunnel, producing classic cubital tunnel syndrome. Ring and small finger distribution paresthesias, positive Tinel's at the posteromedial elbow, and reproduction of symptoms with the maximum elbow flexion test all confirm the diagnosis. Carpal tunnel syndrome involves median nerve distribution (thumb, index, middle) and positive Phalen's/Tinel's at the wrist. Lateral epicondylitis and De Quervain's are tendon pathologies without neurological symptoms. Guyon's canal syndrome would be positive at the wrist, not the elbow, and preserves dorsal ulnar sensation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Prolonged elbow flexion as the mechanism for cubital tunnel is a classic board vignette; the driving truck driver or the reader who leans on the elbow are prototypical presentations. COMLEX: The Tinel's sign location (posteromedial elbow vs volar wrist) differentiates cubital tunnel from CTS and is a COMLEX examination must-know.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 7 — Meralgia paresthetica: epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and treatment"},{"id":"sdl14_q26","sdlNumber":14,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ulnar nerve entrapment at the elbow.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 40-year-old male programmer develops cubital tunnel syndrome confirmed by NCS/EMG. After 3 months of activity modification, elbow padding, and NSAIDs, symptoms have not improved and NCS shows progressive axonal changes. Which surgical intervention is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Carpal tunnel release by dividing the transverse carpal ligament at the volar wrist to decompress the median nerve","B":"Lateral epicondyle ECRB debridement with tendon release to address the ECRB at the lateral epicondyle","C":"First dorsal compartment release by opening the fibro-osseous tunnel at the radial styloid of the wrist","D":"Ulnar nerve transposition to move the nerve to an anterior position at the elbow, relieving tension and compression","E":"Total elbow arthroplasty to decompress the cubital tunnel by replacing the elbow joint articular surfaces"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Ulnar nerve transposition surgically moves the ulnar nerve from its posterior position behind the medial epicondyle to an anterior location (subcutaneous, intramuscular, or submuscular), shortening the path the nerve must travel and relieving tension and compression during elbow flexion. This is the appropriate surgical intervention for cubital tunnel syndrome failing conservative management with progressive NCS changes. Carpal tunnel release is for CTS, not cubital tunnel. ECRB debridement is for lateral epicondylitis. First dorsal compartment release is for De Quervain's. Total elbow arthroplasty is for end-stage arthritis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ulnar nerve transposition is the definitive surgical procedure for refractory cubital tunnel syndrome. COMLEX: Boards expect knowledge of when conservative management has failed and surgery is warranted based on NCS evidence of axonal injury.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 7 — Meralgia paresthetica: epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and treatment"},{"id":"sdl14_q27","sdlNumber":14,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ulnar nerve entrapment at the elbow.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 33-year-old woman with cubital tunnel syndrome has a classic claw-hand deformity involving the ring and small fingers at rest, along with inability to spread her fingers apart. Which of the following mechanisms best explains the finger deformity?","choices":{"A":"Loss of ulnar innervation to the interossei and ulnar lumbricals leaves ring and small finger MCP extension unopposed","B":"Loss of median nerve innervation to the flexor digitorum superficialis causes weakness of ring and small finger flexion","C":"Loss of radial nerve innervation to the extensor digitorum communis causes inability to extend the ring and small fingers","D":"Loss of musculocutaneous nerve innervation to the biceps causes loss of forearm flexion and supination strength","E":"Compression of the anterior interosseous nerve causes isolated weakness of the flexor pollicis longus and pronator quadratus"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The claw-hand deformity in ulnar nerve palsy results from loss of the interossei and ulnar lumbricals, which normally flex the MCP joints and extend the IP joints. Without these muscles, the extrinsic extensors (radial nerve) produce unopposed MCP extension, while the extrinsic flexors (FDP) produce IP flexion, creating the claw posture. The ring and small fingers are most affected because the median-innervated lumbricals maintain normal posture in the index and middle fingers. Median nerve loss affects different muscles. Radial nerve loss produces wrist drop, not claw hand. Musculocutaneous and AIN losses produce entirely different deficits.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The intrinsic minus hand, claw hand, and the ulnar nerve innervation pattern (interossei, hypothenar, medial two lumbricals) is very high-yield; FA 2025 has a detailed nerve injury table for upper extremity entrapments. COMLEX: The functional consequences of ulnar nerve injury at the elbow are expected COMLEX clinical knowledge.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 7 — Meralgia paresthetica: epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and treatment"},{"id":"sdl14_q28","sdlNumber":14,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of carpal tunnel syndrome.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman in her third trimester of pregnancy presents with bilateral hand numbness and tingling worse at night, predominantly in the thumb, index, and middle fingers. Phalen's test is positive bilaterally at 30 seconds. Which mechanism best explains why pregnancy predisposes to carpal tunnel syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Hormonal changes cause direct peripheral axonal degeneration of the median nerve throughout the carpal tunnel","B":"Uterine enlargement displaces the brachial plexus superiorly and compresses the median nerve proximally","C":"Elevated estrogen produces direct cytotoxic injury to the transverse carpal ligament and canal walls","D":"Cervical disc herniation at C6 compresses the root whose sensory distribution overlaps the median nerve","E":"Edema from fluid retention raises carpal canal pressure, compressing the median nerve beneath the ligament"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Pregnancy-related fluid retention and edema increase the pressure within the rigid carpal canal, compressing the median nerve against the transverse carpal ligament. This is the primary mechanism for the high prevalence of CTS in the third trimester and early postpartum period. The symptoms are typically bilateral and often resolve after delivery as edema subsides. Hormonal changes do not cause direct axonal degeneration. Uterine size does not affect the brachial plexus. Estrogen is not directly cytotoxic to the transverse carpal ligament. Cervical radiculopathy at C6 is a separate condition with a different clinical pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pregnancy-associated CTS due to edema is a high-yield board pearl connecting obstetrics and musculoskeletal pathology. FA 2025 lists pregnancy (due to edema) among CTS associations. COMLEX: Postpartum management (bracing, reassurance, expected resolution) is testable on COMLEX for pregnancy-related CTS.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 5 — Hand and wrist fractures: epidemiology, mechanism, presentation, imaging, and management"},{"id":"sdl14_q29","sdlNumber":14,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of carpal tunnel syndrome.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 50-year-old female secretary has a 6-month history of right thumb, index, and middle finger paresthesias that wake her at night. She has tried wrist splinting with partial relief. Exam shows right thenar muscle wasting and positive Phalen's and Tinel's signs. Which intervention is most appropriate at this stage?","choices":{"A":"An additional 3-month trial of nighttime splinting with strict activity modification before considering any procedure","B":"Referral for carpal tunnel release to decompress the median nerve before further irreversible motor function is lost","C":"Oral prednisone taper for 2 weeks to reduce intracanal inflammation and relieve nerve compression","D":"Corticosteroid injection into the carpal tunnel as definitive treatment to avoid the need for surgical release","E":"NCS/EMG testing only, with no further treatment until electrodiagnostic severity grading is obtained"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Thenar muscle wasting indicates chronic severe median nerve compression with motor axonal loss. At this stage, further delay risks irreversible motor deficit; surgical carpal tunnel release (division of the transverse carpal ligament) is the most appropriate intervention to decompress the nerve and halt progression. Additional conservative management trials are not appropriate given objective motor deficits. Oral prednisone is not indicated for CTS. Corticosteroid injection may provide temporary relief but is not definitive treatment when thenar atrophy is present. NCS/EMG is useful for confirmation but should not delay surgery when clinical findings already show motor involvement.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Thenar wasting from CTS is the signal that surgery is urgently needed; boards test the threshold between conservative management and surgical referral, with motor deficit being the key trigger. COMLEX: Timely recognition of motor involvement and referral timing is explicitly tested on COMLEX clinical management questions.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 5 — Hand and wrist fractures: epidemiology, mechanism, presentation, imaging, and management"},{"id":"sdl14_q30","sdlNumber":14,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of carpal tunnel syndrome.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 44-year-old male presents 3 days after a displaced distal radius fracture with acute onset of severe median nerve distribution paresthesias and thumb weakness. He had no prior symptoms. X-ray confirms the fracture. Which of the following most likely explains the acute neurological findings?","choices":{"A":"Median nerve laceration from the sharp fracture fragment directly severing the nerve at the fracture site","B":"Brachial plexus traction injury at C6 and C7 nerve roots from the force of impact during the fall","C":"Radial nerve palsy at the anatomical spiral groove of the humerus from the fracture mechanism","D":"Acute carpal tunnel syndrome from fracture edema and hematoma raising pressure within the carpal canal","E":"Anterior interosseous nerve compression causing isolated flexor pollicis longus and pronator quadratus weakness"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Acute carpal tunnel syndrome is a well-recognized complication of distal radius fractures, caused by post-fracture edema, hematoma, and fragment displacement acutely increasing pressure within the rigid carpal canal. This compresses the median nerve and produces rapid-onset sensory and motor symptoms. A direct nerve laceration is possible but less common than compression. Brachial plexus injury would produce a much more widespread deficit. Radial nerve palsy at the spiral groove produces wrist drop, not thumb weakness or palmar paresthesias. Anterior interosseous syndrome causes isolated deep flexor weakness without sensory loss.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Distal radius fractures causing acute CTS is high-yield for boards; increased canal pressure from fracture hematoma can require emergent carpal tunnel release. COMLEX: Recognizing acute compartment syndrome or acute CTS as a fracture complication requiring urgent intervention is a COMLEX emergency management question.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 5 — Hand and wrist fractures: epidemiology, mechanism, presentation, imaging, and management"},{"id":"sdl14_q31","sdlNumber":14,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ulnar nerve entrapment at the wrist.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Two patients have ulnar neuropathy. One has compression at Guyon's canal from a hook of hamate fracture; the other has a lesion at the elbow above the branches to the forearm flexors. On attempted finger extension, the clawing of the ring and small fingers is clearly more pronounced in the patient with the wrist lesion. Which explanation best accounts for this difference?","choices":{"A":"The wrist lesion also compresses the median nerve in the carpal tunnel, adding index and middle finger lumbrical loss to the claw","B":"The elbow lesion spares the interossei, which are supplied by a branch that leaves the ulnar nerve above the elbow","C":"With the wrist lesion, the ulnar half of flexor digitorum profundus still works and exaggerates flexion at the IP joints","D":"The wrist lesion denervates the extensor digitorum, so the fingers cannot extend at the MCP joints and stay flexed","E":"The elbow lesion paralyzes the hypothenar muscles, whose loss pulls the fingers straight and cancels the claw"},"correct":"C","explanation":"First Aid describes the ulnar claw as best seen with distal lesions: loss of the ulnar lumbricals and interossei lets the fingers extend at the MCP joints, and the remaining extrinsic digital flexors exaggerate flexion at the PIP and DIP joints. With a Guyon canal lesion, the ulnar half of FDP is still innervated, so clawing is marked; a proximal lesion also paralyzes the ulnar FDP, so the deficit is less pronounced. First Aid also lists hook of hamate fracture as a cause of Guyon canal syndrome. A Guyon canal lesion does not compress the median nerve. The interossei are supplied by the deep branch of the ulnar nerve in the hand, not by a branch above the elbow. The extensor digitorum is radial (posterior interosseous) nerve territory. Hypothenar paralysis occurs with both lesions and does not cancel clawing.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ulnar claw = distal ulnar lesion (FDP to digits 4–5 intact); proximal lesion → less clawing plus radial deviation of the wrist on flexion (FCU loss). Guyon canal: cyclists' handlebar pressure, hook of hamate fracture. COMLEX: Dorsal ulnar sensation is spared in Guyon canal lesions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 3 — cubital tunnel syndrome at the elbow"},{"id":"sdl14_q32","sdlNumber":14,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ulnar nerve entrapment at the wrist.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 45-year-old female secretary presents with right hypothenar muscle wasting and weakness of finger abduction without any sensory loss in the hand. Tinel's sign at the cubital tunnel is negative. Which statement best explains why this presentation is consistent with a distal Guyon's canal lesion?","choices":{"A":"Guyon's canal compression always produces both motor and sensory deficits in all patients with this condition","B":"A proximal lesion at Guyon's canal selectively damages motor fibers before sensory fibers in all cases","C":"Sensory fibers in the ulnar nerve always recover faster than motor fibers following any peripheral nerve injury","D":"The proximal ulnar nerve within Guyon's canal carries only sensory information and no motor components at all","E":"A distal Guyon's canal lesion can affect only the deep motor branch of the ulnar nerve, sparing sensation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Within Guyon's canal, the ulnar nerve divides into a superficial sensory branch and a deep motor branch. A lesion at the distal aspect of Guyon's canal, or at the level of the hamate hook, can selectively affect the deep motor branch while sparing the superficial sensory branch, producing pure motor deficits without sensory loss. This is one of the localizing features that distinguishes distal from proximal ulnar nerve lesions. Guyon's canal compression does not always produce combined motor and sensory deficits; the anatomy of the bifurcation within the canal determines the clinical pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pure motor ulnar hand weakness with preserved sensation localizes a lesion to the deep branch of the ulnar nerve within Guyon's canal, a testable anatomical distinction. COMLEX: Sensory vs motor branch involvement within Guyon's canal is a clinically relevant distinction for treatment decision-making.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 8 — Lower extremity peripheral nerve injuries: epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management"},{"id":"sdl14_q33","sdlNumber":14,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ulnar nerve entrapment at the wrist.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 55-year-old male with confirmed Guyon's canal syndrome fails 4 months of conservative management. MSK ultrasound reveals a ganglion cyst compressing the ulnar nerve within the canal. Which next step in management is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Surgical exploration and decompression of Guyon's canal with excision of the compressing ganglion cyst","B":"Repeat corticosteroid injection at 4-week intervals for a total of 3 injections before considering any surgery","C":"MRI of the cervical spine to rule out C8 radiculopathy as the source of symptoms before proceeding","D":"Botulinum toxin injection into the hypothenar muscles to reduce intrinsic muscle tension in the canal","E":"Long arm cast immobilization at 90 degrees of elbow flexion for 6 weeks to reduce canal pressure"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A structural lesion (ganglion cyst) confirmed on ultrasound causing nerve compression in Guyon's canal is an indication for surgical decompression and excision. Conservative management is unlikely to be effective when a physical mass is compressing the nerve. Additional corticosteroid injections will not address the structural problem. Cervical MRI is not needed when imaging has already identified the specific compressive lesion. Botulinum toxin and casting are not indicated and would not address the underlying cause.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Structural causes of nerve compression (ganglion cysts, hamate fractures) require surgical management; boards test the recognition of when imaging findings change the management algorithm from conservative to surgical. COMLEX: MSK ultrasound as a diagnostic tool for peripheral nerve compression is a COMLEX-testable point-of-care imaging modality.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 8 — Lower extremity peripheral nerve injuries: epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management"},{"id":"sdl14_q34","sdlNumber":14,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of De Quervain's tenosynovitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 32-year-old woman who is 5 weeks postpartum presents with right wrist pain at the radial aspect while breastfeeding and lifting her infant. Exam shows tenderness over the radial styloid and a positive Finkelstein maneuver. What is the most likely diagnosis and which tendons are involved?","choices":{"A":"Carpal tunnel syndrome involving median nerve compression under the transverse carpal ligament at the volar wrist","B":"De Quervain's tenosynovitis involving the APL and EPB tendons in the first dorsal compartment at the wrist","C":"Lateral epicondylitis involving ECRB tendon overuse at the lateral epicondyle from repetitive infant lifting","D":"Ganglion cyst arising from the scapholunate ligament on the dorsal radial aspect of the wrist","E":"Radial tunnel syndrome from posterior interosseous nerve compression at the level of the radial head"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Postpartum De Quervain's tenosynovitis is a classic presentation: the combination of hormonal changes, repetitive infant-lifting mechanics (pinch grip with wrist deviation), and breastfeeding positions stresses the APL and EPB tendons within the first dorsal compartment at the radial styloid. The positive Finkelstein maneuver confirms the diagnosis. CTS presents with median nerve distribution paresthesias and does not cause radial styloid tenderness. Lateral epicondylitis presents at the elbow, not the wrist. A ganglion cyst would be a palpable mass. Radial tunnel syndrome presents with pain distal to the lateral epicondyle and a different examination pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The postpartum radial wrist pain presentation is a classic boards scenario (FA 2025: ↑ risk in new mothers); the APL and EPB tendon involvement with positive Finkelstein is the diagnostic combination to memorize. COMLEX: Treatment includes thumb spica bracing and corticosteroid injection; postpartum management considerations (breastfeeding, safe medications) add COMLEX clinical depth.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — Posterior tibial tendon dysfunction/tendinitis: epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management"},{"id":"sdl14_q35","sdlNumber":14,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of De Quervain's tenosynovitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 39-year-old female seamstress with De Quervain's tenosynovitis has tried 3 months of thumb spica bracing and NSAIDs without adequate relief. She cannot take time off work. Finkelstein maneuver remains positive. MSK ultrasound confirms first dorsal compartment tenosynovitis with peritendinous fluid. Which intervention is most appropriate at this stage?","choices":{"A":"Percutaneous first dorsal compartment release in the operating room given failure of all conservative options","B":"Extensor pollicis longus debridement via open surgery at Lister's tubercle to address the third compartment","C":"Carpal tunnel corticosteroid injection targeting the transverse carpal ligament at the volar wrist","D":"Corticosteroid injection into the first dorsal compartment tendon sheath, targeting the APL and EPB","E":"Platelet-rich plasma injection into the radial collateral ligament to reduce compartment inflammation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"After failing NSAIDs and bracing, corticosteroid injection into the first dorsal compartment tendon sheath is the appropriate next step for De Quervain's tenosynovitis. Ultrasound-confirmed peritendinous fluid confirms the clinical diagnosis and guides injection targeting. Surgery (first dorsal compartment release) is reserved for cases failing at least one corticosteroid injection after adequate conservative management. EPL debridement at Lister's tubercle addresses the third compartment, not the first. Carpal tunnel injection treats CTS, not De Quervain's. PRP is used for chronic tendinosis, not acute tenosynovitis with fluid.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The De Quervain's treatment ladder is boards-tested: bracing/NSAIDs first, then corticosteroid injection into the first dorsal compartment, then surgical release for refractory cases. COMLEX: Injection site accuracy (first dorsal compartment vs carpal tunnel) is a COMLEX procedural anatomy question.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — Posterior tibial tendon dysfunction/tendinitis: epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management"},{"id":"sdl14_q36","sdlNumber":14,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of De Quervain's tenosynovitis.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 35-year-old avid cross-country skier presents with bilateral radial wrist pain that began during ski season. She repetitively grasps poles with forceful thumb extension and radial deviation motions. Finkelstein maneuvers are positive bilaterally. No systemic symptoms. Which of the following best explains the mechanism of injury?","choices":{"A":"Repetitive thumb abduction and radial deviation causing friction tenosynovitis in the first dorsal compartment","B":"Repetitive ulnar deviation causing triangular fibrocartilage complex impaction at the distal radioulnar joint","C":"Sustained wrist extension from pole gripping increasing carpal canal pressure and compressing the median nerve","D":"Valgus stress on the wrist from pole planting causing ulnar collateral ligament injury at the wrist joint","E":"Sustained elbow extension during the pole push-off phase compressing the ulnar nerve at the cubital tunnel"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cross-country skiing requires repetitive forceful thumb abduction (poling) and wrist radial deviation, which generate friction and tensile stress on the APL and EPB tendons as they pass through the first dorsal compartment fibro-osseous tunnel at the radial styloid. This repetitive mechanical friction produces stenosing tenosynovitis. TFCC impaction involves the ulnar wrist and a different mechanism. Carpal canal pressure from wrist extension causes CTS, not De Quervain's. UCL injury of the wrist is a separate ligamentous condition. Cubital tunnel syndrome involves the elbow, not the wrist, and affects the ulnar nerve rather than the thumb tendons.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Any activity requiring repetitive thumb abduction or wrist radial deviation (skiing, hammering, lifting infants) is a risk factor for De Quervain's; the mechanism of friction within the fibro-osseous tunnel is the key pathophysiological concept. COMLEX: Identifying repetitive motion mechanics and recommending activity modification as the cornerstone of management aligns with COMLEX's functional medicine approach.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — Posterior tibial tendon dysfunction/tendinitis: epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management"}],"15":[{"id":"sdl15_q01","sdlNumber":15,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ganglion cyst.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the most common anatomical location of a ganglion cyst?","choices":{"A":"Palmar surface of the wrist, arising from the flexor carpi radialis tendon sheath","B":"Volar aspect of the distal interphalangeal joint, adjacent to the nail bed","C":"Lateral border of the index finger MCP joint, arising from the A1 pulley region","D":"Dorsal surface of the ankle, arising from the peroneal tendon sheath","E":"Dorsal wrist, arising from the scapholunate ligament or the dorsal joint capsule"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Ganglion cysts are the most common soft tissue tumors of the hand and wrist. Approximately 70% occur on the dorsal wrist, typically arising from the scapholunate ligament or dorsal joint capsule. About 20% are on the palmar wrist, arising from the scaphotrapezoid joint, and the remaining 10% arise from other locations such as the finger sheaths. The cyst contains thick mucinous fluid derived from joint or tendon sheath tissue.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ganglion cysts are the most common wrist mass — dorsal location (70%) from scapholunate ligament is classic. COMLEX: Transillumination distinguishes fluid-filled ganglion from solid tumor; OMT is not first-line.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 7 — Popliteal cyst: another mucinous synovial cyst arising from a joint cavity — compare anatomical origin and treatment approach"},{"id":"sdl15_q02","sdlNumber":15,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ganglion cyst.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A ganglion cyst is brought to transillumination testing during physical examination. Which finding is expected?","choices":{"A":"The cyst does not transmit light, indicating a solid lipomatous composition","B":"The cyst partially transmits light, consistent with a mixed solid-cystic pattern","C":"The cyst transmits light, confirming its fluid-filled mucinous content","D":"The cyst fluoresces under ultraviolet light due to mucinous protein content","E":"Transillumination is unreliable for ganglion cysts and should not be performed"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Transillumination is a key clinical maneuver for ganglion cysts. Because the cyst is filled with thick, gelatinous mucinous fluid, it transmits light when a light source is held against it — confirming the cystic, fluid-filled nature. This distinguishes ganglion cysts from solid tumors such as lipomas or giant cell tumors of the tendon sheath, which do not transilluminate. The test is simple and requires no equipment beyond a small flashlight.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Transillumination positive = fluid-filled cyst; negative = solid tumor. COMLEX: Ganglion cysts are benign; biopsy is unnecessary if transillumination confirms diagnosis. Aspiration can be both diagnostic and therapeutic.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 7 — Popliteal cyst: another mucinous synovial cyst arising from a joint cavity — compare anatomical origin and treatment approach"},{"id":"sdl15_q03","sdlNumber":15,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ganglion cyst.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 32-year-old woman presents with a 2 cm dorsal wrist mass that has been present for 6 months. It transilluminates and causes mild discomfort with wrist extension. Which of the following is an appropriate management option?","choices":{"A":"Aspiration of the cyst contents, which is both diagnostic and may be therapeutic","B":"Immediate surgical excision is required because recurrence after aspiration exceeds 90%","C":"Corticosteroid injection into the cyst is the preferred first-line treatment","D":"MRI of the wrist is mandatory prior to any intervention to rule out malignancy","E":"No treatment is needed and the patient should be told the cyst will never recur"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Ganglion cysts may be managed conservatively, with aspiration, or with surgical excision. Aspiration is both diagnostic and therapeutic — removal of mucinous fluid can decompress the cyst and relieve symptoms. However, recurrence after aspiration is about 59%, compared to 21% after open surgical excision (SDL 25 reading, CDT Orthopedics Ch 11). Observation is appropriate for asymptomatic cysts. Surgery is reserved for persistent, symptomatic, or recurrent cysts. Corticosteroid injection is reserved for recalcitrant cysts, not first-line, and MRI is not mandatory when clinical diagnosis is clear.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ganglion cyst management — observation, aspiration (59% recurrence), or surgical excision (21% recurrence, the lowest). COMLEX: Aspiration is first-line for symptomatic cysts before surgical referral.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 7 — Popliteal cyst: another mucinous synovial cyst arising from a joint cavity — compare anatomical origin and treatment approach"},{"id":"sdl15_q04","sdlNumber":15,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of wrist and hand osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"Which of the following bony prominences is a hallmark of osteoarthritis at the distal interphalangeal joints of the hand?","choices":{"A":"Bouchard nodes, representing marginal osteophytes at the proximal interphalangeal joints","B":"Haygarth nodes, representing periarticular soft tissue swelling at the MCP joints","C":"Garrod pads, representing fibrous tissue deposition over the DIP joint dorsum","D":"Swan neck deformity nodes, representing hyperextension contractures at the PIP joints","E":"Heberden nodes, representing marginal osteophytes at the DIP joint margins"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Heberden nodes are bony osteophytes that develop at the distal interphalangeal joints and are a classic feature of hand osteoarthritis. They represent marginal osteophyte formation secondary to cartilage degeneration and subchondral bone remodeling. Bouchard nodes are the analogous finding at the proximal interphalangeal joints. Together, Heberden and Bouchard nodes distinguish osteoarthritis from rheumatoid arthritis, which characteristically spares the DIP joints and produces soft, boggy MCP and PIP swelling rather than hard bony prominences.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Heberden nodes = DIP osteophytes (OA); Bouchard nodes = PIP osteophytes (OA); rheumatoid arthritis spares DIP and involves MCP/PIP with soft swelling. COMLEX: OA nodes are hard and non-tender in chronic disease; RA nodes are soft, warm, and tender.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis in general: systemic pathophysiology of cartilage degeneration and osteophyte formation applies to hand and wrist OA"},{"id":"sdl15_q05","sdlNumber":15,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of wrist and hand osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 65-year-old postmenopausal woman presents with pain and stiffness at the base of her thumb that worsens with pinching and gripping. Radiographs show joint space narrowing and osteophytes. Which joint is most commonly affected in this presentation?","choices":{"A":"First carpometacarpal joint, where OA is particularly prevalent in postmenopausal women","B":"Metacarpophalangeal joint of the thumb, which bears the greatest compressive force during pinch grip","C":"Scapholunate joint, which undergoes progressive instability from repetitive grip loading","D":"Trapezioscaphoid joint, which is the primary articulation stressed during lateral pinch","E":"Interphalangeal joint of the thumb, which loses cartilage from repeated flexion under load"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Osteoarthritis of the first carpometacarpal joint is the most common form of wrist and hand OA and is especially prevalent in postmenopausal women. The joint is subjected to high compressive and shear forces during pinch and grip activities. Patients present with pain and tenderness at the base of the thumb, a positive grind test, and radiographic evidence of joint space narrowing. The increased prevalence in postmenopausal women is attributed to the loss of estrogen, which affects collagen synthesis and cartilage integrity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: 1st CMC OA = thumb base pain, postmenopausal women, grind test positive. COMLEX: Distinguish from De Quervain tenosynovitis (radial styloid, Finkelstein positive) — both cause radial-sided wrist pain but different structures involved.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis in general: systemic pathophysiology of cartilage degeneration and osteophyte formation applies to hand and wrist OA"},{"id":"sdl15_q06","sdlNumber":15,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of wrist and hand osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"Erosive osteoarthritis of the hand predominantly affects which joints and is distinguished from classic OA by which additional histological feature?","choices":{"A":"Primarily the IP joints, with subchondral bone erosions and inflammatory synovitis superimposed on degenerative changes","B":"Primarily the wrist radiocarpal joints, with calcium pyrophosphate crystal deposition as the hallmark feature","C":"Primarily the MCP joints, with fibrinoid necrosis and pannus formation distinguishing it from typical OA","D":"Primarily the 1st CMC joint, with diffuse cartilage thinning and absence of osteophyte formation","E":"Primarily all hand joints symmetrically, with anti-CCP antibody positivity distinguishing it from classic OA"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Erosive osteoarthritis is a more aggressive inflammatory variant of hand OA that primarily involves the interphalangeal joints — both DIP and PIP. Unlike classic OA, erosive OA features central subchondral bone erosions visible on radiograph, giving a \"seagull wing\" or \"gull wing\" appearance, along with superimposed inflammatory synovitis. Anti-CCP antibodies are negative, distinguishing it from rheumatoid arthritis. MCP joint involvement and pannus formation are features of rheumatoid arthritis, not erosive OA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Erosive OA = IP joints, inflammatory synovitis, subchondral erosions, anti-CCP negative. COMLEX: Radiographic \"gull wing\" erosion pattern at IP joints distinguishes erosive OA from RA (MCP predominant, anti-CCP positive).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis in general: systemic pathophysiology of cartilage degeneration and osteophyte formation applies to hand and wrist OA"},{"id":"sdl15_q07","sdlNumber":15,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of trigger finger.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Trigger finger results from stenosis of which structure, causing the flexor tendon to catch or lock during finger motion?","choices":{"A":"A2 pulley at the proximal phalanx, which is the strongest annular pulley in the finger sheath","B":"A1 pulley at the base of the finger, which becomes thickened and narrows the tendon sheath","C":"C1 cruciate pulley between the A1 and A2 pulleys, which frays under repetitive flexion loads","D":"Retinacular ligament of the DIP joint, which prevents bowstringing of the terminal extensor","E":"Transverse carpal ligament at the wrist, which compresses the median nerve and adjacent tendons"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Trigger finger, or stenosing flexor tenosynovitis, results from thickening and stenosis of the A1 pulley, located at the level of the MCP joint at the base of the finger. The flexor tendon develops a nodule or thickening that catches against the narrowed A1 pulley as the finger flexes and extends, producing the characteristic catching, triggering, or locking. The A2 pulley is the longest and strongest annular pulley but is not the primary site of stenosis in trigger finger. The transverse carpal ligament is involved in carpal tunnel syndrome.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Trigger finger = A1 pulley stenosis at MCP joint base. The nodule is on the flexor tendon, not the pulley. COMLEX: Most common in middle finger, ring finger, and thumb; also associated with diabetes mellitus and rheumatoid arthritis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 2 — Connective tissue composition: tendon sheath architecture and A1 pulley anatomy underpin trigger finger pathogenesis"},{"id":"sdl15_q08","sdlNumber":15,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of trigger finger.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes presents with a ring finger that locks in flexion and must be passively extended. She has point tenderness at the MCP joint base. Which treatment is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Surgical release of the A1 pulley under local anesthesia in the operating room","B":"Oral methotrexate for 3 months to suppress synovitis of the flexor tendon sheath","C":"Long arm cast with the elbow flexed for 6 weeks to unload the forearm flexors","D":"Ultrasound-guided aspiration of the tendon nodule with a large-bore needle","E":"Corticosteroid injection into the flexor tendon sheath at the A1 pulley level"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Corticosteroid injection into the flexor tendon sheath at the A1 pulley is the most appropriate treatment among these choices. A single injection resolves symptoms in approximately 60-90% of cases, with higher success rates in patients without diabetes. Patients with diabetes tend to have lower response rates and shorter durations of benefit. If symptoms recur after two injections, surgical release of the A1 pulley is indicated. The course slide also lists NSAIDs and splinting, and the Tintinalli reading describes rest, anti-inflammatory medication, and buddy tape or a finger splint as conservative care; neither is among these choices. Methotrexate treats inflammatory arthritis, not idiopathic trigger finger, and a long arm cast immobilizes the elbow and wrist rather than the digital flexor sheath.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Trigger finger first-line = corticosteroid injection into tendon sheath at A1 pulley; surgery if refractory. COMLEX: Diabetes is a risk factor for trigger finger and reduces injection success rates — important COMLEX clinical application.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 2 — Connective tissue composition: tendon sheath architecture and A1 pulley anatomy underpin trigger finger pathogenesis"},{"id":"sdl15_q09","sdlNumber":15,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of trigger finger.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Trigger finger affects about 2% of the population and is most common in women in their 40s and 50s. Which group of systemic conditions does the course list as raising the rate of trigger finger?","choices":{"A":"Hypothyroidism, chronic kidney disease, and sarcoidosis","B":"Osteoporosis, vitamin D deficiency, and hyperparathyroidism","C":"Diabetes, rheumatoid arthritis, and deposition diseases","D":"Multiple sclerosis, Parkinson disease, and prior stroke","E":"Hypertension, coronary disease, and hyperlipidemia"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course lists trigger finger as affecting about 2% of the population, most commonly women in their 40s and 50s, sometimes in multiple fingers or both hands, with higher rates in patients with systemic conditions: diabetes, rheumatoid arthritis, and deposition diseases. These conditions thicken or inflame the flexor tendon sheath and the A1 pulley. The other groups are not listed: hypothyroidism, kidney disease, and sarcoidosis; bone-mineral disorders; neurologic diseases; and cardiovascular risk factors are not named as trigger finger risks in the course.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Trigger finger = stenosing flexor tenosynovitis at the A1 pulley over the MCP joint; ring finger and thumb most often involved. COMLEX: Diabetes predicts multiple digits and a poorer response to corticosteroid injection.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 4 — carpal tunnel syndrome, another diabetes-associated hand condition"},{"id":"sdl15_q10","sdlNumber":15,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ulnar collateral ligament injury of the thumb.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A complete ulnar collateral ligament tear of the thumb MCP joint is sometimes complicated by a Stener lesion. What is a Stener lesion?","choices":{"A":"The flexor pollicis longus tendon displaces into the MCP joint space and blocks reduction","B":"The adductor pollicis aponeurosis is interposed between the torn UCL ends, blocking healing","C":"A bony avulsion fragment from the ulnar base of the proximal phalanx rotates and blocks the joint","D":"Synovial tissue from the MCP joint capsule herniates between the torn ligament ends","E":"The extensor pollicis brevis tendon dislocates medially and tethers the torn UCL in malposition"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A Stener lesion occurs when the torn UCL end flips proximally and the adductor pollicis aponeurosis — a fascial layer overlying the MCP joint — becomes interposed between the two torn ends of the UCL. This prevents the torn ligament ends from healing in proper apposition, making conservative management inadequate. A Stener lesion is a primary indication for surgical repair. MRI is the gold standard for identifying a Stener lesion preoperatively.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Stener lesion = adductor pollicis aponeurosis interposed between torn UCL ends; prevents healing; requires surgery. COMLEX: Complete UCL tears need repair within 1 week (Atlas of Emergency Medicine, SDL 14/15 reading); Tintinalli recommends hand surgery referral within 7 days.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 1 — Interphalangeal joint dislocations: hand ligament and joint injury spectrum that includes UCL disruption and Stener lesion"},{"id":"sdl15_q11","sdlNumber":15,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ulnar collateral ligament injury of the thumb.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 28-year-old skier falls and lands with their thumb hyperabducted against a ski pole. They present with thumb MCP joint pain, swelling, and instability with lateral stress testing. What mechanism produces this injury?","choices":{"A":"Forced ulnar deviation of the thumb at the MCP joint tears the radial collateral ligament on the lateral side","B":"Hyperflexion of the thumb IP joint avulses the flexor pollicis longus from its distal phalangeal insertion","C":"Axial loading through the thumb compresses the sesamoid bones into the MCP joint, rupturing the volar plate","D":"Forced extension of the MCP joint beyond neutral tears the dorsal capsule and extensor mechanism","E":"Forced radial abduction of the thumb at the MCP joint overstresses and tears the ulnar collateral ligament"},"correct":"E","explanation":"UCL injury of the thumb — classically called Gamekeeper's thumb when chronic and Skier's thumb when acute — results from forced radial abduction of the thumb at the MCP joint. This valgus stress mechanism overstresses the UCL on the ulnar aspect of the joint. In skiing, this occurs when the ski pole catches the thumb during a fall, leveraging the thumb radially. The UCL resists this radial abduction force; a sudden excessive force tears the ligament, which can be partial or complete. The RCL is on the opposite, radial side, and is injured by forced ulnar deviation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: UCL thumb injury = forced radial abduction (valgus stress at MCP) = Skier's thumb (acute) or Gamekeeper's thumb (chronic). COMLEX: Clinical valgus stress test under local anesthesia assesses stability; more than 30-35 degrees of radial angulation, or 10-15 degrees more than the other thumb, suggests complete tear (Tintinalli, SDL 25 reading); the Atlas (SDL 14/15 reading) gives 30-40 degrees.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 1 — Interphalangeal joint dislocations: hand ligament and joint injury spectrum that includes UCL disruption and Stener lesion"},{"id":"sdl15_q12","sdlNumber":15,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ulnar collateral ligament injury of the thumb.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following is the gold-standard imaging modality for diagnosing a Stener lesion in a patient with suspected complete UCL tear of the thumb?","choices":{"A":"MRI of the thumb, which directly visualizes the displaced UCL and interposed adductor aponeurosis","B":"Stress radiograph of the thumb in abduction, which quantifies degrees of instability at the MCP joint","C":"Ultrasound of the ulnar aspect of the MCP joint performed dynamically during valgus stress loading","D":"CT arthrogram of the thumb, which outlines the joint capsule disruption and intraarticular hematoma","E":"Plain radiograph of the thumb, which identifies the avulsion fracture fragment at the UCL insertion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"MRI is the gold standard for identifying a Stener lesion because it directly visualizes the torn UCL and the interposed adductor pollicis aponeurosis. This distinction is critical because a Stener lesion requires surgical repair, whereas a partial UCL tear or complete tear without Stener lesion may be managed conservatively with thumb spica casting. Stress radiographs quantify instability but cannot differentiate Stener from non-Stener injuries. Ultrasound can be used by experienced operators but is operator-dependent. Plain radiographs identify bony avulsion fractures but cannot assess soft tissue interposition.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MRI = gold standard for Stener lesion detection. COMLEX: Surgical indication: Stener lesion, or complete-tear laxity (more than 30-35 degrees, or 10-15 degrees more than the other thumb, per Tintinalli; 30-40 degrees per the Atlas). Complete tears need repair within 1 week (Atlas).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 1 — Interphalangeal joint dislocations: hand ligament and joint injury spectrum that includes UCL disruption and Stener lesion"},{"id":"sdl15_q13","sdlNumber":15,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of extensor finger injury of the DIP.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which mechanism of injury is most consistent with mallet finger?","choices":{"A":"Sudden forced extension of a passively flexed DIP joint, tearing the flexor digitorum profundus from its insertion","B":"Axial compression of the fingertip against a hard surface, driving the distal phalanx into the middle phalanx","C":"Sudden forced flexion of an actively extended fingertip, avulsing the terminal extensor tendon from the distal phalanx","D":"Hyperextension of the PIP joint beyond 90 degrees, disrupting the central slip of the extensor mechanism","E":"Chronic repetitive microtrauma to the DIP joint from gripping activities, leading to gradual extensor attrition"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Mallet finger, also called baseball finger, occurs when the terminal extensor tendon at the DIP joint is avulsed — either as a soft-tissue tear or as a bony avulsion fracture — by a sudden forced flexion force applied to an actively extended fingertip. The classic mechanism is a ball striking the end of the outstretched finger, abruptly flexing the DIP joint. This ruptures or avulses the terminal extensor tendon from its insertion on the dorsal distal phalanx, causing the DIP joint to drop into a flexion posture. The injury to the FDP causing jersey finger involves the volar tendon and forced extension, not flexion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Mallet finger = forced DIP flexion → terminal extensor rupture → DIP cannot extend. COMLEX: If less than 1/3 of articular surface involved, treat with extension splinting; greater than 1/3 or volar subluxation may require surgery.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 5 — Hand and wrist fractures: bony mallet finger is an avulsion fracture of the dorsal distal phalanx base requiring fracture management principles"},{"id":"sdl15_q14","sdlNumber":15,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of extensor finger injury of the DIP.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A patient presents with a flexion deformity of the DIP joint after being struck on the fingertip by a basketball. The DIP joint cannot be actively extended but passive extension is preserved. Radiograph shows no fracture. What is the appropriate treatment?","choices":{"A":"Surgical repair of the terminal extensor tendon is required for all acute mallet finger injuries within 24 hours","B":"Buddy taping to the adjacent finger and activity restriction for 3 weeks is sufficient for soft-tissue mallet","C":"Splinting the PIP joint in flexion and the DIP joint in extension simultaneously for 4 weeks restores function","D":"Corticosteroid injection into the DIP joint reduces inflammation and allows tendon reattachment within 2 weeks","E":"Continuous splinting of the DIP joint in full extension for 6-8 weeks without interruption, even during bathing"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Pure soft-tissue mallet finger is managed with continuous DIP extension splinting for 6-8 weeks (CDT Orthopedics, SDL 25 reading; the course slide and the Atlas give at least 6 weeks). The splint must be worn continuously — including during bathing and sleep — because any inadvertent DIP flexion during the healing period restarts the 6-8 week clock. The splint maintains the terminal extensor tendon in apposition with its distal phalanx insertion, allowing scar tissue healing. Buddy taping alone is insufficient. Surgical repair is generally reserved for open injuries, bony avulsion fractures involving greater than 1/3 of the articular surface, or cases with volar subluxation of the distal phalanx.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Soft-tissue mallet finger = DIP extension splint continuously for 6-8 weeks; any DIP flexion during splinting restarts the clock. COMLEX: Bony mallet with less than 1/3 articular surface involvement is still treated conservatively with splinting.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 5 — Hand and wrist fractures: bony mallet finger is an avulsion fracture of the dorsal distal phalanx base requiring fracture management principles"},{"id":"sdl15_q15","sdlNumber":15,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of extensor finger injury of the DIP.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A bony mallet finger involves an avulsion fracture of the dorsal distal phalanx. Which radiographic finding in a bony mallet finger is most likely to require surgical intervention rather than splinting?","choices":{"A":"The avulsion fragment is displaced more than 1 mm from the distal phalanx articular surface on lateral view","B":"The avulsion fracture involves the dorsal cortex without extension into the subchondral bone surface","C":"The patient presents more than 48 hours after the injury occurred due to delayed medical attention","D":"The avulsion fragment involves greater than one-third of the articular surface of the DIP joint","E":"The finger involved is the middle or ring finger, which bear greater functional pinch grip demands"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Bony mallet finger is typically managed conservatively with DIP extension splinting when the avulsion fragment involves less than one-third of the articular surface and the distal phalanx remains reduced without volar subluxation. When the bony fragment involves greater than one-third of the articular surface, joint stability may be compromised and the risk of articular incongruity is higher, making surgical fixation with a pin or screw the preferred approach. Volar subluxation of the distal phalanx with a large dorsal fragment is an absolute indication for surgery. The duration of delay and finger involved do not independently determine surgical need.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Surgical indications for bony mallet finger: articular fragment > 1/3 of DIP surface, OR volar subluxation of distal phalanx. COMLEX: Less than 1/3 fragment = extension splint 6-8 weeks regardless of fragment displacement.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 5 — Hand and wrist fractures: bony mallet finger is an avulsion fracture of the dorsal distal phalanx base requiring fracture management principles"},{"id":"sdl15_q16","sdlNumber":15,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of flexor tendon injury of the DIP.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Jersey finger most frequently involves which digit, and what tendon is avulsed in this injury?","choices":{"A":"The index finger, with avulsion of the flexor digitorum superficialis from its insertion on the middle phalanx","B":"The middle finger, with avulsion of the flexor digitorum profundus from the A2 pulley at the proximal phalanx","C":"The ring finger, with avulsion of the flexor digitorum profundus from its insertion on the volar distal phalanx","D":"The small finger, with avulsion of the flexor carpi ulnaris tendon from the pisiform bony attachment","E":"The thumb, with avulsion of the flexor pollicis longus from its insertion on the volar distal phalanx"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Jersey finger is avulsion of the flexor digitorum profundus from its insertion on the volar distal phalanx, most commonly affecting the ring finger in approximately 75% of cases. The ring finger is vulnerable because it has the least independent flexion compared to adjacent fingers and is held in a more extended position during gripping, making it the last to release when the grip is forcefully opened. The injury occurs when a player grabs a jersey and the opponent pulls away, forcibly extending the DIP joint against active FDP contraction. The FDS inserts on the middle phalanx and is not typically avulsed in jersey finger.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Jersey finger = FDP avulsion, ring finger 75%, inability to flex DIP against resistance. COMLEX: Mechanism = forced DIP extension against resisted FDP contraction; contrast with mallet finger (forced DIP flexion, extensor injury).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 2 — Little league shoulder and elbow: tendon and apophyseal avulsion injuries in athletes — compare mechanism to FDP avulsion in jersey finger"},{"id":"sdl15_q17","sdlNumber":15,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of flexor tendon injury of the DIP.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"In the Leddy-Packer classification of jersey finger, a Type I injury carries the most urgent surgical timeline. Why is surgery most urgent for Type I?","choices":{"A":"The FDP tendon remains at the PIP joint level, where it compresses the digital nerve and requires urgent decompression","B":"A large bony avulsion fragment obstructs the A4 pulley, causing vascular compromise of the entire finger","C":"The tendon avulses with the volar plate of the DIP joint, making anatomical reduction technically time-sensitive","D":"The FDP retracts into the palm, losing its vincular blood supply, so repair is needed within days to prevent necrosis","E":"Both vincula are intact in Type I, preserving blood supply and allowing delayed repair up to 3 months later"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In the Leddy-Packer classification, Type I jersey finger involves the FDP tendon retracting all the way into the palm, rupturing both the long and short vincula — the mesotenon attachments that supply blood to the tendon. Without vascular supply, the tendon undergoes rapid fibrosis and becomes unsuitable for repair. Therefore, surgical repair must be performed within days (typically 7-10 days) of injury. Type II tendons retract to the PIP joint level, maintaining the short vinculum and blood supply, allowing reinsertion within several weeks (SDL 25 reading, CDT Orthopedics Ch 11). Type III involves a bony avulsion fragment that catches at the A4 pulley, retaining blood supply and allowing a less urgent surgical timeline.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Leddy-Packer Type I = tendon into palm, both vincula ruptured, surgery within days. Type II = PIP level, short vinculum intact, repair within several weeks. Type III = bony fragment at A4. COMLEX: Delay in Type I = irreparable tendon necrosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 2 — Little league shoulder and elbow: tendon and apophyseal avulsion injuries in athletes — compare mechanism to FDP avulsion in jersey finger"},{"id":"sdl15_q18","sdlNumber":15,"batch":1,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of flexor tendon injury of the DIP.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which physical examination finding best distinguishes jersey finger from other finger injuries?","choices":{"A":"Loss of active DIP flexion with preserved passive DIP flexion and intact PIP flexion against resistance","B":"Loss of active DIP extension with the DIP joint held in a flexion posture that cannot be voluntarily corrected","C":"Loss of both active DIP and PIP flexion with the finger held in extension by the intact extensor mechanism","D":"Triggering and catching of the finger with active flexion due to a nodule catching on the A1 pulley","E":"Point tenderness at the MCP joint with palpable swelling and clicking on passive range of motion testing"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Jersey finger presents with inability to actively flex the DIP joint because the FDP — the only tendon that flexes the DIP — has been avulsed from its insertion. Passive DIP flexion is preserved because the joint itself is intact. PIP flexion is maintained because the FDS, which inserts on the middle phalanx, is uninjured. This pattern — lost active DIP flexion with preserved active PIP flexion — is pathognomonic for FDP avulsion. Mallet finger, in contrast, presents with lost active DIP extension (the finger droops in flexion). Trigger finger involves catching on active flexion at the MCP joint level.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Jersey finger = cannot actively flex DIP; FDS intact so PIP flexion preserved. Mallet finger = cannot actively extend DIP. COMLEX: Distinguish on exam — jersey finger \"flexion loss at DIP,\" mallet finger \"drop at DIP.\"","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 2 — Little league shoulder and elbow: tendon and apophyseal avulsion injuries in athletes — compare mechanism to FDP avulsion in jersey finger"},{"id":"sdl15_q19","sdlNumber":15,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ganglion cyst.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 26-year-old right-hand-dominant office worker presents with a 2-year history of a slowly enlarging lump on the dorsum of her wrist. She reports mild aching that worsens with prolonged typing. On exam, a 1.5 cm firm, smooth, non-tender mass is found over the dorsal radiocarpal joint. It transilluminates; the overlying skin moves freely over it, but the mass is fixed to deeper structures. Radiographs are normal. What is the most likely origin of this mass?","choices":{"A":"Reactive hypertrophy of the dorsal extensor retinaculum from repetitive friction against the underlying carpal bones","B":"Lipomatous degeneration of the dorsal wrist fat pad secondary to chronic compression from keyboard use","C":"Herniation of synovial fluid through a one-way valve defect in the dorsal scapholunate ligament or joint capsule","D":"Proliferation of synovial lining cells within the joint, forming an exophytic synovial cyst into the dorsal soft tissue","E":"Hematoma organization after an unrecognized minor wrist sprain that was not treated acutely"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Ganglion cysts of the dorsal wrist arise through a one-way valve or pedicle defect in the joint capsule or scapholunate ligament that allows synovial fluid to herniate outward. The fluid collects into a fibrous-walled cyst and becomes gelatinous over time. The \"one-way valve\" mechanism means that joint movement can pump more fluid into the cyst but not back into the joint, which accounts for the cyst's fluctuating size with activity. Synovial proliferation produces villous synovitis or rice bodies in inflammatory arthritis, not discrete cysts. Fixation to deep structures with freely mobile overlying skin reflects its capsular/ligamentous origin.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ganglion cyst arises via one-way valve defect in joint capsule — synovial fluid herniates out and becomes gelatinous. COMLEX: Wrist ganglia fluctuate in size with wrist motion/activity — this history clue supports ganglion over lipoma.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 7 — Popliteal cyst: mucinous synovial cyst arising from posterior knee joint communication via one-way valve — compare mechanism to dorsal wrist ganglion"},{"id":"sdl15_q20","sdlNumber":15,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ganglion cyst.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 35-year-old man presents with a 3 cm mass on the palmar radial aspect of his wrist near the base of the thenar eminence. It has been present for 8 months and causes mild discomfort with wrist flexion. Transillumination is positive. He asks about treatment options and their recurrence rates. Which statement about recurrence is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"Aspiration has approximately 10% recurrence rate and is therefore preferred over surgical excision in most patients","B":"Aspiration has approximately 59% recurrence rate, while open surgical excision has about a 21% recurrence rate","C":"Surgical excision has a 50-60% recurrence rate because the cyst pedicle commonly regrows from residual capsular tissue","D":"Observation leads to spontaneous resolution in less than 5% of cases, making active treatment always necessary","E":"Recurrence rates are identical regardless of treatment modality and depend solely on continued wrist overuse"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Management of ganglion cysts must be discussed with the patient in the context of recurrence rates. Aspiration carries about 59% recurrence, while open surgical excision — which removes the cyst wall and its pedicle from the joint capsule or ligament — has about 21% recurrence (SDL 25 reading, CDT Orthopedics Ch 11). Observation is also appropriate; in children under 10, up to 80% resolve within a year (Nelson). The choice of management should be individualized based on symptom severity, cyst size, patient preference, and hand dominance. Palmar wrist cysts are in proximity to the radial artery and require careful technique for aspiration or excision.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ganglion recurrence — aspiration ~59%, open excision ~21% (CDT Orthopedics); observation is an option. COMLEX: Palmar cysts near radial artery require caution with aspiration; surgical excision is preferred when intervention is needed.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 7 — Popliteal cyst: conservative observation vs aspiration vs surgical excision decision-making parallels ganglion cyst management"},{"id":"sdl15_q21","sdlNumber":15,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ganglion cyst.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 19-year-old male collegiate baseball pitcher presents with a small lump on the volar radial wrist for 4 months. It is 1 cm, non-tender, smooth, and non-fluctuant. He is concerned it might be cancer. Transillumination is positive. There is no change in size with compression. Radiographs are normal. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":{"A":"Reassure the patient that ganglion cysts are benign, and offer observation with activity modification as an initial option","B":"Order an MRI with contrast to rule out low-grade synovial sarcoma before any further management is planned","C":"Perform immediate needle aspiration in the office to confirm diagnosis and provide therapeutic decompression","D":"Refer to hand surgery for urgent excision given the patient's athletic demands and occupational requirements","E":"Obtain an ultrasound-guided core biopsy of the mass because transillumination is not specific enough for diagnosis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A small, non-tender, transilluminating mass on the wrist in a young patient with a typical clinical presentation is reliably a ganglion cyst. Reassurance and observation are appropriate as a first step, given that the mass is benign and the course lists observation as first-line (SDL 14/15 slide p28). MRI or biopsy is not indicated when the clinical diagnosis is clear and there are no atypical features such as rapid growth, firmness, or systemic symptoms suggesting malignancy. Aspiration or referral to hand surgery can be offered if the patient desires intervention, but immediate escalation is not warranted for a small, asymptomatic, and uncomplicated presentation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Transilluminating wrist mass with typical clinical features = ganglion cyst; observation is first-line. COMLEX: Avoid overinvestigation — clinical diagnosis of ganglion does not require biopsy or advanced imaging.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 7 — Popliteal cyst: reassurance and observation appropriate for asymptomatic synovial cysts regardless of anatomical location"},{"id":"sdl15_q22","sdlNumber":15,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of wrist and hand osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 68-year-old retired seamstress presents with 5 years of bilateral hand pain and stiffness. She notes bony enlargement of the DIP and PIP joints of multiple fingers bilaterally. Radiographs reveal joint space narrowing, subchondral sclerosis, and osteophytes at the DIP and PIP joints. Lab work including ESR, CRP, RF, and anti-CCP antibodies are all within normal limits. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Rheumatoid arthritis with seronegative presentation affecting the DIP and PIP joints symmetrically","B":"Psoriatic arthritis with DIP predominant involvement and enthesopathy at the extensor tendon insertions","C":"Gout with monosodium urate crystal deposition causing recurrent periarticular bone erosion at multiple joints","D":"Primary hand osteoarthritis with Heberden nodes at DIP joints and Bouchard nodes at PIP joints","E":"Reactive arthritis triggered by prior infection, causing polyarticular DIP and PIP joint inflammation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Primary hand osteoarthritis is characterized by Heberden nodes at DIP joints and Bouchard nodes at PIP joints, with radiographic findings of joint space narrowing, subchondral sclerosis, and osteophytes. Normal inflammatory markers and seronegative labs distinguish it from rheumatoid arthritis, which is seropositive in most cases and characteristically spares the DIP joints. Psoriatic arthritis can involve the DIP joints but typically shows \"pencil in cup\" erosions on X-ray and is associated with psoriatic skin or nail changes. Gout shows tophi and negatively birefringent urate crystals on joint aspiration. A retired seamstress with bilateral symmetric DIP and PIP bony enlargement and normal labs is classic for primary OA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hand OA = Heberden nodes (DIP) + Bouchard nodes (PIP); OA spares MCP; RA spares DIP. COMLEX: Normal ESR/CRP and seronegative labs confirm OA over inflammatory arthritis in this presentation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis in general: cartilage degeneration, subchondral sclerosis, and osteophyte formation pathophysiology underlying all OA including hand OA"},{"id":"sdl15_q23","sdlNumber":15,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of wrist and hand osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 60-year-old postmenopausal woman presents with a 2-year history of pain and weakness at the base of her right thumb. She reports difficulty opening jars and turning a key. Exam reveals tenderness at the thenar eminence proximal to the MCP joint, a palpable bony ridge at the 1st CMC joint, and a positive grind test. Radiographs show joint space narrowing and osteophytes at the 1st CMC joint. Which of the following best describes the initial management?","choices":{"A":"Surgical trapeziectomy with ligament reconstruction is the recommended initial treatment given radiographic OA","B":"Thumb spica splinting combined with NSAIDs and activity modification as the first-line conservative approach","C":"Systemic corticosteroids for 2 weeks followed by hand therapy to reduce acute flare and restore grip strength","D":"Intraarticular methotrexate injection into the 1st CMC joint to slow cartilage degeneration over time","E":"Platelet-rich plasma injection into the 1st CMC joint should be offered before any splinting or NSAID use"},"correct":"B","explanation":"First CMC joint OA is initially managed conservatively with thumb spica splinting to immobilize the joint and reduce mechanical stress, combined with NSAIDs for pain control and activity modification. Occupational therapy with hand exercises is also beneficial. Corticosteroid injections into the 1st CMC joint are useful for acute flares. Surgical trapeziectomy with ligament reconstruction is reserved for patients who fail conservative management over several months and have functional limitations. Systemic corticosteroids are not appropriate for focal OA. Intraarticular methotrexate and PRP are not standard evidence-based treatments for 1st CMC OA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: 1st CMC OA = thumb spica, NSAIDs, activity modification; corticosteroid injection for flares; surgery if refractory. COMLEX: Grind test positive (axial compression + rotation of 1st metacarpal) = 1st CMC OA; Finkelstein positive = De Quervain.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 1 — Osteoarthritis in general: conservative management principles including splinting, NSAIDs, and activity modification applicable to 1st CMC OA"},{"id":"sdl15_q24","sdlNumber":15,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of wrist and hand osteoarthritis.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"Two women aged 64 have symptomatic hand osteoarthritis with similar radiographic changes. Patient 1 has two involved DIP joints and still does all her own housework. Patient 2 has eleven involved joints across both hands and already needs help with buttons and jars. According to the course, which statement about their outlook is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"Both have the same outlook, because radiographic severity is the main predictor of how hand osteoarthritis will progress","B":"Patient 1 has the worse outlook, because disease limited to a few DIP joints tends to become erosive and spread faster","C":"Patient 2 has the worse outlook, because lower baseline function and more involved joints predict a worse prognosis","D":"Patient 2 has the better outlook, because widespread involvement spreads load and slows progression in any one joint","E":"Neither outlook can be judged until inflammatory markers are measured, because hand OA progression tracks the ESR"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course states that hand osteoarthritis is usually bilateral and symmetric with variable progression, that radiographic severity does not necessarily correlate with the clinical presentation, and that a worse prognosis is linked to lower baseline function and an increasing number of involved joints. Patient 2 has both. Equal radiographs do not mean an equal outlook, since films do not track clinical course closely. Limited DIP disease is not described as a predictor of erosive spread. More involved joints worsen, not improve, the outlook. Hand OA is not monitored by ESR; inflammatory markers are typically normal in primary hand OA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hand OA: DIP (Heberden) and PIP (Bouchard) nodes, first CMC in postmenopausal women; erosive OA targets IP joints with central erosions. RA spares DIPs. COMLEX: Management follows a modified OARSI approach with orthoses; surgery for refractory cases.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 8 — OARSI framework for osteoarthritis management"},{"id":"sdl15_q25","sdlNumber":15,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of trigger finger.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 46-year-old woman has 3 weeks of painful catching of the right thumb when she bends and straightens it, relieved by a snap. It never locks, and there is a tender nodule at the base of the thumb over the MCP joint. She wants to avoid injections and surgery for now. Which treatment is part of the conservative care described in the course readings?","choices":{"A":"Excision of the palpable nodule under local anesthesia, since removing it stops catching without opening the A1 pulley","B":"Long arm casting with the elbow flexed for 6 weeks, since resting the forearm flexor muscles unloads the flexor sheath","C":"Daily forceful passive stretching into full extension through the catch, since repeated snapping wears the nodule down","D":"Oral corticosteroids for 3 months, since a systemic taper reaches the tendon sheath as well as a local injection does","E":"Rest, anti-inflammatory medication, and immobilizing the digit with a finger splint or buddy tape to settle the sheath"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The Tintinalli hand chapter assigned with this SDL lists conservative treatment of trigger finger as rest, anti-inflammatory medication, and immobilization with buddy tape or a finger splint to reduce inflammation and swelling of the flexor sheath; the course slide likewise lists NSAIDs and splinting with corticosteroid injection, and surgery is rare. Corticosteroid injection treats early triggering, and A1 pulley release is curative when conservative care and injection fail. Excising the nodule alone is not a described treatment. A long arm cast does not target the digital sheath. Repeated forceful snapping irritates the sheath rather than treating it. A prolonged oral steroid course is not part of the course plan.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Trigger finger: nodular flexor tendon catches at the A1 pulley; ring finger and thumb most common. Ladder: rest/NSAIDs/splint → sheath corticosteroid injection → A1 pulley release (>90% cure). COMLEX: Diabetes and RA increase risk and recurrence.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 6 — De Quervain tenosynovitis, another stenosing tenosynovitis"},{"id":"sdl15_q26","sdlNumber":15,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of trigger finger.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman presents with trigger finger of the right long finger that has failed two corticosteroid injections over 6 months. She now has a finger that locks fully in flexion and must be manually extended. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?","choices":{"A":"A third corticosteroid injection combined with formal hand therapy for 8 weeks before considering surgery","B":"Surgical release of the A1 pulley under local anesthesia, which has a greater than 90% cure rate","C":"Splinting the MCP joint in full extension for 6 weeks to allow A1 pulley remodeling and tendon decompression","D":"Oral methotrexate for 3 months to address underlying inflammatory tenosynovitis contributing to pulley stenosis","E":"Excision of the flexor tendon nodule alone, leaving the thickened A1 pulley intact to preserve finger flexion"},"correct":"B","explanation":"After two failed corticosteroid injections, surgical A1 pulley release is the appropriate next step. The procedure involves dividing the A1 pulley longitudinally under local anesthesia, either through an open incision or percutaneously, which decompresses the tendon and resolves triggering. Surgical release is curative in more than 90% of digits refractory to steroid injection (SDL 25 reading, CDT Orthopedics Ch 11), and complications are rare. A third injection provides minimal additional benefit after two failures, especially in a locking trigger finger. Splinting alone does not decompress a stenotic pulley. Methotrexate is used for inflammatory arthritis, not idiopathic trigger finger. Excising the tendon nodule while leaving the stenotic A1 pulley intact does not decompress the tendon.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Refractory trigger finger after 2 injections = A1 pulley surgical release; greater than 90% cure rate (CDT Orthopedics). COMLEX: Open vs percutaneous A1 release both effective; percutaneous avoided near neurovascular structures in thumb and index finger.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 2 — Connective tissue composition and roles: A1 pulley surgical division relieves mechanical obstruction to tendon gliding through fibro-osseous canal"},{"id":"sdl15_q27","sdlNumber":15,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of trigger finger.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 42-year-old man with no significant medical history develops trigger finger of the right middle finger after 6 weeks of intensive grip training at a new warehouse job. He describes a painful snap when bending the finger, with the finger occasionally locking in a bent position. The MCP base is tender. Which comorbid condition, if present, would most significantly reduce the expected response rate to a single corticosteroid injection?","choices":{"A":"Diabetes mellitus, which promotes fibrosis of the A1 pulley and impairs tendon sheath response to corticosteroids","B":"Hypertension, which impairs microvascular supply to the tendon sheath and prolongs inflammatory resolution","C":"Osteoporosis, which results in subchondral softening of the MCP joint that amplifies compressive forces on the A1 pulley","D":"Hypothyroidism, which causes diffuse mucin deposition in the tenosynovium and is not responsive to steroids","E":"Obesity, which increases grip force demand on the A1 pulley and accelerates pulley stenosis after any injection"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Diabetes mellitus is the most significant comorbidity that reduces corticosteroid injection efficacy in trigger finger. Diabetes promotes glycation of collagen and fibrosis of the tendon sheath and pulleys, creating a more fibrotic and less responsive pathological substrate. Additionally, corticosteroid injections in diabetic patients can transiently elevate blood glucose. In the general population, a single corticosteroid injection resolves trigger finger in 60-90% of patients, but diabetics are less likely to respond (SDL 25 reading, CDT Orthopedics) and recurrence is more common. Hypothyroidism is associated with trigger finger due to mucinous tenosynovial deposits but does respond to treatment. Hypertension and obesity are not directly associated with reduced injection response rates.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Diabetes mellitus reduces corticosteroid injection efficacy for trigger finger and increases recurrence — plan for earlier surgical referral. COMLEX: Also note hypothyroidism as a risk factor for trigger finger (mucinous tenosynovitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 2 — Connective tissue composition and roles: collagen glycation in diabetes promotes A1 pulley fibrosis, reducing responsiveness to steroid-mediated inflammation modulation"},{"id":"sdl15_q28","sdlNumber":15,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ulnar collateral ligament injury of the thumb.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 24-year-old skier presents after a fall in which her right thumb was caught on a ski pole and forced radially. She reports MCP joint pain and difficulty pinching. Exam reveals swelling and ecchymosis over the ulnar MCP joint, tenderness at the ulnar collateral ligament, and laxity with valgus stress at 30 degrees of MCP flexion but not at 0 degrees. What does the pattern of laxity suggest?","choices":{"A":"Complete UCL rupture with Stener lesion, because laxity at 30 degrees confirms total ligament disruption","B":"Volar plate rupture of the MCP joint, which produces laxity at 30 degrees flexion and 0 degrees equally","C":"Radial collateral ligament sprain, because the radial side is stressed when the thumb is forced into radial deviation","D":"Physeal fracture at the base of the proximal phalanx, which must be ruled out by radiograph before stress testing","E":"Proper UCL injury with an intact accessory collateral ligament and volar plate, since laxity appears only in flexion"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In thumb MCP stress testing, laxity at 30 degrees of flexion indicates injury to the proper UCL, as the proper UCL is the primary stabilizer in flexion. Laxity at both 0 degrees and 30 degrees of MCP flexion indicates a more complete injury involving both the proper and accessory UCL. In this vignette, laxity only at 30 degrees with stability at 0 degrees localizes the injury to the proper UCL with an intact accessory UCL and volar plate; the degree of opening and comparison with the other thumb determine whether the tear is partial or complete. Complete tears show laxity at both angles. Volar plate injury would not isolate laxity to 30 degrees flexion only. A Stener lesion cannot be confirmed or excluded by clinical examination alone — MRI is required.","boardPrep":"Step 1: UCL stress test — laxity at 30 degrees = proper UCL; laxity at 0 degrees = accessory UCL also torn. More than 30-35 degrees or 10-15 degrees more than contralateral = complete tear (Tintinalli, SDL 25 reading); the Atlas (SDL 14/15 reading) gives 30-40 degrees. COMLEX: Always radiograph first before stress testing to rule out avulsion fracture.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 1 — Interphalangeal joint dislocations: valgus and varus stress testing principles for hand joint ligament integrity assessment"},{"id":"sdl15_q29","sdlNumber":15,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ulnar collateral ligament injury of the thumb.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 30-year-old rugby player sustains a UCL injury of the right thumb. MRI confirms a complete UCL tear with a Stener lesion. The injury occurred 4 days ago. What is the most appropriate management?","choices":{"A":"Thumb spica cast for 6 weeks because Stener lesions can resolve spontaneously with immobilization alone","B":"Corticosteroid injection into the MCP joint to reduce inflammation followed by functional bracing for 4 weeks","C":"Platelet-rich plasma injection at the UCL insertion to promote tissue regeneration and avoid surgery","D":"Surgical repair of the UCL with reapproximation of the torn ends within about 1 week, before fibrosis occurs","E":"Dynamic splinting of the MCP joint in ulnar deviation for 4 weeks to allow UCL scar tissue approximation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A Stener lesion — interposition of the adductor pollicis aponeurosis between the torn UCL ends — prevents anatomic healing of the torn ligament with conservative treatment. Surgical repair is required and should be performed within 1 week (Atlas of Emergency Medicine, SDL 14/15 reading; Tintinalli advises hand surgery referral within 7 days), as fibrosis and scar tissue formation after that time make anatomic repair technically more difficult. The surgeon excises the interposed aponeurosis, reapproximates the UCL ends, and may augment with a suture anchor. Conservative management with thumb spica casting is appropriate only for partial tears without Stener lesion. Corticosteroid injection and PRP are not indicated for acute ligamentous injuries requiring operative repair.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Stener lesion = surgery required; repair within 1 week (Atlas). COMLEX: Failure to diagnose Stener lesion leads to chronic MCP instability — clinical pearl for COMLEX clinical presentations.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 1 — Interphalangeal joint dislocations: surgical vs conservative management decision-making based on ligamentous integrity and interposed tissue"},{"id":"sdl15_q30","sdlNumber":15,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of ulnar collateral ligament injury of the thumb.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 50-year-old gamekeeper presents with chronic right thumb weakness for 2 years. He reports difficulty with pinch grip and a history of repetitive thumb hyperabduction while wringing the necks of game birds. Exam reveals MCP joint laxity with valgus stress and weak key pinch. Radiographs show mild 1st MCP OA. What term describes his injury, and what is its key distinguishing feature from acute Skier's thumb?","choices":{"A":"Skier's thumb — an acute UCL rupture with Stener lesion that presents identically in chronic form with pinch weakness","B":"De Quervain tenosynovitis — chronic radial-sided wrist pain from repetitive thumb loading that mimics UCL pathology","C":"Gamekeeper's thumb — chronic UCL insufficiency from repetitive microtrauma, rather than one acute complete tear","D":"Trigger thumb — chronic A1 pulley stenosis from repetitive thumb gripping that secondarily loosens the UCL over time","E":"First CMC OA — degenerative joint disease of the thumb base from grip overuse that does not affect the UCL"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Gamekeeper's thumb describes chronic UCL insufficiency from repetitive microtrauma — originally described in Scottish gamekeepers who routinely wrung the necks of game, stressing the thumb UCL thousands of times. Over time, the UCL stretches and becomes insufficient, producing progressive MCP instability, pinch weakness, and secondary OA. This is distinguished from Skier's thumb, which is an acute complete UCL tear from a single traumatic event. Both involve the UCL, but gamekeeper's thumb represents cumulative attenuation rather than acute rupture, and Stener lesions are less common in chronic cases. Treatment for chronic gamekeeper's thumb may require ligament reconstruction rather than primary repair.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Gamekeeper's thumb = chronic UCL laxity from repetitive stress; Skier's thumb = acute UCL rupture from single event. COMLEX: Both cause MCP valgus instability and difficulty pinching — distinguish by mechanism and chronicity on boards.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 1 — Interphalangeal joint dislocations: chronic ligamentous insufficiency from repetitive microtrauma compared to acute dislocation mechanisms"},{"id":"sdl15_q31","sdlNumber":15,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of extensor finger injury of the DIP.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 24-year-old man has a closed soft-tissue mallet finger of the left middle finger with no fracture on radiographs. In urgent care, the finger was placed in an aluminum splint that holds both the PIP and DIP joints straight. According to the course reading, how should the splinting be corrected?","choices":{"A":"Splint the DIP joint by itself in extension and leave the PIP free, wearing it continuously for at least 6 weeks","B":"Splint the PIP joint by itself in flexion and leave the DIP free, so the lateral bands relax and pull the tendon back","C":"Splint the wrist and all fingers in a resting cast, since the extensor tendon must be unloaded at the forearm","D":"Remove the splint and begin active DIP flexion exercises now, so that the joint does not become stiff in extension","E":"Splint the DIP joint in 45 degrees of flexion for 3 weeks, so the tendon heals at its resting length first"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The Emergency Medicine Atlas reading for this SDL states that a closed mallet finger without joint involvement is treated by splinting the DIP joint in extension, that the PIP joint should not be splinted, and that the splint is worn continuously for at least 6 weeks; operative treatment is usually not required unless more than one-third of the articular surface is avulsed. The course slide gives full extension for 6 weeks. Splinting the PIP alone leaves the DIP to droop and does not oppose the injury. A forearm and hand cast immobilizes joints that do not need it. Early DIP flexion would pull the healing tendon ends apart. Splinting the DIP in flexion places the torn extensor at its longest and prevents healing.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Mallet finger = terminal extensor tendon avulsion (forced flexion of an extended DIP); most common finger tendon injury, more common in males. COMLEX: Any DIP flexion during the 6 weeks restarts the clock; untreated mallet → swan neck deformity.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 1 — interphalangeal joint dislocation management"},{"id":"sdl15_q32","sdlNumber":15,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of extensor finger injury of the DIP.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 34-year-old man presents 10 days after sustaining a mallet finger of the right long finger while playing basketball. He has been using buddy tape since the injury. Radiographs show a dorsal avulsion fracture involving approximately 15% of the DIP articular surface without volar subluxation. The DIP joint rests in 40 degrees of flexion. What is the most appropriate management at this time?","choices":{"A":"The 10-day delay makes conservative splinting ineffective; immediate surgical pin fixation is now required","B":"The avulsion fracture size mandates surgical ORIF regardless of delay to restore articular congruence","C":"Obtain MRI to assess soft tissue terminal extensor integrity before committing to surgical vs conservative care","D":"Apply a DIP extension splint now and treat conservatively with continuous splinting for 8 weeks from this visit","E":"Apply a short arm cast with the wrist in extension and DIP in neutral for 6 weeks to off-load the extensor"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Mallet finger presenting 10 days after injury can still be managed conservatively with DIP extension splinting, provided the fracture involves less than one-third of the articular surface and there is no volar subluxation of the distal phalanx. A 15% articular surface avulsion fragment without subluxation meets these criteria. The splinting period is calculated from the time splinting begins — so 8 weeks of continuous splinting from this visit is appropriate. Surgical indications are: fragment > 1/3 articular surface, volar subluxation, or open injury. A short delay in presentation does not automatically mandate surgery. MRI is not needed when radiographs provide sufficient information for surgical decision-making.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Conservative mallet finger criteria — fragment less than 1/3 articular surface AND no volar subluxation, regardless of delay. Splinting duration starts from when splinting begins. COMLEX: Counsel patient that any DIP flexion during the 8 weeks restarts the clock.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 5 — Hand and wrist fractures: articular surface involvement criteria determine conservative vs operative management for intraarticular avulsion fractures"},{"id":"sdl15_q33","sdlNumber":15,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of extensor finger injury of the DIP.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A chronic mallet finger deformity that is left untreated for more than 3 months can lead to a secondary deformity affecting the PIP joint. What is this secondary deformity and what causes it?","choices":{"A":"Boutonniere deformity, in which the central slip tears at the PIP joint and causes the PIP to flex and DIP to extend","B":"Swan neck deformity, in which the lateral bands migrate dorsally and hyperextend the PIP joint as the volar plate becomes lax","C":"Trigger finger deformity, in which the lateral bands tighten the flexor sheath at the A1 pulley and cause PIP locking","D":"Mallet deformity extension proximally, in which the extensor tendon attrition spreads from the DIP to the PIP level","E":"Z-deformity of the finger, in which the extensor mechanism imbalance causes alternating flexion and extension at each joint"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Chronic untreated mallet finger can lead to swan neck deformity. As the DIP rests in flexion chronically, the extensor mechanism proximal to the DIP becomes slack. The lateral bands — normally held in position by the triangular ligament — can migrate dorsally at the PIP joint level, creating a tethering force that hyperextends the PIP joint. The volar plate, which normally prevents PIP hyperextension, stretches and becomes lax over time. The resulting swan neck deformity has DIP flexion and PIP hyperextension. Boutonniere deformity involves central slip disruption causing PIP flexion and DIP extension — the opposite pattern. Recognizing swan neck as the secondary complication of untreated mallet finger is a board-tested correlation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Untreated mallet finger → swan neck deformity (DIP flexion + PIP hyperextension) from dorsal lateral band migration. Boutonniere = central slip rupture → PIP flexion + DIP extension. COMLEX: Swan neck deformity is also seen in RA; distinguish by history.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 5 — Hand and wrist fractures: extensor mechanism anatomy at the DIP and PIP levels is foundational to understanding secondary deformities from untreated bony and soft-tissue injuries"},{"id":"sdl15_q34","sdlNumber":15,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of flexor tendon injury of the DIP.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 20-year-old rugby player cannot bend the tip of his ring finger after grabbing an opponent's jersey. With the MCP and PIP joints held in extension, he cannot flex the DIP joint; passive motion is full. Radiographs show no bony fragment. What is the most appropriate management in the emergency department?","choices":{"A":"Splint the DIP joint in full extension for 6 weeks without interruption and recheck in clinic, as for a mallet finger","B":"Buddy tape the ring finger to the long finger and start active flexion exercises, since most cases heal with motion","C":"Order MRI before any splinting, because radiographs are not useful for this injury and must be skipped","D":"Splint the PIP and DIP joints in mild flexion and refer to orthopedics, since every jersey finger needs referral","E":"Inject corticosteroid into the flexor sheath at the A1 pulley to reduce swelling and restore active DIP flexion"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The course slide lists acute management of jersey finger as a splint with the PIP and DIP in mild flexion, with all cases referred to orthopedics; the Atlas reading notes that jersey finger often needs surgical repair, that early referral is recommended, and that prognosis worsens with delay or severe tendon retraction. Isolating the DIP by holding the MCP and PIP in extension tests FDP function. Splinting the DIP in full extension is mallet finger treatment and would stretch the injured flexor. Buddy taping with active motion does not repair an avulsed tendon. Radiographs are indicated to look for a bony avulsion, with MRI if radiographs are unclear. Steroid injection at the A1 pulley treats trigger finger, not tendon avulsion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Jersey finger = FDP avulsion from forced extension of a flexed DIP; ring finger ~75%; test FDP by isolating the DIP. COMLEX: Leddy-Packer type I (retraction into the palm) needs repair within days because the vincular supply is lost.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — mallet finger, the extensor counterpart at the DIP"},{"id":"sdl15_q35","sdlNumber":15,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of flexor tendon injury of the DIP.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"An 18-year-old wrestler is diagnosed with a Leddy-Packer Type II jersey finger of the right ring finger, 8 days after the injury. On radiographs, no bony fragment is seen. Which statement about this injury type and its timing of repair is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"Type II involves FDP retraction into the palm with both vincula ruptured, requiring surgery within 3-5 days to prevent necrosis","B":"Type II involves a bony avulsion fragment at the A4 pulley level, and repair can be delayed up to 3 months without tendon necrosis","C":"Type II involves FDP avulsion with intact A2 pulley that prevents any proximal tendon retraction beyond the midshaft","D":"Type II is the rarest subtype and involves simultaneous FDS and FDP avulsion at the DIP joint level","E":"Type II involves FDP retraction to the PIP joint level with intact short vinculum, allowing repair within several weeks of injury"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In the Leddy-Packer classification, Type II jersey finger occurs when the FDP tendon retracts to the PIP joint level, where it is held by the intact short vinculum (long vinculum is ruptured). Because the short vinculum maintains some blood supply to the tendon, the tendon is viable for a longer window — the tendon may be reinserted within several weeks of injury (SDL 25 reading, CDT Orthopedics Ch 11). Type I (retraction into palm) disrupts both vincula and requires surgery within days. Type III involves a bony avulsion fragment large enough to catch at the A4 pulley, retaining blood supply and allowing surgical repair even after a longer delay. The absence of a radiographic bony fragment in this case makes Type III less likely.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Leddy-Packer — Type I: palm retraction, both vincula gone, urgent surgery within days. Type II: PIP level, short vinculum intact, repair within several weeks. Type III: bony fragment at A4, delayed repair possible. COMLEX: Timing of repair determines tendon viability.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — Little league shoulder and elbow: vascular supply to avulsed tendons determines surgical urgency — same principle applies to Leddy-Packer classification of jersey finger"},{"id":"sdl15_q36","sdlNumber":15,"batch":2,"objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnosis, and management of flexor tendon injury of the DIP.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 25-year-old rugby player presents 3 weeks after sustaining a jersey finger of the right ring finger that was never diagnosed. He now has a small firm mass at the proximal palm and cannot flex his DIP at all. MRI confirms the FDP tendon has retracted into the palm with disruption of both vincula. What is the expected finding at surgery, and what does this mean for repair options?","choices":{"A":"The tendon is supple and can be pulled back through the intact sheath for primary repair despite the 3-week delay","B":"Tendon fibrosis and retraction make primary repair impossible; tendon grafting or two-stage reconstruction is required","C":"The tendon has healed onto the lumbrical origin in the palm and can be re-routed through the digital canal primarily","D":"The A4 pulley has remodeled and widened in the absence of the tendon, allowing graft passage without pulley reconstruction","E":"Spontaneous healing of the FDP tendon to the A2 pulley has occurred, and DIP arthrodesis is therefore unnecessary"},"correct":"B","explanation":"When a Type I jersey finger (FDP retracted into palm with both vincula disrupted) is diagnosed after 3 weeks, the tendon has undergone significant fibrosis, shortening, and loss of elasticity. The tendon cannot be retrieved and primarily repaired through the intact digital flexor sheath without prohibitive tension and risk of sheath rupture. Surgical options at this stage include tendon grafting (one-stage if sheath is supple) or two-stage reconstruction (Hunter rod placement first, then graft). In some patients, DIP arthrodesis in a functional position is preferred for its reliability. The firm palmar mass corresponds to the fibrosed, retracted FDP tendon end. Primary repair is not feasible after this delay in Type I injuries.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Neglected Type I jersey finger (past the 7-10 day repair window, CDT Orthopedics) = tendon fibrosis; primary repair not possible; requires tendon grafting or two-stage reconstruction. COMLEX: Delayed jersey finger is a common board scenario — know the time-sensitive Type I window.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — Little league shoulder and elbow: delayed avulsion repair leads to fibrosis and requires staged reconstruction — principle applies to neglected jersey finger"}],"16":[{"id":"sdl16_q01","sdlNumber":16,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of humerus fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A midshaft humerus fracture in the spiral groove is most commonly associated with injury to which nerve, and what is the classic clinical deficit?","choices":{"A":"Axillary nerve, causing loss of deltoid function and shoulder abduction deficit","B":"Median nerve, causing loss of thumb opposition and thenar muscle wasting","C":"Radial nerve, causing wrist drop from loss of wrist and finger extension","D":"Ulnar nerve, causing claw hand deformity and loss of intrinsic hand muscle function","E":"Musculocutaneous nerve, causing loss of elbow flexion and forearm supination"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The radial nerve travels through the spiral groove of the midshaft humerus and is the most commonly injured nerve with midshaft humerus fractures. Injury produces wrist drop — inability to extend the wrist and fingers — due to paralysis of the extensor muscles of the forearm. The axillary nerve, in contrast, is injured with surgical neck fractures of the proximal humerus, producing deltoid weakness and loss of shoulder abduction. Median nerve injury at the elbow level is associated with supracondylar fractures. Ulnar nerve injury occurs with medial epicondyle fractures.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Midshaft humerus = radial nerve = wrist drop. Surgical neck = axillary nerve = deltoid weakness. Supracondylar = anterior interosseous nerve (median branch). COMLEX: Radial nerve runs in the spiral groove; Holstein-Lewis fracture is a distal third spiral fracture with especially high radial nerve injury risk.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 3 — Rotator cuff injuries and shoulder pathologies: surgical neck of humerus fractures damage the axillary nerve in the same anatomical region"},{"id":"sdl16_q02","sdlNumber":16,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of humerus fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A proximal humerus fracture at the surgical neck is most likely to injure which nerve, and what is the primary clinical deficit?","choices":{"A":"Radial nerve, producing wrist drop and loss of finger extension distal to the elbow","B":"Suprascapular nerve, producing weakness of both supraspinatus and infraspinatus with external rotation loss","C":"Long thoracic nerve, producing winging of the scapula due to serratus anterior paralysis","D":"Axillary nerve, producing weakness of deltoid contraction and loss of active shoulder abduction","E":"Median nerve, producing loss of thumb opposition and flexor pollicis longus weakness"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The axillary nerve runs around the surgical neck of the humerus in the quadrilateral space, making it vulnerable to injury with proximal humerus fractures at this location. Axillary nerve injury causes weakness or paralysis of the deltoid muscle, with loss of active shoulder abduction. There is also loss of sensation over the lateral deltoid region (regimental badge area). Proximal humerus fractures are especially common in older women, accounting for 4-5% of all fractures. Radial nerve injury is associated with midshaft fractures, not surgical neck. Suprascapular nerve injury occurs with shoulder dislocations or space-occupying lesions at the spinoglenoid notch.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Surgical neck humerus fracture = axillary nerve injury = deltoid weakness + lateral shoulder numbness (regimental badge area). COMLEX: Also test for axillary nerve injury after anterior shoulder dislocation, which can also stretch the nerve.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 3 — Rotator cuff injuries and shoulder pathologies: surgical neck of humerus fractures damage the axillary nerve in the same anatomical region"},{"id":"sdl16_q03","sdlNumber":16,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of humerus fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the initial management of a non-displaced proximal humerus fracture in a 75-year-old woman?","choices":{"A":"Open reduction and internal fixation is indicated because osteoporosis prevents adequate fracture stability","B":"Shoulder arthroplasty is the preferred initial treatment for all proximal humerus fractures in older adults","C":"Percutaneous pinning under fluoroscopy is the standard of care for all proximal humerus fractures","D":"Immediate surgical repair is required for all proximal humerus fractures to prevent axillary nerve injury","E":"Non-surgical management with a sling or coaptation splint and early gentle range-of-motion exercises"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The majority of proximal humerus fractures are non-displaced and can be managed non-surgically with a sling or coaptation splint, followed by early supervised gentle range-of-motion exercises to prevent shoulder stiffness. This is especially true in older adults where surgical risks may outweigh benefits. Displaced and/or comminuted fractures may require surgical intervention — including ORIF or shoulder arthroplasty — depending on patient factors and fracture pattern. Routine surgery for all proximal humerus fractures regardless of displacement is not indicated and may lead to unnecessary morbidity in the elderly.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Non-displaced proximal and midshaft humerus fractures → conservative management with sling. Displaced or comminuted → consider surgery. COMLEX: Sling with early range of motion starting about 2 weeks after injury (SDL 25 reading, CDT Orthopedics Ch 3), then progressive PT; early motion prevents frozen shoulder.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 3 — Rotator cuff injuries and shoulder pathologies: surgical neck of humerus fractures damage the axillary nerve in the same anatomical region"},{"id":"sdl16_q04","sdlNumber":16,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of supracondylar fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A Gartland Type III supracondylar humerus fracture in a 5-year-old is defined by which radiographic feature, and what is the standard treatment?","choices":{"A":"Non-displaced fracture with effusion and normal alignment; treated with long arm splinting alone","B":"Fracture displaced greater than 2 mm with anterior humeral line disruption; treated with closed reduction only","C":"Fracture with medial cortical comminution; treated with open reduction and internal fixation immediately","D":"Complete displacement of the distal fragment; treated with urgent closed reduction and percutaneous pinning","E":"Stress fracture of the distal humerus without cortical disruption; treated with observation and activity restriction"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Gartland Type III supracondylar fractures are completely displaced with no cortical contact between fracture fragments. They require urgent orthopedic consultation for closed reduction and percutaneous Kirschner wire fixation (CRPP) under general anesthesia. The urgency is driven by the risk of neurovascular injury — particularly to the brachial artery and anterior interosseous nerve — and the risk of compartment syndrome. Type I fractures are minimally displaced and treated with splinting; Type II fractures are displaced more than 2 mm with intact posterior cortex and may be treated with casting or CRPP depending on stability.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Gartland I = splint; Type II = cast vs CRPP; Type III = CRPP urgently. COMLEX: AIN (anterior interosseous nerve, branch of median) is the most commonly injured nerve; \"OK sign\" inability tests AIN.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 3 — Ulnar nerve entrapment at the elbow: medial epicondyle anatomy is directly relevant to nerve injury patterns in pediatric elbow fractures"},{"id":"sdl16_q05","sdlNumber":16,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of supracondylar fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which nerve is most commonly injured in a pediatric supracondylar humerus fracture, and what is the classic clinical test for its function?","choices":{"A":"Radial nerve, tested by asking the child to extend the wrist and fingers against resistance","B":"Ulnar nerve, tested by asking the child to abduct and adduct the fingers against resistance","C":"Anterior interosseous nerve, tested by having the child make an \"OK\" sign with thumb and index finger","D":"Axillary nerve, tested by assessing sensation over the lateral deltoid and deltoid contraction strength","E":"Median nerve proper, tested by asking the child to oppose the thumb to the little finger"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The anterior interosseous nerve (AIN), a purely motor branch of the median nerve, is the most commonly injured nerve in supracondylar humerus fractures. AIN injury produces inability to flex the terminal phalanx of the thumb and index finger, so the child cannot make a perfect \"OK\" sign — instead producing a pinch pattern with the interphalangeal joints extended rather than flexed. The AIN also innervates the flexor pollicis longus, the flexor digitorum profundus to the index/middle fingers, and pronator quadratus. Radial nerve and ulnar nerve injuries occur less frequently but should also be assessed. Brachial artery injury is the feared vascular complication.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Supracondylar fracture — AIN (median branch) most commonly injured; cannot make \"OK\" sign. COMLEX: Also assess radial pulse and brachial artery perfusion; \"pink pulseless hand\" after reduction may be observed if perfused via collaterals.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 3 — Ulnar nerve entrapment at the elbow: medial epicondyle anatomy is directly relevant to nerve injury patterns in pediatric elbow fractures"},{"id":"sdl16_q06","sdlNumber":16,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of supracondylar fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Supracondylar fractures account for what proportion of pediatric elbow fractures, and what is the most common mechanism of injury?","choices":{"A":"Approximately 20% of pediatric elbow fractures; most result from a direct blow to the posterior elbow during contact sports","B":"Approximately 80% of pediatric elbow fractures; most result from a throwing motion with sudden valgus overload at the elbow","C":"Approximately 40% of pediatric elbow fractures; most result from axial loading during falls in a flexed elbow position","D":"Approximately 10% of pediatric elbow fractures; most result from forced hyperextension during gymnastics tumbling","E":"Approximately 60% of pediatric elbow fractures; most result from a fall on an outstretched hand with the elbow in extension"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Supracondylar humerus fractures represent approximately 60% of all pediatric elbow fractures, making them the most common elbow fracture in children. They are most prevalent between ages 2 and 7 and are rare in adults. The predominant mechanism is a fall on an outstretched hand (FOOSH) with the elbow extended, which occurs in about 70% of cases. The non-dominant extremity is more commonly affected. The hyperextension force drives the olecranon into the olecranon fossa, levering the distal humerus and fracturing the thin supracondylar region of the bone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Supracondylar fracture = 60% of pediatric elbow fractures; peak age 2-7; FOOSH with elbow extended. COMLEX: Supracondylar fracture is rare in adults because the bone remodels and becomes stronger past the pediatric growth phase.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 3 — Ulnar nerve entrapment at the elbow: medial epicondyle anatomy is directly relevant to nerve injury patterns in pediatric elbow fractures"},{"id":"sdl16_q07","sdlNumber":16,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of clavicular fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which segment of the clavicle is fractured in the majority of clavicle fractures, and what proportion does it represent?","choices":{"A":"Distal third of the clavicle near the AC joint, representing approximately 69% of all clavicle fractures","B":"Proximal third of the clavicle near the sternoclavicular joint, representing approximately 50% of all fractures","C":"Junction of the middle and distal thirds, representing approximately 40% of all clavicle fractures","D":"Middle third of the clavicle, representing approximately 69% of all clavicle fractures","E":"Entire clavicle with comminution, representing the majority of fractures in high-energy trauma patients"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Clavicle fractures occur most commonly in the middle third of the clavicle, accounting for approximately 69% of all cases. The distal third accounts for about 28% and the proximal third for only 2.8%. The middle third is vulnerable because it is the thinnest part of the clavicle and lacks the support of the acromioclavicular and sternoclavicular ligaments that stabilize the distal and proximal thirds, respectively. Middle-third fractures result from direct blows or indirect forces transmitted through the shoulder. Clavicle fractures represent about 2.6% of all fractures and peak in children and young adults.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Clavicle fracture location — middle 1/3 = 69%, distal 1/3 = 28%, proximal 1/3 = 2.8%. COMLEX: Middle-third is the most common because it lacks ligamentous support that stabilizes the other thirds.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 1 — Diagnostic workflow for shoulder pain: clavicle fractures enter the shoulder pain differential and are distinguished by location and crepitus"},{"id":"sdl16_q08","sdlNumber":16,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of clavicular fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"What is the first-line treatment for a non-displaced, closed, mid-shaft clavicle fracture in a 25-year-old with no neurovascular compromise?","choices":{"A":"A sling for comfort and pain control, with progressive return to activity as healing occurs","B":"Surgical ORIF with plate fixation because clavicle fractures have high non-union rates without surgery","C":"Figure-of-eight bandage applied in the ER, which is superior to sling for anatomic reduction","D":"Rigid shoulder immobilizer combined with intravenous antibiotics to prevent periosteal infection","E":"Immediate orthopedic referral for closed reduction under fluoroscopy before beginning conservative care"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The vast majority of non-displaced, closed, mid-shaft clavicle fractures are managed conservatively with a sling for comfort and analgesics. A sling is preferred over a figure-of-eight bandage, which does not improve outcomes and causes more patient discomfort. Surgery is not routinely required for non-displaced fractures. Indications for orthopedic referral include open fractures, skin tenting, neurovascular compromise, complete displacement, shortening, comminution, or patients who are athletes concerned about cosmetic deformity. Most mid-shaft fractures heal well with conservative management. The course treats a closed mid-shaft fracture with a sling alone; neither a rigid immobilizer nor antibiotics are part of that plan.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Non-displaced mid-shaft clavicle fracture = sling; figure-of-eight bandage is not superior to sling. Surgery for open, displaced, comminuted, neurovascular compromise, or shortening. COMLEX: Sling preferred over figure-of-eight — boards commonly test this distinction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 1 — Diagnostic workflow for shoulder pain: clavicle fractures enter the shoulder pain differential and are distinguished by location and crepitus"},{"id":"sdl16_q09","sdlNumber":16,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of clavicular fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which finding in a clavicle fracture is an indication for urgent orthopedic referral rather than conservative management with a sling?","choices":{"A":"Tenderness at the fracture site on palpation, which is expected in all clavicle fractures regardless of severity","B":"Radiographic evidence of a distal third fracture, which is always managed with surgery due to AC joint proximity","C":"A patient age greater than 60 years, because osteoporosis prevents adequate healing without internal fixation","D":"Fracture occurring during a recreational sport activity, which indicates higher energy and mandates surgical evaluation","E":"Tenting of the skin overlying the fracture site, indicating risk of skin breakdown and imminent open fracture"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Skin tenting — where the sharp fracture end pushes against the overlying skin from inside — indicates imminent risk of skin penetration and conversion to an open fracture, which carries infection risk and requires urgent orthopedic consultation. Other indications for referral include open fractures, neurovascular compromise (including brachial plexus and subclavian vessel injury), complete displacement, shortening, comminution, and in athletes where cosmetic outcome or return-to-sport timeline matters. Routine mid-shaft tenderness, patient age, and recreational mechanism do not automatically mandate surgery. Distal-third fractures with AC ligament disruption may require surgery but are not automatically referred.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Clavicle ortho referral = open fractures, tenting, neurovascular compromise, complete displacement, shortening, comminution. COMLEX: Neurovascular structures at risk include the brachial plexus and subclavian artery/vein deep to the clavicle.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 1 — Diagnostic workflow for shoulder pain: clavicle fractures enter the shoulder pain differential and are distinguished by location and crepitus"},{"id":"sdl16_q10","sdlNumber":16,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of forearm fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Forearm fractures are the most common fractures in children, representing what proportion of all childhood fractures?","choices":{"A":"Approximately 10-15% of all childhood fractures, with the proximal third being the most frequent location","B":"Approximately 40-50% of all childhood fractures, with the distal third being the most frequent location","C":"Approximately 20-25% of all childhood fractures, with the midshaft being the most frequent location","D":"Approximately 60-70% of all childhood fractures, with the entire forearm equally distributed by region","E":"Approximately 5% of all childhood fractures, with forearm fractures less common than femur or tibia fractures"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Forearm fractures are the most common fractures in children, accounting for approximately 40-50% of all childhood fractures. The distal third of the forearm is the most commonly fractured segment, responsible for approximately 75% of all forearm fractures in children and 20-25% of all pediatric fractures. The peak age is 10-14 years, when a relative mismatch between rapidly increasing body mass and bone strength creates vulnerability during the adolescent growth spurt. Falls on an outstretched hand (FOOSH) are the predominant mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Forearm fractures = most common childhood fracture (40-50%); distal 1/3 most common (75% of forearm); peak age 10-14. COMLEX: Without plausible mechanism in a child, forearm fracture warrants consideration of non-accidental trauma (child abuse).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 3 — Differences between adult and pediatric bone stress: buckle, greenstick, and physeal fractures occur in children due to differences in bone flexibility and growth plate vulnerability"},{"id":"sdl16_q11","sdlNumber":16,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of forearm fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Forearm fractures are the most common fractures of childhood and peak between ages 10 and 14. According to the course, how does the typical fracture pattern change with age in children?","choices":{"A":"Under age 10, physeal separations predominate; over age 10, buckle and greenstick fractures predominate","B":"Under age 10, complete transverse fractures predominate; over age 10, plastic bowing without fracture predominates","C":"Fracture pattern does not change with age, because the distal third is involved in 75% of cases at every age","D":"Under age 10, buckle and greenstick fractures predominate; over age 10, physeal separations become typical","E":"Under age 10, Monteggia injuries predominate; over age 10, Galeazzi injuries predominate as the bone matures"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The course states that forearm fractures make up 40 to 50% of childhood fractures, that the distal third accounts for 75% of forearm fractures, and that the peak age is 10 to 14 because body mass outpaces bone strength. Under age 10 the typical patterns are buckle (torus) and greenstick fractures of the more compliant bone; over age 10 physeal separations appear, and adolescent fractures are linked to the growth spurt. Reversing the age groups contradicts the course. Complete transverse fractures and plastic bowing are not the age patterns listed. The distal-third predominance is true but does not mean the pattern is fixed. Monteggia and Galeazzi injuries are not described as age-defined patterns. A fracture without a plausible history warrants consideration of child abuse.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pediatric bone (more collagen, thicker periosteum) → torus and greenstick fractures; growth plate is the weak link near maturity → physeal (Salter-Harris) injuries. COMLEX: Most forearm fractures follow a FOOSH; consider abuse when the history does not fit.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 6 — Salter-Harris, greenstick, and torus fractures"},{"id":"sdl16_q12","sdlNumber":16,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of forearm fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A Monteggia fracture involves which combination of injuries, and what distinguishes it from a Galeazzi fracture?","choices":{"A":"Monteggia = proximal ulna fracture with radial head dislocation; Galeazzi = distal radius fracture with distal radioulnar joint dislocation","B":"Monteggia = distal radius fracture with volar angulation; Galeazzi = distal ulna fracture with radial head subluxation","C":"Monteggia = midshaft radius fracture with interosseous membrane rupture; Galeazzi = ulna fracture with olecranon avulsion","D":"Monteggia = both-bone forearm fracture with posterior interosseous nerve injury; Galeazzi = isolated radius fracture with DRUJ stability","E":"Monteggia = radial head fracture with ulnar styloid avulsion; Galeazzi = scaphoid fracture with radial styloid impaction"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A Monteggia fracture is a fracture of the proximal ulna combined with dislocation of the radial head at the radiocapitellar joint. A Galeazzi fracture is a fracture of the distal radius combined with disruption of the distal radioulnar joint (DRUJ). A useful mnemonic is \"MUGR\" — Monteggia = Ulna fracture, Galeazzi = Radius fracture. Both are considered unstable injury patterns that typically require surgical fixation. Monteggia fractures are associated with radial nerve (posterior interosseous nerve) injury due to the radial head dislocation. Both injuries can occur in children and adults and should not be confused with isolated radial or ulnar shaft fractures.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Monteggia = proximal Ulna + radial head dislocation; Galeazzi = distal Radius + DRUJ dislocation. Mnemonic: MUG (Monteggia-Ulna) + GR (Galeazzi-Radius). COMLEX: Both require surgery; isolated ulna or radius shaft fractures in adults also usually require fixation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 3 — Differences between adult and pediatric bone stress: buckle, greenstick, and physeal fractures occur in children due to differences in bone flexibility and growth plate vulnerability"},{"id":"sdl16_q13","sdlNumber":16,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of hand and wrist fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which property of the scaphoid bone makes it uniquely prone to avascular necrosis (AVN) following a fracture?","choices":{"A":"The scaphoid is the densest carpal bone, requiring greater forces to fracture and causing more vascular disruption","B":"The scaphoid spans two carpal rows, making it subject to greater shear forces that disrupt all entering blood vessels simultaneously","C":"The scaphoid is entirely covered by articular cartilage except at the tubercle, leaving minimal periosteal vascularity","D":"The scaphoid waist is avascular in all individuals due to developmental absence of nutrient vessels in this region","E":"The scaphoid receives a retrograde blood supply from distal to proximal, so the proximal pole depends on distal vessels"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The scaphoid receives its primary blood supply through branches of the radial artery that enter distally at the dorsal ridge and waist, traveling proximally in a retrograde fashion to supply the proximal pole. A fracture through the scaphoid waist or proximal pole disrupts this blood supply to the proximal fragment, which has no independent arterial supply. This makes the proximal pole susceptible to avascular necrosis if blood flow is not restored. This also explains why proximal pole fractures carry the highest AVN risk and require the longest immobilization. Initial radiographs are often negative because the fracture line is subtle, and MRI is the most sensitive early imaging modality.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Scaphoid = retrograde blood supply → proximal pole AVN risk after waist/proximal fracture. Initial X-rays often negative — MRI is gold standard. COMLEX: Anatomic snuffbox tenderness after FOOSH = presumed scaphoid fracture; treat as fracture even with negative X-ray.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 4 — Carpal tunnel syndrome: acute distal radius fractures can cause median nerve compression and acute carpal tunnel syndrome as a complication"},{"id":"sdl16_q14","sdlNumber":16,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of hand and wrist fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A Boxer's fracture involves a fracture of which bone and at which location?","choices":{"A":"The neck of the fifth metacarpal, typically resulting from striking a hard surface with a clenched fist","B":"The shaft of the fourth metacarpal, resulting from direct axial loading during a punch with the ring finger knuckle","C":"The head of the second metacarpal, resulting from a hyperextension injury of the index MCP joint","D":"The base of the fifth metacarpal, resulting from an inversion injury of the ulnar wrist with associated hamate impaction","E":"The proximal phalanx of the fifth finger, resulting from a valgus stress fracture at the small finger MCP joint"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A Boxer's fracture is a fracture of the neck of the fifth metacarpal, which occurs when a clenched fist strikes a hard surface. The fracture produces volar angulation of the metacarpal head. It is one of the most common metacarpal fractures in adults. In clinical practice, the acceptable angulation for the small finger metacarpal is 50 to 60 degrees (30 to 40 degrees for the ring finger; SDL 25 reading, CDT Orthopedics Ch 11), given the fifth metacarpal's mobility at the carpometacarpal joint, which compensates for some residual angulation. Neurovascular assessment and rotational alignment must be checked. Malrotation, unlike angulation, is not compensated and requires reduction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Boxer's fracture = 5th metacarpal neck, volar angulation; conservative if angulation is within 50-60 degrees (CDT Orthopedics). COMLEX: Always assess rotational alignment — malrotation (scissors deformity with fingers overlapping on flexion) requires reduction regardless of angulation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 4 — Carpal tunnel syndrome: acute distal radius fractures can cause median nerve compression and acute carpal tunnel syndrome as a complication"},{"id":"sdl16_q15","sdlNumber":16,"batch":1,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of hand and wrist fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A young athlete presents with wrist pain after a FOOSH. Radiographs are negative but anatomic snuffbox tenderness is present. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":{"A":"Discharge with a wrist brace and return precautions because negative radiographs effectively exclude scaphoid fracture","B":"Treat as a presumed scaphoid fracture with thumb spica splinting and arrange MRI or CT to confirm the diagnosis","C":"Immobilize and repeat radiographs in 5-7 days as the fracture line will invariably become visible by then on plain film","D":"Refer immediately to hand surgery for urgent ORIF because scaphoid fractures always require surgical fixation","E":"Perform immediate wrist aspiration to evaluate for hemarthrosis, which confirms intraarticular carpal bone injury"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Scaphoid fractures are notoriously missed on initial radiographs — the fracture line can be occult on the first films, which is why repeat X-rays 7-10 days after injury were the traditional approach (Practical Office Orthopedics, SDL 9 reading). However, anatomic snuffbox tenderness after a FOOSH is the classic clinical sign of a scaphoid fracture and should prompt immediate empiric treatment. The standard approach is to apply a thumb spica splint and obtain advanced imaging — MRI is the gold standard for early diagnosis, with CT as an alternative. Simply discharging the patient or waiting for repeat films risks delayed diagnosis, AVN, and nonunion. Not all scaphoid fractures require surgery — undisplaced waist fractures can often be managed conservatively with casting.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Scaphoid = anatomic snuffbox tenderness after FOOSH; X-ray often negative; MRI gold standard. Treat empirically with thumb spica even if X-ray negative. COMLEX: Delayed diagnosis leads to nonunion and AVN of proximal pole — key complication tested on boards.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 4 — Carpal tunnel syndrome: acute distal radius fractures can cause median nerve compression and acute carpal tunnel syndrome as a complication"},{"id":"sdl16_q16","sdlNumber":16,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of humerus fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 77-year-old woman falls onto her right shoulder and holds the arm against her side. There is swelling and tenderness over the proximal humerus, and neurovascular examination is intact. Which radiographic series does the course recommend for this suspected fracture?","choices":{"A":"A shoulder AP and an axillary view, with examination and imaging above and below the suspected fracture site","B":"A humerus AP, lateral, and oblique series, the views listed for midshaft and supracondylar humerus fractures","C":"A single chest radiograph, since the proximal humerus is visible and further views add radiation without benefit","D":"No radiographs before MRI, because MRI is the first-line test for proximal humerus fractures in older adults","E":"Bilateral stress views with weights in both hands, to compare the injured humerus with the uninjured side"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The course lists the radiographs by fracture location: proximal humerus fractures need a shoulder AP and an axillary view, while midshaft and supracondylar fractures need humerus AP, lateral, and oblique views. It adds that, out of caution, the area above and below the suspected fracture should always be examined and imaged. Proximal humerus fractures are most prevalent at ages 73 to 78 and in women. The humerus AP, lateral, and oblique series is the set for midshaft and supracondylar injuries. A chest film is not an adequate shoulder series. MRI is not the first test. Weighted stress views are used for AC joint questions, not for a suspected humerus fracture.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Proximal humerus fracture = 3rd most common fracture in older adults (osteoporosis, FOOSH); surgical neck → axillary nerve. COMLEX: Always obtain two orthogonal views (for the shoulder, AP + axillary) and image the joints above and below.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 3 — axillary view in glenohumeral dislocation"},{"id":"sdl16_q17","sdlNumber":16,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of humerus fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 24-year-old man falls while snowboarding and has a closed, nondisplaced transverse fracture of the midshaft of the left humerus. Wrist and finger extension are intact, sensation is normal, and the skin is intact. According to the course, how are most fractures like this managed?","choices":{"A":"Open reduction and internal fixation within 24 hours, since every midshaft humerus fracture in a young adult is unstable","B":"Nonsurgical management, since most midshaft fractures are nondisplaced; surgery is considered if displaced or comminuted","C":"Exploration of the radial nerve at the spiral groove before splinting, since nerve injury must be excluded surgically","D":"Immediate intramedullary nailing so that early weight bearing can begin, since prolonged splinting causes nonunion","E":"No immobilization, since the humerus is non-weight-bearing and nondisplaced shaft fractures heal without support"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The course states that the majority of proximal and midshaft humerus fractures are nondisplaced and can be managed nonsurgically, with surgery considered for displaced or comminuted fractures. Midshaft fractures make up about 2% of all fractures and are bimodal (men in their 20s, women in their 60s). Routine ORIF for every young adult is not the course approach. With normal wrist and finger extension, there is no radial nerve deficit to explore. Immediate nailing is not indicated for a nondisplaced fracture. Fractures still need protection from displacement until they heal, so leaving the arm unsupported is not appropriate.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Midshaft humerus fracture → radial nerve in the spiral groove (wrist drop, dorsal hand numbness). Nondisplaced → splint/brace; displaced or comminuted → surgical consult. COMLEX: Document radial nerve function before and after any manipulation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 2 — fracture nomenclature: displacement and comminution"},{"id":"sdl16_q18","sdlNumber":16,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of humerus fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 68-year-old man with a non-displaced proximal humerus fracture presents for a 2-week follow-up. He has been in a sling and is asking when he should start moving the shoulder. He has no neurovascular deficits. Which of the following best describes the role of early motion in management?","choices":{"A":"All shoulder motion must be restricted until radiographic healing is complete at 8-12 weeks to prevent displacement","B":"Active range-of-motion exercises against resistance should begin immediately to prevent rotator cuff atrophy","C":"Gentle pendulum exercises begun about 2 weeks after injury reduce shoulder stiffness without harming healing","D":"Surgery should be performed within 2 weeks for any proximal humerus fracture in patients over 65 to ensure optimal recovery","E":"Corticosteroid injection into the glenohumeral joint at 2 weeks reduces inflammation and accelerates return to function"},"correct":"C","explanation":"For non-displaced proximal humerus fractures managed conservatively, early supervised gentle range of motion such as pendulum exercises starts about 2 weeks after the injury (SDL 25 reading, CDT Orthopedics Ch 3). This is critical to prevent shoulder stiffness and adhesive capsulitis, which are significant complications that can cause long-term functional impairment. Pendulum exercises use gravity to provide gentle traction and rotation of the humeral head within the glenoid. Active assisted range-of-motion exercises are added progressively over the following weeks. Prolonged complete immobilization until radiographic healing predictably results in stiffness. Surgical intervention is not indicated for non-displaced fractures in otherwise healthy patients.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Non-displaced proximal humerus fracture → sling + early pendulum exercises starting about 2 weeks after injury → progressive PT. COMLEX: Adhesive capsulitis is a known complication of prolonged immobilization of the shoulder — early motion is key to prevention.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 4 — Frozen shoulder (adhesive capsulitis): prolonged immobilization of the shoulder after proximal humerus fracture is a recognized precipitating cause of secondary adhesive capsulitis"},{"id":"sdl16_q19","sdlNumber":16,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of supracondylar fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 5-year-old girl is brought to the ED after falling from a playground structure. She is guarding her right elbow, which is swollen and markedly bruised. Radiographs show a Gartland Type III supracondylar humerus fracture with complete displacement. Her radial pulse is absent but the hand appears pink and warm with capillary refill of 2 seconds. What is the appropriate next step?","choices":{"A":"Immediate vascular surgery consultation and brachial artery exploration before any fracture reduction is attempted","B":"Urgent orthopedic closed reduction and percutaneous pinning; a perfused but pulseless hand may then be observed","C":"Apply a long arm cast in flexion immediately and reassess vascular status after 6 hours of observation","D":"Discharge home with sling and urgent outpatient orthopedic follow-up within 48 hours for reduction planning","E":"Perform bedside closed reduction under procedural sedation and reassess pulse before orthopedic consultation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A \"pink pulseless hand\" after a supracondylar fracture — where the hand is perfused via collateral circulation (anterior interosseous and ulnar collateral vessels) despite absent radial pulse — is a recognized entity that does not require emergency vascular surgery before fracture reduction. The standard approach is urgent orthopedic consultation for CRPP, after which the radial pulse typically returns as the brachial artery kinking from fracture displacement is relieved. If the hand is cold, pale, and pulseless after reduction, then vascular surgery exploration is indicated for brachial artery injury. This is an emergency; casting without reduction or discharge is inappropriate for a Type III fracture.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pink pulseless hand in supracondylar fracture = urgent CRPP; pulse often returns after reduction. Cold, pale, pulseless hand post-reduction = vascular surgery. COMLEX: Brachial artery is at risk in Type III; compartment syndrome is the feared complication.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 3 — Ulnar nerve entrapment at the elbow: neurovascular anatomy of the elbow is critical for understanding nerve and vessel injury patterns in pediatric supracondylar fractures"},{"id":"sdl16_q20","sdlNumber":16,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of supracondylar fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 4-year-old boy with a Gartland Type I supracondylar fracture has a visible elbow effusion on X-ray but no displacement and normal anterior humeral line. He is otherwise neurovascularly intact. What is the most appropriate management?","choices":{"A":"Closed reduction and percutaneous pinning because all supracondylar fractures in children require operative fixation","B":"Velpeau bandage alone for 6 weeks with no follow-up radiographs because Type I fractures never displace","C":"Observation only with no immobilization because joint effusion without cortical disruption does not require treatment","D":"Open reduction and internal fixation to restore the anterior humeral line and prevent future elbow varus deformity","E":"Long arm posterior splint or cast for about 4 weeks, with close radiographic follow-up to confirm alignment"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Gartland Type I supracondylar fractures are non-displaced (displaced less than 2 mm) with an elbow effusion and normal elbow alignment. They are managed conservatively with a long arm posterior splint or cast; most surgeons immobilize pediatric elbow fractures for about 4 weeks (CDT Orthopedics Ch 12). Surgery is not indicated for Type I fractures in the absence of neurovascular compromise or instability. Close radiographic follow-up is appropriate to ensure the fracture does not displace during healing. Observation without immobilization is inadequate. Cubitus varus (gunstock deformity) is a late complication of malunion, most commonly seen after Type III fractures managed with inadequate reduction, not Type I fractures.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Gartland Type I (non-displaced) = long arm cast about 4 weeks; no surgery. Type II = CRPP vs cast depending on stability; Type III = urgent CRPP. COMLEX: Cubitus varus is the most common late complication of supracondylar fractures treated with inadequate reduction.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 3 — Ulnar nerve entrapment at the elbow: medial epicondyle anatomy and cubital tunnel are directly involved in elbow fracture deformity complications including progressive ulnar nerve neuropathy from cubitus varus"},{"id":"sdl16_q21","sdlNumber":16,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of supracondylar fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 6-year-old boy fell from his bicycle 2 hours ago and was found to have a supracondylar fracture. He is now developing increasing elbow pain out of proportion to the injury, forearm tenseness, and paresthesias in the fingers. Passive finger extension markedly worsens his pain. What is the most likely developing complication?","choices":{"A":"Reflex sympathetic dystrophy of the forearm, which presents with burning pain and is managed with nerve blocks","B":"Heterotopic ossification of the elbow, which presents with progressive stiffness and is managed with NSAIDs","C":"Acute compartment syndrome of the forearm, requiring emergent fasciotomy to prevent permanent ischemic contracture","D":"Osteomyelitis of the distal humerus from a hairline open fracture, managed with IV antibiotics and debridement","E":"Volkmann ischemic contracture that has already occurred and is irreversible without tendon lengthening surgery"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The scenario describes the classic presentation of developing acute compartment syndrome in a child with a supracondylar fracture: pain out of proportion, forearm tenseness, and pain with passive stretch of the fingers (the most reliable early sign). Compartment syndrome after supracondylar fracture most commonly affects the volar forearm compartment and is a surgical emergency requiring fasciotomy. If untreated, ischemia progresses to Volkmann ischemic contracture — a devastating permanent flexion deformity of the wrist and fingers from irreversible muscle fibrosis. This is why supracondylar fractures with neurovascular compromise require emergency consultation rather than elective management.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Compartment syndrome after supracondylar fracture = pain with passive stretch, tenseness, paresthesias → emergent fasciotomy. Delayed = Volkmann contracture. COMLEX: Compartment syndrome and neurovascular compromise are the two emergency ortho consult indications for supracondylar fractures.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 3 — Ulnar nerve entrapment at the elbow: compartment pressure in the forearm can simultaneously compress ulnar, median, and radial nerves — forearm fasciotomy must release all compartments"},{"id":"sdl16_q22","sdlNumber":16,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of clavicular fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 19-year-old male cyclist is thrown over his handlebars and lands on his right shoulder. He presents with right shoulder pain, a visible step-off deformity at the mid-clavicle, and crepitus on palpation. Radiographs reveal a displaced mid-shaft clavicle fracture. His radial pulse is intact and he has no sensory deficits. What is the most appropriate management?","choices":{"A":"Apply a figure-of-eight bandage immediately in the ER to reduce the fracture and hold the shoulder in retraction","B":"Sling for comfort with analgesics and orthopedic referral for evaluation of surgical candidacy given displacement","C":"Immediate ORIF with plate fixation is required for all displaced clavicle fractures in active young males","D":"Admit for IV antibiotics because the crepitus indicates an occult open fracture requiring operative debridement","E":"Perform closed reduction under procedural sedation in the emergency department and cast the shoulder for 6 weeks"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Displaced mid-shaft clavicle fractures in young active patients may benefit from surgical fixation compared to conservative management, with evidence suggesting lower nonunion rates and faster return to function. However, the initial management is a sling for comfort and pain control, followed by orthopedic evaluation to determine whether surgical fixation (ORIF with plate or intramedullary pin) is appropriate given patient factors such as fracture displacement, shortening, activity level, and occupation. A figure-of-eight bandage does not effectively reduce displaced clavicle fractures and adds patient discomfort without benefit. IV antibiotics are not indicated for a closed fracture. Closed reduction cannot be maintained for clavicle fractures in adults.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Displaced mid-shaft clavicle in young active patient = sling + orthopedic referral for surgical consideration; not all displaced fractures require surgery. COMLEX: Referral indications include shortening, complete displacement, comminution, neurovascular compromise, or athlete/cosmetic concern.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 1 — Diagnostic workflow for shoulder pain: clavicle fractures are distinguished from AC joint separation and shoulder dislocation by palpation at the clavicular shaft rather than AC joint or glenohumeral joint"},{"id":"sdl16_q23","sdlNumber":16,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of clavicular fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 16-year-old soccer player sustains a clavicle fracture during a collision. On exam, a sharp fracture fragment is visibly tenting the overlying skin, and the patient has paresthesias along the medial forearm and ulnar two fingers. What neurovascular structures are most at risk in this injury?","choices":{"A":"The axillary nerve and circumflex humeral vessels, which are at risk because of their proximity to the shoulder joint capsule","B":"The radial nerve and radial artery, which pass through the radial groove of the humerus at risk from the clavicular spike","C":"The brachial plexus and subclavian vessels, which run deep to the clavicle between it and the first rib","D":"The phrenic nerve and internal thoracic artery, which run on the anterior surface of the scalene muscles medially","E":"The suprascapular nerve and artery, which cross the superior border of the scapula through the suprascapular notch"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The brachial plexus and subclavian artery and vein run in the costoclavicular space, passing between the clavicle superiorly and the first rib inferiorly. Severely displaced clavicle fractures with skin tenting can impale or compress these structures. Medial forearm and ulnar two-finger paresthesias suggest involvement of the lower trunk or medial cord of the brachial plexus (C8-T1 distribution). Subclavian vein injury can produce significant hemorrhage. These findings represent emergency indications for orthopedic consultation and vascular surgery involvement. The axillary nerve is at risk in proximal humerus fractures and shoulder dislocations, not mid-shaft clavicle fractures.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Clavicle fracture neurovascular risk = brachial plexus and subclavian vessels in costoclavicular space. Skin tenting = imminent open fracture = emergent ortho referral. COMLEX: Lower trunk brachial plexus (C8-T1) = medial forearm and hand paresthesias.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 1 — Diagnostic workflow for shoulder pain: clavicle and shoulder anatomy is foundational to identifying neurovascular structures at risk with proximal upper extremity injuries"},{"id":"sdl16_q24","sdlNumber":16,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of clavicular fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 14-year-old male baseball player falls and fractures his left clavicle. He is right-hand dominant, his left arm is in a sling, and the fracture is not displaced. His parents ask when he can return to baseball. Which factor is most important in determining return-to-sport timeline?","choices":{"A":"His dominant hand is unaffected, so he may return to batting and pitching within 2 weeks of the injury","B":"He must wait exactly 12 months before any upper extremity sport because clavicle fractures have very high reinjury rates","C":"Pain resolution alone at 3 weeks is sufficient to clear him for full return to pitching and throwing sports","D":"Radiographic and clinical evidence of union on follow-up X-rays, with full pain-free motion and restored strength","E":"Surgical fixation is mandatory before return to any contact or overhead sport regardless of fracture displacement"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Return to sport after clavicle fracture requires radiographic evidence of healing combined with full pain-free range of motion and restoration of strength. A typical plan is 4 weeks in a sling and 4-6 weeks out of it before X-rays confirm healing; weight training may start at 3 months, and contact sports wait until the fracture has healed and 3-6 months have passed (Practical Office Orthopedics, SDL 9 reading). Pain resolution alone does not indicate sufficient structural healing. The non-dominant arm being injured does not permit early return for sports requiring two-arm function or physical contact that could displace the fracture. While some athletes with displaced fractures in the dominant arm may be candidates for surgical fixation to expedite return to sport, this is an individualized decision and not mandatory for all athletes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Return to sport after clavicle fracture requires radiographic union and full painless ROM; healing usually confirmed on X-ray 8-10 weeks after injury; weight training at 3 months; contact sport after healing and 3-6 months (Practical Office Orthopedics). COMLEX: Athletes may request surgery to shorten return-to-sport timeline — this is an acceptable indication for consideration of ORIF in high-demand athletes.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 1 — Diagnostic workflow for shoulder pain: sport-specific return-to-play protocols for shoulder girdle injuries require clinical and radiographic assessment before full clearance"},{"id":"sdl16_q25","sdlNumber":16,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of forearm fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"An 8-year-old girl falls off a trampoline onto her outstretched left hand. Radiographs show a buckle (torus) fracture of the distal radius without physeal involvement. She has full neurovascular function and minimal swelling. What is the most appropriate management?","choices":{"A":"Long arm cast with the elbow at 90 degrees for 6-8 weeks because buckle fractures involve the physis and risk growth arrest","B":"Immediate orthopedic referral for percutaneous pinning because all forearm fractures in children under 10 require fixation","C":"Short arm splint or removable wrist splint for 3-4 weeks, with activity restriction and return precautions","D":"No immobilization is needed; buckle fractures are radiographic findings only and do not require treatment","E":"Open reduction under general anesthesia because the periosteum in children is too thick for closed treatment"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Buckle (torus) fractures of the distal radius are inherently stable injuries — the compressed cortex provides structural integrity and the fracture does not significantly displace. They are treated with a short arm splint or removable wrist splint for 3-4 weeks. Long arm casting with elbow flexion is unnecessary as the fracture is stable and the elbow is uninvolved. Surgery is not indicated. Importantly, buckle fractures do not involve the physis (growth plate) and therefore do not carry growth arrest risk — that concern applies to Salter-Harris fractures. A removable splint is increasingly preferred over a cast in stable buckle fractures because it improves patient comfort and hygiene without compromising outcomes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Buckle fracture = stable; short arm splint 3-4 weeks. Greenstick = incomplete cortical disruption, may require gentle manipulation if angulated. Salter-Harris = physeal fracture, growth arrest risk. COMLEX: Distinguish buckle (stable, no physis) from Salter-Harris (physis involved) on boards.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 3 — Differences between adult and pediatric bone stress: buckle fractures are unique to children due to the relative plasticity and porosity of pediatric cortical bone under compressive loads"},{"id":"sdl16_q26","sdlNumber":16,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of forearm fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 32-year-old man presents after a FOOSH. Radiographs show a fracture through the proximal ulna shaft with anterior dislocation of the radial head at the elbow. Which of the following best describes this injury and its required management?","choices":{"A":"Monteggia fracture; requires ORIF of the ulna fracture, which reliably reduces the radial head if performed anatomically","B":"Galeazzi fracture; requires ORIF of the radius with closed reduction of the distal radioulnar joint under fluoroscopy","C":"Essex-Lopresti injury; requires reduction of the radial head and forearm interosseous membrane repair under general anesthesia","D":"Isolated radial head dislocation without associated fracture; requires closed reduction and posterior interosseous nerve exploration","E":"Both-bone forearm fracture with secondary radial head subluxation; requires ORIF of both radius and ulna simultaneously"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A Monteggia fracture is defined as a fracture of the proximal ulna combined with dislocation of the radial head. In children, it is often managed with closed reduction under general anesthesia; in adults, ORIF of the ulna is typically required. The key principle is that anatomic fixation of the ulna fracture is the primary treatment — once the ulna is restored to proper length and alignment, the radial head typically reduces spontaneously. Failure to recognize the radial head dislocation and treating it as an isolated ulna fracture is a classic missed diagnosis. The posterior interosseous nerve (branch of radial nerve) can be injured with the radial head dislocation. A Galeazzi fracture involves the distal radius and DRUJ, not the proximal ulna.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Monteggia = proximal Ulna fracture + radial head dislocation; ORIF ulna reduces radial head. Galeazzi = distal Radius + DRUJ dislocation. COMLEX: Posterior interosseous nerve at risk with Monteggia; always image the elbow with forearm fractures.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 3 — Differences between adult and pediatric bone stress: Monteggia fractures can be treated with closed reduction in children but typically require ORIF in adults because of differences in bone healing capacity"},{"id":"sdl16_q27","sdlNumber":16,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of forearm fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 13-year-old boy presents with a distal forearm fracture after falling during basketball. His parents ask why this age group is so prone to forearm fractures. Which explanation is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"Teenagers participate in higher-risk sports than younger children, so their fracture rate reflects behavioral rather than biological factors","B":"The distal radius physis closes at age 12-14 in males, leaving a biomechanically weak zone that is uniquely vulnerable until full fusion","C":"Bone mineral density reaches its lifetime peak at age 10-14, making the cortex stiff and brittle so that ordinary falls fracture it","D":"The cartilaginous epiphysis at age 13 is softer than adult bone and more susceptible to shear fractures during torsional loading","E":"During the growth spurt, body mass and bone length rise faster than bone strength and mineral content, causing relative skeletal fragility"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The peak incidence of forearm fractures at age 10-14 coincides with the adolescent growth spurt (course slides: \"Body mass vs bone strength\"), during which bone length increases rapidly through endochondral ossification at the growth plates. This rapid longitudinal growth temporarily outpaces the accumulation of bone mineral density, creating a period of relative skeletal fragility sometimes called the \"calcium deficit\" of adolescence. The distal radius metaphysis is particularly vulnerable. While behavioral factors (increased athletic activity) contribute, the biological timing of the peak fracture incidence — tracking closely with peak height velocity — demonstrates a true skeletal vulnerability rather than purely behavioral causation. The physis in this age group is still open and is a site of susceptibility for Salter-Harris fractures. Peak bone mass is not reached until early adulthood, so the cortex is not at its stiffest at this age.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Forearm fracture peak age 10-14 = growth spurt with bone mass lagging bone length growth. COMLEX: Body mass vs bone strength imbalance during puberty explains the peak; the growth plate also remains open and is a Salter-Harris fracture risk zone.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 3 — Differences between adult and pediatric bone stress: the adolescent growth spurt creates a transient period of reduced bone mineral density relative to body mass, increasing forearm fracture risk"},{"id":"sdl16_q28","sdlNumber":16,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of hand and wrist fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 62-year-old man treated in a cast for a distal radius fracture 8 months ago now notices a firm nodule in the palm at the base of the ring finger, and the finger is starting to resist full extension. There is no triggering. He asks whether the fracture caused it. Which statement best reflects the course?","choices":{"A":"This is a flexor tendon rupture caused by fracture fragments, so the finger cannot extend and needs urgent repair","B":"This is Dupuytren contracture, a fibrosis of the palmar fascia; a direct link to the fracture is not established","C":"This is complex regional pain syndrome, which always follows wrist fractures and causes palmar nodules","D":"This is a ganglion cyst from the healing fracture, which transilluminates and resolves after aspiration","E":"This is trigger finger at the A1 pulley, caused by the cast pressing on the flexor sheath during healing"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The course lists Dupuytren contracture among complications discussed with hand and wrist fractures: its pathology is fibrosis of the palmar fascia, there is no direct link to prior wrist or hand fracture, it is suspected in a small fraction of fracture patients, and some fracture patients develop a nodule. A palmar nodule with a ring finger that resists full extension and no triggering fits Dupuytren contracture. A flexor tendon rupture would cause loss of active flexion, not a contracture. CRPS presents with pain, swelling, and vasomotor changes, not a palmar cord, and does not always follow fractures. A ganglion is a fluid-filled cyst, not a fascial contracture. Trigger finger causes catching and locking at the A1 pulley.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Dupuytren contracture = fibroblastic proliferation and thickening of the superficial palmar fascia, typically at the base of the ring and little fingers; most often men over 50 of Northern European descent (FA). COMLEX: Distinguish from trigger finger (catching at the A1 pulley) and tendon rupture (no active motion).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 3 — trigger finger and A1 pulley stenosis"},{"id":"sdl16_q29","sdlNumber":16,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of hand and wrist fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman with osteoporosis falls on her outstretched right hand. She presents with wrist pain, dorsal swelling, and a visible \"dinner fork\" deformity. Radiographs show a distal radius fracture with dorsal angulation and displacement. What is the name of this fracture pattern and what is the deformity caused by?","choices":{"A":"Colles fracture, in which the distal radius fragment angulates and displaces dorsally, creating a dorsal prominence resembling a dinner fork","B":"Smith fracture, in which the distal radius fragment angulates volarly, creating a volar prominence resembling a reversed dinner fork","C":"Barton fracture, in which an intraarticular rim of the distal radius fractures and displaces with the carpus into the wrist joint","D":"Monteggia fracture, in which the distal radius fractures with concomitant disruption of the proximal radioulnar joint","E":"Chauffeur fracture, in which the radial styloid avulses from a direct blow transmitted through the scaphoid into the radial styloid"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A Colles fracture is a distal radius fracture with dorsal angulation and displacement of the distal fragment, producing the classic \"dinner fork\" deformity — a dorsal prominence just proximal to the wrist on lateral profile. It is the most common fracture in osteoporotic women who fall on an outstretched hand. Management depends on displacement and patient factors. Non-displaced or minimally displaced fractures can be treated with a short arm cast; displaced fractures require closed reduction with or without surgical fixation depending on stability. A Smith fracture is the reverse — volar angulation of the distal radius fragment after a fall onto a flexed wrist. A Barton fracture is intraarticular and associated with carpal subluxation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Colles = dorsal angulation = dinner fork = FOOSH in osteoporotic patient. Smith = volar angulation = \"garden spade\" = fall on flexed wrist. COMLEX: Most common fracture in osteoporotic women; always assess for median nerve injury (acute CTS complication).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — Carpal tunnel syndrome: distal radius fractures (Colles) can acutely compress the median nerve within the carpal tunnel, presenting with acute carpal tunnel syndrome requiring urgent decompression"},{"id":"sdl16_q30","sdlNumber":16,"batch":2,"objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Describe the epidemiology, mechanism of injury, presentation, imaging of, and management of hand and wrist fractures.","isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 23-year-old man punches a wall during an argument. He presents with pain, swelling, and tenderness at the ulnar dorsum of the right hand. Radiographs show a fifth metacarpal neck fracture with 35 degrees of volar angulation. He has full extension of all fingers and no rotational deformity on exam. What is the most appropriate management?","choices":{"A":"Immediate surgical ORIF because any angulation greater than 30 degrees in a metacarpal neck fracture mandates fixation","B":"No reduction needed; immobilize in an ulnar gutter splint, since 35 degrees is acceptable for this fracture","C":"Thumb spica cast for 6 weeks because the fifth metacarpal neck fracture is treated identically to a scaphoid fracture","D":"Closed reduction to anatomic (0 degree) alignment, which is mandatory before any splinting of a boxer's fracture","E":"Buddy taping alone is sufficient because metacarpal neck fractures heal without immobilization in young adults"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A Boxer's fracture (5th metacarpal neck fracture with volar angulation) at 35 degrees may be treated conservatively — the acceptable angulation for the small finger is 50 to 60 degrees (SDL 25 reading, CDT Orthopedics Ch 11), because the CMC joint of the small finger provides compensatory mobility. At 35 degrees with no rotational deformity and full MCP extension, this fracture falls within the acceptable range and can be managed with splinting in an ulnar gutter splint. Rotational deformity (scissoring of the fingers with flexion) and pseudoclawing are not acceptable and mandate reduction regardless of angulation. ORIF is reserved for fractures with significant rotational malrotation, irreducible angulation, or intraarticular extension. Buddy taping alone is insufficient for acute displaced fractures, and closed reduction to anatomic alignment is unnecessary when angulation is already within the acceptable range.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Boxer's fracture = 5th metacarpal neck; acceptable angulation up to 50-60 degrees (CDT Orthopedics); rotational deformity is never acceptable. COMLEX: Ulnar gutter splint with the MCP joints flexed (intrinsic plus position) prevents flexion contracture.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — Carpal tunnel syndrome: hand anatomy and the metacarpal/phalangeal relationship is foundational to understanding safe splinting positions that prevent intrinsic muscle contracture during immobilization"}],"17":[{"id":"sdl17_q01","sdlNumber":17,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Develop an understanding of the history of footwear, especially as it pertains to running footwear.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"Which individual is credited with introducing spikes to running footwear in 1852, a design innovation that dramatically improved athletic traction on natural surfaces?","choices":{"A":"Adolf Dassler, who developed differentiated sprint and endurance shoe designs for competitive athletes in the 1920s","B":"Wait Webster, whose vulcanized rubber and leather sole design in 1832 laid the foundation for modern athletic footwear","C":"Bill Bowerman, who collaborated with Phil Knight in the early 1960s to develop what became the Nike brand","D":"Joseph William Foster, whose spiked shoes became the predecessor to what eventually became the Reebok brand","E":"Kihachiro Onitsuka, who founded the post-WWII Japanese athletic shoe company that eventually became Asics"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Joseph William Foster introduced spiked running shoes in 1852, providing athletes with enhanced traction on grass and natural running surfaces. Foster's innovation was a key milestone in the evolution of performance footwear. Adolf Dassler (1920) later differentiated sprint versus endurance shoe designs and famously supplied shoes at the 1936 Berlin Olympics. Wait Webster (1832) introduced rubber soles. Onitsuka (1949) founded what became Asics in post-WWII Japan. Bowerman and Knight (early 1960s) founded Nike.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low direct yield — footwear history is contextual background. COMLEX: Understand how the athletic footwear industry grew from biomechanical innovation rather than evidence-based medicine, setting the stage for marketing-science gaps explored in OBJ 3.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 5 — Achilles tendinopathy: the evolution of running shoe heel design was partly driven by efforts to reduce Achilles tendon loading in distance runners"},{"id":"sdl17_q02","sdlNumber":17,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Develop an understanding of the history of footwear, especially as it pertains to running footwear.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"In the history of running footwear, which company first marketed its shoes as \"scientifically tested,\" representing the earliest attempt to use clinical/scientific language to sell athletic footwear?","choices":{"A":"Nike, founded in the early 1960s by Bill Bowerman and Phil Knight, whose early waffle-sole design was derived from Bowerman's biomechanical experiments at the University of Oregon","B":"Adidas, developed by Adolf Dassler, who differentiated sprint versus endurance shoe construction and provided shoes to athletes at the 1936 Berlin Olympics","C":"Asics, originally Onitsuka Tiger, which incorporated research from Japanese sports scientists after its founding in 1949 in post-WWII Japan","D":"Reebok, whose predecessor company Joseph William Foster pioneered spiked running shoes in 1852 and later adopted modern materials science in their manufacturing processes","E":"New Balance, in 1962, was the first to market running shoes with a \"scientifically tested\" claim, pioneering the commercial use of science-based language in footwear marketing"},"correct":"E","explanation":"New Balance, in 1962, was the first company to market running shoes as \"scientifically tested,\" introducing science-based language to athletic footwear marketing. This milestone is historically significant because it marked the beginning of the conflation of commercial claims with scientific validation — a tension that the course explores at length. Nike (early 1960s) used biomechanical inspiration (waffle sole) but later marketing. Adidas/Dassler and Asics/Onitsuka preceded this with performance designs but not explicit scientific testing claims.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low yield — historical context. COMLEX: The 1962 New Balance claim represents the beginning of a long-standing gap between marketing language and evidence-based footwear science, which directly informs the critical appraisal framework in OBJ 3.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 5 — Achilles tendinopathy: the evolution of running shoe heel design was partly driven by efforts to reduce Achilles tendon loading in distance runners"},{"id":"sdl17_q03","sdlNumber":17,"batch":1,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Develop an understanding of the history of footwear, especially as it pertains to running footwear.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"Adolf Dassler's 1920 contribution to athletic footwear was notable because he was the first to do which of the following in footwear design?","choices":{"A":"Introduce vulcanized rubber outsoles to replace leather soles, dramatically improving durability and traction on wet playing surfaces","B":"Incorporate spikes into running shoes for use on natural grass and track surfaces, improving athlete traction during competitive events","C":"Differentiate shoe construction based on athletic event type, designing separate shoe models optimized for sprinting versus endurance running","D":"Market shoes using claims of scientific testing, setting the commercial precedent for evidence-based language in athletic footwear","E":"Develop the first canvas and rubber shoe marketed for general athletic use, eventually evolving into what became the Keds brand"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Adolf Dassler's 1920 innovation was the differentiation of shoe design by athletic event — creating distinct construction features for sprinting versus endurance running. This was a fundamental departure from the one-shoe-fits-all approach. Dassler's shoes were famously worn at the 1936 Berlin Olympics. Vulcanized rubber (Goodyear, 1892), spikes (Foster, 1852), \"scientifically tested\" claims (New Balance, 1962), and canvas/rubber shoes (Goodyear/Keds, 1892) were all separate milestones by other companies.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low direct yield. COMLEX: The Dassler differentiation principle (sprint vs endurance) foreshadows modern footwear biomechanics debates about whether any shoe design truly optimizes performance for a given runner or running style.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 5 — Achilles tendinopathy: the evolution of running shoe heel design was partly driven by efforts to reduce Achilles tendon loading in distance runners"},{"id":"sdl17_q04","sdlNumber":17,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Identify how footwear does and does not correlate to specific running-based injuries.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A patient who runs 25 miles per week asks whether switching to a more heavily cushioned \"traditional\" running shoe will reduce the impact forces on their joints. Based on the biomechanical evidence, which response is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"Heavily cushioned traditional running shoes actually INCREASE impact forces by lengthening stride length and decreasing stride frequency, producing greater ground reaction forces","B":"Heavily cushioned shoes reduce ground reaction forces by absorbing impact energy through the midsole, directly lowering the mechanical load transmitted to the tibia and knee","C":"Heavily cushioned shoes have a neutral effect on impact forces because the body's kinematics fully adapt to any shoe type, maintaining a constant ground reaction force regardless of cushion","D":"Heavily cushioned shoes reduce impact forces specifically on hard surfaces like asphalt, though they have no effect on softer surfaces where ground reaction forces are inherently lower","E":"Heavily cushioned shoes reduce impact forces only in runners over age 50, because natural shock-absorbing capacity declines with age and supplemental cushioning compensates for this deficit"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Counterintuitively, modern \"traditional\" heavily cushioned running shoes INCREASE impact forces by lengthening stride length, increasing contact times, and decreasing stride frequency — all of which elevate ground reaction forces (GRF). Hard surfaces do NOT increase GRF independently, and \"absorption\" within the shoe does NOT decrease GRF. Furthermore, more cushioning leads to less tactile sensation from the ground, which reduces the body's natural impact-moderating reflexes (Robbins phenomenon), paradoxically increasing injury risk. Natural shock-absorbing capacity does decrease with age, but cushioned shoes do not reliably compensate.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low direct yield but a conceptually important counterintuitive finding. COMLEX: The Robbins phenomenon (cushioning → reduced tactile feedback → larger actual impact) is clinically relevant when counseling runners; more cushion ≠ less injury.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 3 — tibial stress injury from repetitive loading (MTSS, stress fracture), the running injury footwear is meant to prevent"},{"id":"sdl17_q05","sdlNumber":17,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Identify how footwear does and does not correlate to specific running-based injuries.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A runner with a history of recurrent tibial stress fractures asks whether wearing rigid orthotics will help prevent future fractures. Based on the evidence, which statement most accurately reflects what the research shows?","choices":{"A":"Custom rigid orthotics significantly reduce tibial stress fracture recurrence by redistributing ground reaction forces away from the medial tibial cortex during running","B":"Orthotics reduce tibial stress fracture risk primarily in runners with pes planus by correcting excessive pronation that otherwise transmits greater torsional force to the tibial shaft","C":"Orthotics provide equivalent tibial stress protection compared to barefoot running, making them a cost-effective alternative to gait retraining for stress fracture prevention","D":"Orthotics do NOT prevent tibial stress fractures; some evidence suggests semirigid orthotics may actually INCREASE tibial strain rather than reduce it","E":"Semirigid orthotics are protective against tibial stress fractures in military recruits but have no demonstrated benefit in recreational runners with lower training volumes"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Based on Ekenman 2002, orthotics do NOT prevent tibial stress fractures, and there is some evidence that semirigid orthotics may actually INCREASE tibial strain during running — the opposite of their intended effect. Butler 2003 similarly showed no difference in eversion excursion, peak eversion, eversion velocity, loading rate, or leg stiffness between no orthotic, multifunctional orthotic, and rigid orthotic conditions. The evidence for orthotics in injury prevention is mixed, with many trials examining military walking populations rather than runners.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low direct yield. COMLEX: The negative data on orthotics for tibial stress fracture prevention is clinically important for sports medicine practitioners; orthotics may improve subjective comfort but should not be prescribed with expectations of biomechanical injury prevention.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 3 — tibial stress injury from repetitive loading (MTSS, stress fracture), the running injury footwear is meant to prevent"},{"id":"sdl17_q06","sdlNumber":17,"batch":1,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Identify how footwear does and does not correlate to specific running-based injuries.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"Compared to shod running, non-shod (barefoot) running consistently produces which biomechanical pattern according to published studies?","choices":{"A":"Barefoot running reduces knee joint torques but increases ankle and metatarsophalangeal joint loading due to forefoot strike mechanics, resulting in an equivalent overall injury risk profile","B":"Barefoot running lowers ground reaction forces, lowers impact peak, reduces loading rate, decreases stance time, and decreases joint torques compared to running in conventional footwear","C":"Barefoot running reduces ground reaction forces on soft surfaces but produces higher impact forces than cushioned shoes on hard surfaces due to the absence of shock-absorbing midsole material","D":"Barefoot running reduces loading rate and impact peak during initial contact but increases stance time and ground reaction forces during push-off, producing no net biomechanical advantage","E":"Barefoot running produces identical ground reaction forces to cushioned shoes because the body's kinematics fully adapt to any running surface, maintaining homeostatic loading across all footwear conditions"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Studies (Morley 2010, Lieberman 2010, Kerrigan 2009, Squadrone 2009) consistently show that non-shod (barefoot) running lowers ground reaction forces, lowers the impact peak, reduces the loading rate, decreases stance time, and decreases joint torques compared to conventional shod running. This is mechanistically explained by the shorter stride length and anterior foot strike pattern induced by barefoot conditions. However, it is the change in RUNNING STYLE (anterior foot strike, shorter stride) rather than the absence of the shoe itself that drives these benefits — as demonstrated by Connors 2023.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low direct yield. COMLEX: Understanding that barefoot running benefits derive from gait changes, not shoe absence, is clinically useful when counseling patients — running style modification can be achieved in any shoe.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 3 — tibial stress injury from repetitive loading (MTSS, stress fracture), the running injury footwear is meant to prevent"},{"id":"sdl17_q07","sdlNumber":17,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Summarize key principles when it comes to the lack of intersection between commercial marketing and the science of footwear design.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A study by Marti (1989) examined the relationship between running shoe price and lower extremity injury rates after correcting for previous injuries and training load. Which finding most accurately summarizes the results?","choices":{"A":"Runners in the >$95 shoe group were twice as likely to sustain a lower extremity injury compared to those in the <$40 shoe group, suggesting that higher shoe price does not protect against injury","B":"Runners spending between $65 and $95 on shoes had the lowest injury rates, supporting a moderate price range as optimal for injury prevention in recreational distance runners","C":"No significant difference in injury rates was found between high-priced and low-priced shoe groups when training volume and prior injury history were controlled, confirming price as a neutral variable","D":"Higher-priced shoes reduced soft-tissue injuries such as Achilles tendinopathy and plantar fasciitis but showed no protective effect on bony stress fractures of the tibia or metatarsals","E":"The >$95 shoe group showed lower injury rates overall but higher rates of metatarsal stress fractures, suggesting that premium cushioning shifts injury risk from soft-tissue to bone pathology"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Marti (1989) found that runners in the >$95 shoe group were twice as likely to sustain a lower extremity injury compared to those in the <$40 group, after correcting for prior injuries and training regimen. This finding is striking and counterintuitive — more expensive shoes, which often carry greater marketing claims, are associated with MORE injuries, not fewer. This likely relates to the Robbins \"impact moderating behavior\" phenomenon: expensive, heavily cushioned shoes reduce tactile feedback, blunt natural protective reflexes, and thereby increase impact forces and injury risk.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low direct yield but a high-impact teaching point. COMLEX: This is the key data point for counseling runners: expensive shoes are NOT protective, and clinical footwear prescriptions should not be driven by cost. The evidence base for shoe marketing claims is generally weak.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 3 — Challenges of integrative medicine research and regulation: the same evidence-based scrutiny that exposes commercial footwear myths applies to unproven integrative therapies"},{"id":"sdl17_q08","sdlNumber":17,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Summarize key principles when it comes to the lack of intersection between commercial marketing and the science of footwear design.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"The traditional shoe prescription paradigm matches arch type to shoe type: overpronation (pes planus) receives motion control shoes, underpronation (pes cavus) receives cushioned shoes, and neutral motion receives stability shoes. Multiple systematic reviews have concluded that this approach is:","choices":{"A":"Supported by moderate-quality evidence for injury prevention in civilian runners but not validated in military populations, where rapid training volume increases confound shoe prescription outcomes","B":"Simplistic, subjective, and does NOT reduce pain or injury rates — prescribing footwear based on arch type is not evidence-based according to available systematic reviews","C":"Effective for reducing knee and hip pain in runners with pes planus through motion control correction, though evidence for pes cavus cushioning and neutral stability shoes remains limited","D":"Validated in the short term for pain reduction during training, but not effective for long-term injury prevention beyond the first 12 weeks of a new running program","E":"Evidence-based for runners with BMI greater than 25, in whom arch instability and excess body weight compound to increase injury risk, but not in normal-weight recreational runners"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Multiple systematic reviews (Ryan 2009, van Gent 2007, Knapik 2010) have concluded that prescribing footwear based on arch type is simplistic, subjective, and does NOT reduce pain or injury. Richards 2008 specifically states: \"Prescription of this shoe type is not evidence based.\" Relph 2022 further confirmed: \"There is no evidence that prescribing footwear based on foot type reduces running-related lower-limb injuries in adults.\" This represents one of the most important marketing-versus-science disconnects in sports medicine — a widely practiced paradigm that lacks supporting evidence.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low direct yield. COMLEX: Understanding that the arch-type → shoe-type paradigm is not evidence-based is directly applicable to clinical practice; physicians should not simply match arch type to shoe type when counseling runners.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 3 — Challenges of integrative medicine research and regulation: the same evidence-based scrutiny that exposes commercial footwear myths applies to unproven integrative therapies"},{"id":"sdl17_q09","sdlNumber":17,"batch":1,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Summarize key principles when it comes to the lack of intersection between commercial marketing and the science of footwear design.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"Hoffmann (1905) conducted anatomical observations comparing barefoot populations to shoe-wearing populations. Which finding from his work directly supports the concept that conventional shoe design negatively alters foot structure and function?","choices":{"A":"Shoe-wearing populations develop stronger plantar intrinsic muscles compared to barefoot populations, as the mechanical resistance of the shoe sole provides passive resistance training during the gait cycle","B":"Shoe-wearing populations demonstrate lower rates of hallux valgus deformity because the medial counter of the shoe provides structural support for the great toe during push-off mechanics","C":"Shoe wearing does not alter foot anatomy in populations that begin wearing shoes after age five, as the foot skeleton has completed the majority of its ossification and remodeling by this age","D":"Shoe-wearing populations in both industrial and non-industrial societies develop identical foot anatomy, suggesting that footwear style rather than shoe wearing per se determines anatomical outcome","E":"Shoe wearing after the first year of life causes progressive narrowing of the anterior foot and diminution of the range of motion of the phalanges, tarsal, and ankle joints compared to barefoot populations"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Hoffmann (1905) observed that barefoot and shoe-wearing populations have similar foot anatomy until shoes that compress and splint the foot are introduced — typically around the end of the first year of life. After this, shoe wearers develop progressive narrowing of the anterior portion of the foot and diminution of the range of motion of the phalanges, tarsal, and ankle joints. He also noted that the great toe maintains foot adduction and provides leverage — functions that are compromised by narrow-toed shoes. This historical data supports the broader SDL theme that conventional shoe design may harm rather than help natural foot function.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low yield — historical data. COMLEX: Hoffmann's findings support recommending toe-box-wide shoes for patients, particularly children, to avoid acquired foot deformities. Great toe function (adduction, push-off leverage) is mechanically important and worth preserving.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 3 — Challenges of integrative medicine research and regulation: the same evidence-based scrutiny that exposes commercial footwear myths applies to unproven integrative therapies"},{"id":"sdl17_q10","sdlNumber":17,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Begin to develop a paradigm for running footwear prescription.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"When counseling a competitive runner about shoe selection, the evidence supports using shoe WEIGHT as a meaningful prescription variable. Which relationship correctly describes the physiological impact of shoe weight on running economy?","choices":{"A":"Each 100 grams of additional shoe weight increases oxygen consumption by approximately 3 to 5%, making shoe weight the single most important determinant of running performance and economy","B":"Each 100 grams of additional shoe weight increases oxygen consumption (VO2) by approximately 0.7 to 1%, directly reducing running economy and metabolic efficiency","C":"Shoe weight does not independently affect oxygen consumption; only midsole stiffness and heel-to-toe drop alter metabolic efficiency, with heavier shoes being offset by greater energy return per stride","D":"Shoe weight increases oxygen consumption only when carried at the foot distal to the ankle; weight distributed proximally in the midsole and heel has no measurable effect on metabolic cost","E":"The relationship between shoe weight and oxygen consumption is nonlinear, with shoes under 200 grams having no metabolic effect and the cost increasing exponentially only above this threshold"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Shoe weight is one of the evidence-based prescription variables in footwear selection. Each 100-gram increase in shoe weight results in approximately a 0.7 to 1% increase in oxygen consumption (VO2), meaningfully reducing running economy. This is why lighter shoes — all else being equal — are metabolically advantageous. The other evidence-based prescription variables include stack height, heel-to-toe drop, flexibility, toe box width, and heel cup width. These are contrasted with non-evidence-based marketing features (price, brand claims, motion control systems) that do not reliably correlate with performance or injury prevention.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low direct yield. COMLEX: The 100 gm / 0.7-1% VO2 relationship is the only specific quantitative fact worth memorizing from the prescription paradigm section — relevant when advising competitive runners or when evaluating claims about racing flats versus training shoes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 3 — Physical therapy prescription components: evidence-based footwear prescription mirrors PT prescription by emphasizing individualized, functional goals over generic protocols"},{"id":"sdl17_q11","sdlNumber":17,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Begin to develop a paradigm for running footwear prescription.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"According to the lever arm phenomena described by Hamill (1992), a running shoe with a softer midsole compared to one with a firmer midsole produces which biomechanical consequence for the foot and ankle?","choices":{"A":"A softer midsole produces greater pronation magnitude and a shorter time to reach maximum pronation compared to a firmer midsole, so cushioning can increase rather than control rearfoot motion","B":"A softer midsole reduces pronation magnitude by absorbing rearfoot eversion forces before they can be transmitted to the subtalar joint, producing a more neutral midfoot position at heel strike","C":"A softer midsole produces identical pronation magnitude to a firm midsole but extends the time to maximum pronation, allowing muscles more time to eccentrically decelerate subtalar motion","D":"A softer midsole shifts loading from the rearfoot to the forefoot, decreasing heel strike pronation while increasing metatarsophalangeal dorsiflexion torque during push-off","E":"A softer midsole increases pronation only in runners with pes planus; runners with neutral arch mechanics demonstrate no difference in pronation between soft and firm midsole conditions"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Hamill (1992) described the lever arm phenomenon: a softer midsole collapses under load more readily, allowing greater rearfoot eversion (pronation) and reducing the time to reach maximum pronation. Additionally, a wider sole base creates a longer lever arm, increasing the demand on the foot to overcome pronation forces. Counterintuitively, motion control shoes with dense thermoplastic medial posts often have WIDER soles — which actually increases the pronation-inducing lever arm rather than reducing it. This is another example of a shoe feature that is mechanistically opposite to its marketing claim.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low yield. COMLEX: The lever arm phenomenon explains why wide, soft-soled motion control shoes may worsen rather than correct pronation — a mechanistic argument against the arch-type prescription paradigm covered in OBJ 3.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 3 — Physical therapy prescription components: evidence-based footwear prescription mirrors PT prescription by emphasizing individualized, functional goals over generic protocols"},{"id":"sdl17_q12","sdlNumber":17,"batch":1,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Begin to develop a paradigm for running footwear prescription.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"The evidence-based \"take home points\" for running shoe prescription recommend that patients prioritize which set of shoe characteristics when selecting footwear?","choices":{"A":"Motion control features, arch support, and cushioned heel collar — selected based on static arch height assessment using the wet footprint test or a commercially available pressure plate system","B":"Heel-to-toe drop greater than 10 mm, a wide toe box, and a reinforced medial post — prioritizing rearfoot stability for runners who log more than 30 miles per week","C":"Maximum cushioning with a thick midsole, a neutral motion category designation, and replacement on a fixed schedule of every 250 to 500 miles regardless of shoe condition","D":"Light weight, flexibility, and a thin profile — chosen for immediate comfort upon fitting while ignoring cost, brand features, and the marketing claims made about the shoe","E":"High cost within the runner's budget, a brand with independent biomechanics laboratory testing, and a heel cup width matched to the runner's measured calcaneal width"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The evidence-based shoe-buying recommendations from the course emphasize selecting shoes that are light, flexible, and thin, with the most important practical criterion being that the shoe is immediately comfortable when tried on. Patients should get properly sized (feet change with age and weight), try multiple pairs, and buy a new shoe when the current one is no longer comfortable — not on a fixed mileage or time schedule. The features to IGNORE include cost, brand marketing, motion control elements, bells, and whistles. This directly contradicts most commercial shoe retail advice and represents the core clinical take-away of OBJ 4.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low direct yield. COMLEX: The prescription framework (light, flexible, thin, immediately comfortable) is the answer to \"how do you prescribe running shoes?\" on clinical vignettes. Price, brand, and arch type are explicitly NOT evidence-based selection criteria.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 3 — Physical therapy prescription components: evidence-based footwear prescription mirrors PT prescription by emphasizing individualized, functional goals over generic protocols"},{"id":"sdl17_q13","sdlNumber":17,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Develop an understanding of the history of footwear, especially as it pertains to running footwear.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A sports medicine lecture traces how several modern running shoe brands began. The lecturer describes a company founded in post-World War II Japan in 1949. Which modern brand did this company become?","choices":{"A":"Nike, founded from Onitsuka's Japanese factory after Bill Bowerman and Phil Knight bought it in 1949","B":"Converse, whose Chuck Taylor shoes were first made in Japan and worn by the 1936 US basketball team","C":"Asics, which grew out of the Onitsuka company founded in post-World War II Japan in the year 1949","D":"Reebok, which grew from Joseph William Foster's Japanese spike factory opened after the war","E":"Keds, which Goodyear launched in Japan in 1949 after developing rubber vulcanization there"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course timeline of running shoes lists 1949 (Onitsuka) in post-World War II Japan as the origin of Asics. The same timeline places Bill Bowerman and Phil Knight's Nike in the early 1960s in the United States, not in 1949 Japan. Converse Chuck Taylors were worn by the US basketball team in 1936, and they did not come from Japan. Joseph William Foster introduced spikes in 1852 in England, the line that later became Reebok. Goodyear's vulcanization and Keds date to 1892, not post-war Japan.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Running shoe timeline: 1832 Webster rubber soles; 1852 Foster spikes; 1892 Goodyear vulcanization (Keds); 1920 Dassler sprint vs endurance shoes; 1936 Berlin Olympics; 1949 Onitsuka (Asics); 1962 New Balance \"scientifically tested\"; early 1960s Nike. COMLEX: Marketing language entered footwear long before outcome evidence did.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 3 — commercial marketing versus the science of footwear design"},{"id":"sdl17_q14","sdlNumber":17,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Develop an understanding of the history of footwear, especially as it pertains to running footwear.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A sports medicine attending notes that Adolf Dassler's shoes were worn at the 1936 Berlin Olympics. Dassler's company was the direct predecessor to which modern athletic brand?","choices":{"A":"Adidas — Adolf Dassler's company, operating under the Dassler Brothers brand before the split, evolved into Adidas (from \"Adi Dassler\") after a post-WWII family dispute","B":"Nike — because Phil Knight and Bill Bowerman initially imported Dassler shoes for distribution in the United States before founding their own brand in the early 1960s","C":"New Balance — because the German engineering approach developed by Dassler was adopted by New Balance when it introduced its \"scientifically tested\" shoe line in 1962","D":"Puma — which was the sole successor company to the original Dassler Brothers Shoe Factory, retaining the full patent portfolio and manufacturing facilities after the family split","E":"Asics — because Kihachiro Onitsuka licensed the Dassler spiked shoe design when founding his Japanese athletic company in 1949 after World War II"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Adolf Dassler's company evolved into Adidas (named from his nickname \"Adi\" and surname \"Dassler\"). After a post-WWII family dispute, his brother Rudolf founded Puma — so both Adidas and Puma share the Dassler Brothers lineage. For this course, Dassler's 1920 innovation (sprint vs endurance shoe differentiation) and his presence at the 1936 Berlin Olympics are the testable historical facts. Nike was founded independently by Bowerman and Knight. New Balance, Asics, and the others have separate founding histories with no direct Dassler connection.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low yield — brand history. COMLEX: The Dassler → Adidas/Puma origin story is a contextual detail. The clinically relevant lesson is that the earliest \"performance differentiation\" in footwear was driven by athletic intuition, not randomized evidence.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 5 — Achilles tendinopathy: Dassler's sprint vs endurance differentiation foreshadows modern heel-to-toe drop debates, as sprint shoes are zero-drop and endurance shoes historically had a raised heel — each influencing Achilles tendon loading differently"},{"id":"sdl17_q15","sdlNumber":17,"batch":2,"objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Develop an understanding of the history of footwear, especially as it pertains to running footwear.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A sports history question on an exam presents the following timeline of footwear milestones. Which item in this list is placed in the INCORRECT year?","choices":{"A":"1852 — Joseph William Foster introduced spikes to running footwear, improving traction for competitive runners on natural grass and cinder track surfaces","B":"1949 — Kihachiro Onitsuka founded the company in post-WWII Japan that eventually became Asics, producing athletic footwear for Japanese Olympic development programs","C":"1920 — Adolf Dassler began differentiating shoe construction by athletic event type, creating separate designs for sprint and endurance running athletes","D":"1962 — New Balance became the first athletic shoe company to explicitly market its products using the phrase \"scientifically tested\" in its commercial advertising","E":"1852 — Wait Webster introduced rubber soles combined with leather uppers, giving athletic footwear a more flexible and durable sole for running and walking"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Wait Webster's rubber-and-leather sole dates to 1832, not 1852; 1852 is Foster's spikes. The 1852 milestone belongs to Joseph William Foster and the introduction of spikes. Goodyear's contribution in 1892 was vulcanization, which was used in the production of Keds. All other dates in the answer choices are correct as stated. This question requires distinguishing between the 1832 rubber sole milestone (Webster) and the 1852 spike milestone (Foster), which are commonly confused.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low yield. COMLEX: Historical chronology for footwear is background context. Remember the key dates: 1832 (rubber soles/Webster), 1852 (spikes/Foster), 1892 (vulcanization/Goodyear/Keds), 1920 (Dassler/sprint vs endurance), 1962 (New Balance/scientifically tested).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 5 — Achilles tendinopathy: the 1892 vulcanization milestone enabled mass-produced shoe soles, beginning the era of standardized heel heights that altered Achilles tendon resting length across populations who adopted footwear"},{"id":"sdl17_q16","sdlNumber":17,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Identify how footwear does and does not correlate to specific running-based injuries.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 32-year-old recreational runner presents with medial tibial pain after increasing her mileage. She recently purchased expensive motion control shoes with maximum cushioning on the advice of a running store specialist. Which statement from the published evidence most directly challenges the assumption that her shoe choice will reduce her injury risk?","choices":{"A":"Motion control shoes with rigid thermoplastic medial posts increase eversion control at the subtalar joint, reducing pronation-related tibial torsional forces and thereby lowering medial tibial stress","B":"More cushioning reduces tactile ground sensation, which blunts the body's natural impact-moderating reflexes, paradoxically increasing actual impact forces despite the perception of softer contact","C":"Highly cushioned shoes improve injury outcomes in runners with pes cavus who underpronate, but offer no additional protection for neutral runners or those with pes planus foot type","D":"Maximum cushioning reduces the vertical loading rate during heel strike, which is the primary biomechanical determinant of medial tibial stress syndrome and tibial stress fracture risk","E":"Cushioned shoes provide equivalent impact protection to barefoot running on soft surfaces, making them the preferred option for runners transitioning from high-mileage training blocks"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The Robbins phenomenon (1987-1997) is the key mechanism: more cushioning → less tactile sensation from the plantar surface → reduced natural absorption reflexes → larger actual impact forces. The runner's body typically modulates impact based on sensory feedback from the foot; when this feedback is muffled by thick cushioning, the brain cannot appropriately trigger protective muscle co-contraction, and ground reaction forces increase. Additionally, Marti 1989 showed that shoes costing >$95 were associated with TWICE the lower extremity injury rate compared to shoes under $40, challenging the assumption that premium cushioned shoes reduce injury risk.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low direct yield. COMLEX: The Robbins impact-moderating behavior concept is the core mechanistic explanation for why more cushion ≠ less injury. This is the most clinically applicable concept from OBJ 2 when counseling runners who present with overuse injuries.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 1 — Medial tibial stress syndrome: MTSS involves repetitive tibial loading; the evidence that cushioned shoes do not reduce impact forces directly explains why shoe type has failed to demonstrate preventive efficacy for this condition"},{"id":"sdl17_q17","sdlNumber":17,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Identify how footwear does and does not correlate to specific running-based injuries.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 38-year-old recreational runner with knee pain wants to move all of his training from asphalt roads to a soft dirt trail because he believes hard surfaces raise the ground reaction forces that reach his joints. Which statement from the course best addresses his belief?","choices":{"A":"Hard surfaces raise ground reaction forces in direct proportion to their stiffness, so a softer trail will lower joint loading","B":"Soft surfaces absorb impact and lower ground reaction force, which is why cushioned shoes also lower joint loading","C":"Ground reaction forces depend on the running surface exclusively, so his stride length and shoes will not affect impact","D":"Hard surfaces do not increase ground reaction force, because the runner's kinematics adapt to the surface on landing","E":"Hard surfaces increase muscle damage and hemolysis, so his knee pain reflects microtrauma from road running"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The course's review of cushioning and impact states that hard surfaces do not increase ground reaction force, that absorption does not decrease it, and that to some degree a runner's kinematics adapt to accommodate a change in running surface. It also notes that increased stride length on its own raises ground reaction force, and that more cushioning reduces tactile sensation and leads to larger impact force. Saying hard surfaces raise force in proportion to stiffness, or that soft surfaces and cushioned shoes lower force by absorption, contradicts these points. Surface is not the only determinant; stride length and footwear matter. The course notes no difference in muscle damage or hemolysis with cushioning.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ground reaction force rises with longer stride and lower cadence; runners stiffen or soften the leg to match the surface. Natural shock-absorbing capacity decreases with age. COMLEX: Address training load and running mechanics before surface or shoe changes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 4 — shoe factors that are useful in prescription (weight, stack, drop, flexibility)"},{"id":"sdl17_q18","sdlNumber":17,"batch":2,"objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Identify how footwear does and does not correlate to specific running-based injuries.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A research team studies the biomechanics of minimalist footwear use and finds that runners transitioning to minimalist shoes demonstrate lower vertical loading rate, higher cadence, less energy absorption at the knee, and more energy absorption at the ankle. The researchers conclude that these changes are most directly attributable to:","choices":{"A":"The reduction in shoe weight associated with minimalist footwear, which decreases the metabolic cost of swing phase and reflexively produces a shorter stride and more anterior foot contact angle","B":"The elimination of heel cushioning in minimalist shoes, which prevents heel strike by making heel contact painful, thereby mechanically forcing an anterior foot strike pattern in all runners","C":"The change in running style induced by minimalist shoes (anterior foot strike, shorter stride) rather than the physical properties of the shoe itself, such as its weight, cushioning, or drop","D":"The zero or near-zero heel-to-toe drop of minimalist shoes, which anatomically positions the foot in plantarflexion at heel contact and biomechanically requires a forefoot or midfoot strike pattern","E":"The increased plantar tactile feedback from minimalist shoes, which amplifies afferent signals from ground contact and triggers spinal reflex arcs that automatically shorten stride length"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Connors (2023) explicitly concluded that improvements seen with minimalist footwear — lower vertical loading rate, higher cadence, less knee energy absorption, more ankle energy absorption, and improvement in prior injuries — result from the CHANGE IN RUNNING STYLE (anterior/forefoot foot strike, higher cadence, shorter stride) rather than any inherent property of the shoe itself. This is a clinically critical distinction: running gait retraining, regardless of shoe type, can replicate these benefits. Minimalist shoes also increase metatarsophalangeal and ankle joint loading compared to conventional shoes (Sun 2020), so they carry trade-offs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low direct yield. COMLEX: The Connors 2023 conclusion is the capstone finding for OBJ 2 — it unifies the data by showing that footwear effects on biomechanics are mediated through gait changes. Gait retraining is therefore more fundamental than shoe selection for injury prevention.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 5 — Achilles tendinopathy: the increased ankle energy absorption with anterior foot strike induced by minimalist shoes increases eccentric Achilles loading, which explains the higher rates of Achilles tendinopathy reported in runners who transition too rapidly to minimalist footwear"},{"id":"sdl17_q19","sdlNumber":17,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Summarize key principles when it comes to the lack of intersection between commercial marketing and the science of footwear design.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 45-year-old marathon runner visits a sports medicine clinic and reports that a running store employee recommended replacing his shoes every 6 months or after 250-500 miles, citing \"complete cushioning breakdown.\" When asked if this recommendation is evidence-based, the physician should respond that:","choices":{"A":"The 250-500 mile replacement guideline is not outcome-based; EVA midsole cushioning loses about 40% of its properties after 400 miles, but replacement timing should be guided by comfort rather than a fixed mileage schedule","B":"The 250-500 mile guideline is supported by prospective trials showing that runners who replace shoes on this schedule have significantly lower rates of tibial stress fractures and Achilles tendinopathy","C":"The 250-500 mile guideline is appropriate for runners over age 40 because natural shock-absorbing capacity decreases with age, making midsole cushioning degradation more biomechanically consequential in older runners","D":"The guideline is evidence-based for heavy runners (BMI greater than 27) in whom midsole compression is accelerated, but lacks evidence for normal-weight runners who can use shoes until sole tread wear is visible","E":"The 500-mile upper limit is based on controlled wear-testing data showing complete EVA foam failure at this threshold, making continued use unsafe due to uneven surface contact and increased lateral ankle sprain risk"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The 250-500 mile replacement recommendation is NOT outcome-based. The course notes that EVA foam cushioning loses approximately 40% of its cushioning properties after 400 miles — but this has not been translated into a clinical trial demonstrating that replacement at this threshold reduces injury. Moreover, the \"right\" replacement interval is highly variable based on runner weight, frequency of use, running conditions, and shoe storage. The evidence-based recommendation is simply: replace shoes when they are no longer comfortable. This is another example of a commercially convenient guideline that lacks scientific support.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low yield. COMLEX: The 40% EVA cushioning loss figure at 400 miles is the only specific number worth knowing. Clinical pearl: advise patients to replace based on comfort, not mileage — a simple, evidence-consistent, and cost-conscious recommendation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 3 — Integrative medicine research challenges: fixed-interval shoe replacement guidelines parallel unproven integrative medicine protocols — both are commercially promoted practices that lack patient-oriented outcome evidence but persist due to industry interests"},{"id":"sdl17_q20","sdlNumber":17,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Summarize key principles when it comes to the lack of intersection between commercial marketing and the science of footwear design.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A study (Stacoff 2001) examined whether shoe type — specifically motion control versus cushioning versus stability designs — could reliably control tibiocalcaneal kinematics during running. The most accurate summary of this study's finding is:","choices":{"A":"Motion control shoes significantly reduce tibiocalcaneal eversion in overpronators when compared to neutral shoes, supporting their prescription in runners with pes planus and rearfoot valgus alignment","B":"Stability shoes reduce tibiocalcaneal eversion in neutral runners but have no effect in overpronators, suggesting that motion control features only benefit runners who already demonstrate moderate arch integrity","C":"Shoe type reliably controls tibiocalcaneal kinematics in runners who weigh less than 75 kg, but not in heavier runners in whom foot mass and ground reaction forces overwhelm the shoe's mechanical resistance","D":"Tibiocalcaneal kinematics during running are individually unique and not reliably controlled by shoe type, undermining the biomechanical rationale for motion control shoe prescription","E":"Motion control shoes reduce the loading rate of tibiocalcaneal eversion but do not alter the total range of pronation, providing partial biomechanical benefit without complete kinematics correction"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Stacoff (2001) found that tibiocalcaneal kinematics during running are individually unique — i.e., each runner's pronation pattern is specific to them and NOT predictably controlled by shoe type. This finding is foundational to the marketing-versus-science argument: the entire motion control shoe category is built on the premise that shoe design can reliably correct rearfoot mechanics, but Stacoff's data shows this is not the case. Combined with the Ryan/Richards/Knapik systematic review data showing no injury reduction from arch-based prescription, this study represents a mechanistic basis for skepticism about motion control shoes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low direct yield. COMLEX: Stacoff 2001 provides the mechanistic explanation for why motion control shoes don't work as marketed — individual kinematics variability means no shoe type can reliably control rearfoot motion for all runners. This supports a comfort-first prescription approach.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 3 — Integrative medicine research challenges: the failure of shoe design to predictably control tibiocalcaneal kinematics parallels the failure of many physical integrative therapies to produce consistent biomechanical changes — individualized responses undermine group-based intervention protocols"},{"id":"sdl17_q21","sdlNumber":17,"batch":2,"objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Summarize key principles when it comes to the lack of intersection between commercial marketing and the science of footwear design.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A study of 2,300 children aged 4-13 years compared the rate of static anatomic changes of the foot by footwear type. Closed-toe shoes were associated with a 13.2% rate, sandals 6.0%, slippers 8.2%, and unshod children 2.8%. Which conclusion does this data most directly support regarding footwear in children?","choices":{"A":"All footwear types produce equivalent rates of static anatomic change compared to the unshod condition, suggesting that walking itself, rather than shoe type, is the primary driver of pediatric foot anatomy changes","B":"Sandals and slippers produce similar rates of anatomic change because they provide insufficient medial arch support, whereas closed-toe shoes with built-in arch structures paradoxically reduce change in older children","C":"Closed-toe shoes are associated with the highest rate of static anatomic change and going unshod with the lowest, suggesting that wearing shoes, not going without them, may contribute to anatomic changes of the foot","D":"The 13.2% rate of anatomic change in closed-toe shoe wearers reflects selection bias, as children with pre-existing foot problems are more likely to be prescribed closed-toe orthopedic footwear by their physicians","E":"Children who wear sandals have lower rates of anatomic change than slipper wearers because sandal straps provide dynamic lateral containment of the foot, reducing hallux valgus drift during the push-off phase"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course slide (SDL 17 p12, Rao 1992) asks whether bulky shoes cause static anatomic changes in children and answers \"Maybe\": in 2,300 children aged 4-13, the rate was 13.2% with closed-toe shoes, 8.2% with slippers, 6.0% with sandals and 2.8% unshod. The gradient fits a possible contribution of shoe wearing, which is why the slide stops at \"maybe\" rather than proving cause. The slides that follow (p13-p14, Hoffmann 1905) show the same direction: foot impressions of children after a few weeks of shoe-wearing show narrowing of the front of the foot and a change in the long axis of the great toe, and once footwear that compresses and splints the foot is used, there is progressive narrowing of the anterior foot and reduced range of motion of the phalanges, tarsal and ankle joints. The data shows no equal rates across footwear, does not test arch support or sandal straps, and gives no information on prescribing patterns.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low yield. COMLEX: Rao 1992 (course slide p12): static anatomic foot changes in children were most common with closed-toe shoes (13.2%) and least common unshod (2.8%); the course answer to \"do bulky shoes cause them?\" is \"maybe\". Hoffmann 1905: shoe-wearing narrows the forefoot and reduces range of motion of the phalanges, tarsal and ankle joints.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 3 — Integrative medicine research challenges: the pediatric foot deformity data illustrates how a cultural norm (children wearing closed-toe shoes) persists despite evidence of harm — the same uncritical acceptance seen with many unregulated integrative health products"},{"id":"sdl17_q22","sdlNumber":17,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Begin to develop a paradigm for running footwear prescription.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A sports medicine physician is developing an individualized running shoe prescription for a 28-year-old male recreational runner with no prior injuries. He uses an evidence-based shoe prescription framework. Which combination of shoe characteristics does the evidence-based framework identify as the most clinically relevant for this prescription?","choices":{"A":"Medial post stiffness, arch height index, rearfoot flare angle, and pronation control category — selected based on a dynamic foot pressure analysis and static arch height measurement in the clinic","B":"Shoe weight, stack height, heel-to-toe drop, flexibility, toe box width, and heel cup width — with comfort upon immediate fitting as the overriding clinical criterion for the final selection","C":"Brand reputation, independent biomechanical testing certification, outsole durometer rating, and midsole foam density — as these are the construction variables most strongly correlated with injury prevention","D":"Price point within the runner's budget, outsole tread depth, upper breathability rating, and motion control category — prioritizing long-term durability and cost-effectiveness over immediate comfort","E":"Sole stiffness in the sagittal plane, heel counter height, toe spring angle, and midsole thickness at the metatarsal heads — selected based on the runner's habitual foot strike pattern as assessed by treadmill video analysis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The evidence-based prescription framework from the course identifies six useful shoe characteristics: weight, stack height, heel-to-toe drop, flexibility, toe box width, and heel cup width. Immediate comfort upon fitting is the most practical overriding criterion. The framework explicitly rejects arch-type matching, price-based selection, and brand-specific claims as non-evidence-based prescription methods. The paradigm also considers the interaction between congenital and acquired foot anatomy, foot function, and running gait — all of which are individually unique and cannot be addressed by a standardized shoe prescription protocol.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low yield. COMLEX: Memorize the six evidence-based shoe variables (weight, stack height, heel-to-toe drop, flexibility, toe box width, heel cup width) and the take-home rule: light, flexible, thin, immediately comfortable — ignore cost and marketing.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 3 — Physical therapy prescription components: the shoe prescription framework parallels PT prescription — both require individualized goal setting, specific measurable parameters (drop, weight, flexibility), and patient-centered comfort outcomes rather than generic protocol adherence"},{"id":"sdl17_q23","sdlNumber":17,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Begin to develop a paradigm for running footwear prescription.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"During a footwear consultation, a physician lists heel-to-toe drop among the useful factors when choosing a running shoe. A medical student asks for clarification. Which description correctly defines heel-to-toe drop?","choices":{"A":"Heel-to-toe drop is the total midsole stack height at the midfoot, measured in millimeters at the navicular level; higher drop values indicate greater overall cushioning and are associated with more posterior foot strike patterns","B":"Heel-to-toe drop refers to the degree of heel counter rigidity relative to toe box flexibility; a high-drop shoe has a stiff heel and a compliant forefoot, promoting rearfoot stability during heel strike","C":"Heel-to-toe drop measures the vertical distance between the ground and the ball of the foot during midstance; higher drop values indicate greater arch height and are used to classify shoe type as motion control, stability, or neutral","D":"Heel-to-toe drop is the rearfoot-to-forefoot weight distribution ratio measured by force plate analysis; a 12 mm drop corresponds to approximately 60% rearfoot loading and 40% forefoot loading during heel strike","E":"Heel-to-toe drop is the difference between rearfoot stack height and forefoot stack height; a shoe with a 24 mm rearfoot and a 12 mm forefoot has a 12 mm drop, and it is listed with weight and stack height as useful shoe factors"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Heel-to-toe drop (the \"drop\" of a shoe) is the difference between the rearfoot and forefoot stack heights. The course slide (SDL 17 p40) gives the example: 24 mm rearfoot minus 12 mm forefoot equals a 12 mm drop. The course lists weight, stack height, heel-to-toe drop and flexibility as the factors that are useful in choosing a running shoe (p41). Drop is not a midfoot stack height, a measure of heel counter rigidity, an arch height or a force-plate weight distribution. Related course points on minimalist shoes: they are associated with an anterior foot strike, and the effect comes from the change in running style rather than the shoe itself (p34, Connors 2023); they can increase Achilles tendon cross-sectional area and stiffness but raise metatarsophalangeal and ankle joint loading compared with conventional shoes (p33, Sun 2020).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low yield. COMLEX: Drop = rearfoot stack height − forefoot stack height (24 mm − 12 mm = 12 mm; course slide p40). Useful shoe factors: weight, stack height, heel-to-toe drop, flexibility (p41). Minimalist shoes: anterior foot strike, a running-style effect (Connors 2023); thicker, stiffer Achilles but more MTP and ankle loading (Sun 2020).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 3 — Physical therapy prescription components: like heel-to-toe drop in a shoe, each PT prescription element is a specific, stated variable"},{"id":"sdl17_q24","sdlNumber":17,"batch":2,"objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Begin to develop a paradigm for running footwear prescription.","isHighYield":false,"stem":"A Sun (2020) systematic review of 63 studies on footwear construction and running biomechanics identified four key conclusions. Which set of findings most accurately reflects the systematic review's conclusions?","choices":{"A":"Higher midsole cushioning uniformly reduces injury risk; motion control features reliably decrease pronation in overpronators; minimalist shoes produce equivalent joint loading to conventional shoes when worn by experienced barefoot runners","B":"No shoe construction variable demonstrated consistent biomechanical effects across subjects; individual kinematic variability negated population-level findings; only gait retraining produced statistically significant reductions in running-related injury rates","C":"Optimal midsole stiffness benefits performance; softer midsoles reduce impact forces and loading rates; thicker midsoles cushion but reduce plantar sensation; minimalist shoes improve running economy and Achilles tendon stiffness but increase MTP and ankle joint loading","D":"Maximalist cushioned shoes reduce GRF more than any other design; rigid carbon-plate shoes improve running economy equally across all runner types; heel-to-toe drop does not independently affect foot strike pattern or Achilles tendon loading","E":"Thicker midsoles reduce injury risk in all runners; stiffer midsoles increase injury risk in runners over age 40; minimalist shoes should only be prescribed to runners with pes cavus who already demonstrate an anterior foot strike pattern"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Sun (2020) identified four key conclusions from 63 studies: (1) Increasing midsole stiffness at the optimal range can benefit performance-related variables; (2) Softer midsoles can reduce impact forces and loading rates; (3) Thicker midsoles provide better cushioning but may decrease plantar sensations — reducing the natural impact-moderating reflexes; (4) Minimalist shoes can improve running economy and increase cross-sectional area and stiffness of the Achilles tendon, but would increase metatarsophalangeal and ankle joint loading compared to conventional shoes. This four-point summary represents the most evidence-consolidated framework for understanding the trade-offs in shoe construction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low yield. COMLEX: The Sun 2020 four conclusions are the most board-testable framework from OBJ 4 — they synthesize the minimalist shoe trade-offs (better Achilles/economy but more MTP and ankle load) and explain why no single shoe design is universally superior across all runners.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 3 — Physical therapy prescription components: Sun 2020's four-conclusion framework mirrors a PT prescription in its acknowledgment of trade-offs — every shoe construction variable that benefits one outcome worsens another, requiring individualized clinical judgment rather than universal protocols"}]};
